{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md | sed -n '234,470p'\"","aggregated_output":"   234\t  - FY2027 revenue: 1→2 (readers: rung 2);\n   235\t  - total CPU: 1→2;\n   236\t  - FY2028 gross margin: 3→2;\n   237\t  - Vera FY2028: 3→2;\n   238\t  - states' moratoriums: 2→1 (readers: zero states).\n   239\t\n   240\t  These are a code reading only. The registered blind reading of the moved answers is owed before any claim.\n   241\t- **The controls that moved:** C-new-3-Q33 4→5 (3/3), and C-old-2-Q15 5→{2,4,5}. To be read.\n   242\t- **The confound, measured.** In the hidden-piece requests the previous conclusion carries many pieces: 13 to 82 per question, and\n   243\t  2,264 for FY2027 revenue. Every item the rejected answer cited is in the shown set, so the moved answers had the evidence the\n   244\t  error was built from. The previous derivation and the hidden pieces are removed together, though, so their effects are not\n   245\t  separated. Only 8 clean targets exist, with +2 moves.\n   246\t- **This bears on D198** (Alex, 2026-10-27: a judgement reads the evidence in view; \"the previous conclusion carries\" the rest). The\n   247\t  carrier is also what keeps a wrong derivation alive for a median of 8.5 judgements.\n   248\t\n   249\t## Council G (`council-g-codex.md`): J2a and J2b retired; the separating test (rule written before any send)\n   250\t- **J2a and J2b are retired as candidates.**\n   251\t  - Rule (c) missed for both; J2b regressed d3-q18.\n   252\t  - The falsifier fired for J2a.\n   253\t  - Without the previous conclusion, J2b moves 16/24 targets against v7's 14/24, with 2/24 controls each. Its check adds about 2 on\n   254\t    top of removing the anchor.\n   255\t  - The 432-card reading finishes for the record.\n   256\t- **The separating test (council G's (a)).**\n   257\t  - **Cases:** the 21 cases with 1 to 100 hidden pieces: 13 targets and 8 controls.\n   258\t  - **Requests:** each request is rebuilt by the judge's own `build_prompt` on the store as of the stored judgement. Later\n   259\t    judgements, links, sources, relations, switches and successions are shadowed by temporary tables. **48 of 48 stored requests\n   260\t    rebuild byte for byte**, then every piece in force is shown (954 pieces restored), with no hidden line.\n   261\t  - **Arms**, v7, 3 repeats each:\n   262\t    - **fullprev**: full evidence with the previous conclusion;\n   263\t    - **fullnoprev**: full evidence without it.\n   264\t  - **Comparisons:**\n   265\t    - fullnoprev against fullprev is the anchor's effect on complete evidence, the separation itself;\n   266\t    - fullprev against v7 (clipped, with the previous conclusion) is the effect of restoring evidence under the anchor;\n   267\t    - fullnoprev against v7-noprev (clipped) is the effect of restoring evidence without the anchor.\n   268\t  - **Screen, before any claim:** fullnoprev against fullprev shows **at least 5 net target repairs** that are jointly supported by\n   269\t    both blind readers in at least 2 of 3 repeats. It loses no control and adds no refusals.\n   270\t  - **Reading:** a blind deck of every repeat of every moved case and control, plus a matched unmoved sample chosen before reading. It\n   271\t    is shuffled with the stored answers and v7 re-sends, each card showing what its answer actually received.\n   272\t  - **Budget:** about $0.4 on a new $2 ledger, `phase1b.json`. Phase 1's $3 ledger is nearly spent. The total stays inside D439's\n   273\t    $40, with about $5 across the merge test and phase 1.\n   274\t- **For Alex once the confound is separated (D198 is his):**\n   275\t  1. keep what the previous conclusion carries and change only how it is presented;\n   276\t  2. replace the carried derivation with a summary of the omitted evidence;\n   277\t  3. show the full evidence.\n   278\t  Option 1 stays within D198; options 2 and 3 change it.\n   279\t\n   280\t## Result 4: the frozen judgement gold without the previous conclusion (v7-noprev, 51 × 3)\n   281\t- **Agreement 43, 45, 45 (mean 44.3) against v7's 45.0.** Strict 42, 45, 44. Refused 2 of 153.\n   282\t- **Questions that differ:**\n   283\t  - **Regressions:** d3-q17, China share (v7 3/3 agree; noprev 0/3, rung 4→5); d1-q17 (1→0); d1-q09 and d3-q09 (3→2, one repeat\n   284\t    each).\n   285\t  - **Repairs:** d2-q22, FY2028 revenue (1→3); d2-q08, H200 authorization (2→3); d3-q18, Kyber (2→3).\n   286\t- **Reading.** At the gold's cutoff (07-14) the previous conclusions are young. Removing them is not a pure gain there: the China-share\n   287\t  question loses the consistency the previous conclusion held with the H200 theme. Any design that changes the previous conclusion\n   288\t  must hold this gold question.\n   289\t\n   290\t## Result 5: the separating test (council G's (a)), 13 targets and 8 controls with 1–100 hidden pieces, 3 repeats ($0.28)\n   291\t| Condition | Targets moved off the rejected rung (at least 2 of 3) | Controls moved |\n   292\t|---|---|---|\n   293\t| Clipped evidence, with the previous conclusion (v7 re-send) | 0 / 13 | 0 / 8 |\n   294\t| Clipped, without (v7-noprev) | 9 / 13 | 1 / 8 |\n   295\t| **Full evidence, with the previous conclusion (fullprev)** | **0 / 13** | 0 / 8 |\n   296\t| **Full evidence, without (fullnoprev)** | **8 / 13** | **0 / 8** |\n   297\t\n   298\t- **Restoring the hidden evidence moves nothing while the previous conclusion is shown.** Removing the previous conclusion moves 8 of\n   299\t  13 on complete evidence, with no control moving. The carried conclusion, not the missing evidence, holds the rejected rungs.\n   300\t- **The hidden pieces matter little to the moves.** Clipped without the previous conclusion moves 9 targets, full moves 8. The one\n   301\t  control that moved when clipped (C-old-2-Q15) is stable with full evidence: that move was evidence loss.\n   302\t- **Directions (code, against the rung both readers named):**\n   303\t  - equity commitments: old-1 Q28 3→1/2, old-2 Q29 3→2, old-3 Q33 3→2;\n   304\t  - 2027 capex: old-1 Q7 and old-3 Q7, 4→3;\n   305\t  - total CPU: old-2 Q16 1→3, old-3 Q14 1→2;\n   306\t  - FY2028 gross margin: old-3 Q37 3→2.\n   307\t\n   308\t  old-2 Q6 holds rung 4 on full evidence. The blind reading of all 126 answers is the registered screen (`fullscore`) and is running.\n   309\t- Each card is built by the comparison's own card builder from the request the answer actually received. Refusals are recomputed\n   310\t  against that request (the full deck's key refusals used the clipped request and are not used).\n   311\t\n   312\t## Result 6: the 432-card blind reading of the final-request probe, scored by the locked rule (`probescore`)\n   313\t| Arm | Targets jointly supported in at least 2 of 3 (of 24) | Paired net against v7 | Controls lost (of 24) | Refused | Rule (a)(b)(d) |\n   314\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   315\t| v7 re-send | 1 | — | 3 (new-2 Q14, new-3 Q33, old-2 Q15) | 0 | — |\n   316\t| J2a | 2 | +1 (new-3 Q26) | 2 | 0 | **does not pass** |\n   317\t| J2b | 4 | +3 (new-2 Q4, new-3 Q26, old-2 Q16) | 1 | 0 | **does not pass** |\n   318\t\n   319\t- **Plants:** 3 of 3 unsupported by both readers.\n   320\t- **v7's own re-send loses 3 controls.** A same-rung re-answer is not always re-supported, so the readers' own noise is about 3 in 24\n   321\t  controls.\n   322\t- **J2a and J2b are retired by the rule as written** ((a): a floor of 12 and a net of 8). This agrees with D682.\n   323\t\n   324\t## Result 7: the separating test's blind reading, scored by council G's screen (`fullscore`)\n   325\t| | Full evidence, with the previous conclusion | Full evidence, without it |\n   326\t|---|---|---|\n   327\t| Targets jointly supported in at least 2 of 3 (of 13) | 0 | **4**: old-1 Q7 and old-3 Q7 (2027 capex), old-2 Q16 (total CPU), old-3 Q37 (FY2028 gross margin) |\n   328\t| Controls jointly supported (of 8) | 8 | 8 |\n   329\t| Refused (recomputed against the request actually sent) | 0 | 0 |\n   330\t\n   331\t- **Screen: net repairs 4 against the 5 required, so the screen does not pass, by one.** No control lost, no refusals added. Plants 3/3\n   332\t  unsupported.\n   333\t- **Why the moved M08 answers are not confirmed.** old-1 Q28, old-2 Q29 and old-3 Q33 moved from rung 3 to rung 2. Both readers say\n   334\t  rung 1: \"$18B runs off by fiscal year-end, leaving ~$7B plus partly-funded new deals\". The move is in the readers' direction, not far\n   335\t  enough. old-3 Q14 is 1 of 3. old-2 Q6 stays at rung 4.\n   336\t- **The mechanism is confirmed by code:**\n   337\t  - with the previous conclusion, 0 of 13 move even on complete evidence;\n   338\t  - without it, 8 of 13 move, toward the readers' rung, with 0 of 8 controls moving.\n   339\t- **The fix, removing it, is not accepted.** The screen missed by one, and the frozen gold without it is 44.3 against v7's 45.0, with\n   340\t  d3-q17 regressed.\n   341\t\n   342\t## The fresh test (D684), written before any reading of the fresh marks\n   343\t**Alex, 2026-11-20:** \"那你自己持续迭代\" (the reply to the request for approval of council H's test). Harness: `fresh_test.py`.\n   344\t\n   345\t**The pool ($0, done).**\n   346\t- Each question's latest judgement on or before **2026-09-06** (the day before the dev window), in the 5 draws.\n   347\t- Excluded, before any reading:\n   348\t  - the 73 question-and-draw pairs whose answers the design read (the 24 targets, their 24 controls, the 49 census answers);\n   349\t  - a question whose last judgement is that same one (its answer was read at 09-20): 8;\n   350\t  - no answer by the cut: 13; a first judgement (no previous conclusion): 2; a retry: 1.\n   351\t- **116 answers, all rebuilt byte for byte** under the frozen judge. 114 of them hold the previous rung.\n   352\t\n   353\t**The census reading.** The comparison's own card builder and readers (Codex and Claude, closed sessions, rounds 0–2), with 3\n   354\tplants confirmed by a separate closed reader. Two changes, both for the date only:\n   355\t- the card's library list holds the questions opened by 09-06;\n   356\t- the readers' brief says \"as it stood on 6 September 2026\".\n   357\t\n   358\t**The selection rule.**\n   359\t- **Targets:** both readers rate the question worth tracking, and both reject the answer (unsupported or insufficient, a split between\n   360\t  the two included). All of them are used; n is their number.\n   361\t- **Controls:** ceil(8n/13) answers that both readers support on a worth-tracking question. Each is matched to a target by draw and\n   362\t  type, then by type alone, chosen with seed 20261120, none twice.\n   363\t- **If n < 8:** the pool is widened by the same rule at 2026-08-23, for questions with no target at 09-06, before any send.\n   364\t\n   365\t**The arms** (`fresh_test.py`, `arm_parts`).\n   366\t- **v7:** the frozen judge, re-sent the same day. It is the control.\n   367\t- **H1 (council H):** the heading `[Previous conclusion, for comparison only]` becomes `[Previous conclusion: earlier assessment and\n   368\t  carried evidence. Derive today's forecast afresh.]`. The concept's name stays, so the system text's three uses of \"the previous\n   369\t  conclusion\" still point at the block. The rung and the reasons are unchanged, which is what D198 carries.\n   370\t- **H2 (proposed to council I):** H1, plus one system sentence restated in its source's words.\n   371\t  - The sentence: \"Do not add to or subtract from the previous score.\"\n   372\t  - Its source: PRD 06, line 19: \"rather than adjusting its previous score\".\n   373\t  - The restatement: \"Derive the rung from this evidence rather than adjusting the previous score.\"\n   374\t  - Why: one of the 24 accounts the model gave of its own answers (`account/T-new-2-Q31.json`) reads that sentence as \"do not move\n   375\t    without new evidence\". As the model reads it, the sentence is an instruction to anchor, which is the opposite of its source.\n   376\t  - H2 against H1 isolates the sentence.\n   377\t\n   378\t**The screen (each candidate arm against v7, paired, 3 repeats).**\n   379\t- **Jointly supported:** both readers' final answer mark is `supported` in at least 2 of 3 repeats.\n   380\t- **Net target repairs at least ceil(5n/13).**\n   381\t- **No control lost.**\n   382\t- **Refusals not above v7's.** They are recomputed against the request each answer actually received; an invalid rung counts as\n   383\t  refused and not supported.\n   384\t- **Frozen gold:** the mean per repeat at least v7's 45.0. d3-q17 must agree in 3 of 3, with its reasons read against both H200\n   385\t  conclusions.\n   386\t- **Readers:** every answer of every arm and repeat, plus 3 plants. Each card is built from the stored v7 text, so the heading the arms\n   387\t  change is not on any card.\n   388\t- **Falsifier (code, before reading):** if H1 moves fewer than 2 targets off the stored rung in at least 2 of 3 repeats, the heading\n   389\t  does not loosen the anchor.\n   390\t\n   391\t**Budget.** The `phase1b` ledger's remaining $1.72 of its $2 ceiling; no new ceiling is opened. At about $0.0022 a call (`phase1b`):\n   392\t- each arm costs 3 × (n + controls) calls, about $0.17 for 25 cases;\n   393\t- the gold costs 153 calls per candidate arm, about $0.35.\n   394\t\n   395\tThree arms and two gold runs come to about $1.2. The total stays inside D439's $40.\n   396\t\n   397\t### Council I (`council-i-codex.md`, DISAGREE) and the amendment, written before any fresh mark was opened\n   398\t**Verified.** The pool held questions the design had read under another label or in another draw. Examples:\n   399\t- old-1 Q8 is the same FY2027 data-center revenue question as the exposed old-2 Q9, word for word;\n   400\t- the same holds for FY2027 gross margin, FY2028 gross margin, and two OpenAI chip-financing questions.\n   401\t\n   402\tClaude's own reflection (`council-i-claude.md`) had called this mild. It is not: the census looked at these questions.\n   403\t\n   404\t**Amended rule:**\n   405\t- **Bets.** Each pooled question gets a bet, read by Claude from its text against the 73 questions the design read\n   406\t  (`fresh-bets.json`). The file was written before any fresh mark was opened.\n   407\t  - 52 answers sit on a bet the design read, and are excluded.\n   408\t  - Questions that are the same bet across draws share one label. **Each bet gives at most one case**: from its answers in seeded\n   409\t    order, the first rejected one becomes a target; otherwise the first supported one becomes a control candidate.\n   410\t  - Bets the frozen matter list keeps apart stay apart: 2026 capex (M04) is not the exposed 2027 capex (M14). A bet adjacent to an\n   411\t    exposed one is excluded: stand-alone CPU revenue next to total CPU revenue, and AI-buildout bond issuance next to the\n   412\t    bond-issuance share.\n   413\t- **Prerequisites enforced in code before any send:**\n   414\t  - n ≥ 8 targets;\n   415\t  - ceil(8n/13) controls;\n   416\t  - all 3 census plants rejected by both readers.\n   417\t\n   418\t  Otherwise there is no send, and the shortfall goes back to the council.\n   419\t- **Scoring.**\n   420\t  - A production refusal counts as not supported.\n   421\t  - A plant the readers do not reject voids the reading.\n   422\t  - Rung moves are reported as a description only. The falsifier on moves stays a description, not a gate.\n   423\t- **H2 is not sent.** One account in 24 is too little, and \"this evidence\" could be read to leave out the dependencies and themes. It\n   424\t  is parked with that reason. Claude agrees: the cheaper course.\n   425\t\n   426\t## The fresh census and council J\n   427\t**The fresh selection was refused by its own rule. No send was made.**\n   428\t- 27 fresh bets, with 37 worth-tracking answers, gave **1 target** (F-new-3-Q3, H200). The prerequisite is 8.\n   429\t- The 3 plants were rejected by both readers.\n   430\t- At 09-06, 5 of 75 worth-tracking answers are rejected by both readers. 4 of the 5 are on numeric bets the design read: FY2027\n   431\t  data-center revenue, FY2027 CPU revenue (twice), and the Poolside obligation. The fifth is the fresh H200 question.\n   432\t- **On this corpus, the judge's errors sit in the big numeric bets, and the design has read all of them.** A fresh test of a judge\n   433\t  change needs a fresh window.\n   434\t- **Error of mine.** I first read 7% at 09-06 against 17% at 09-20 as \"errors accumulate\". Council J: the two pools are chosen\n   435\t  differently (the 09-06 pool excludes what the design read at 09-20), so the comparison cannot show it. Withdrawn.\n   436\t\n   437\t**Council J (`council-j-codex.md`, consensus): one small blinded diagnostic of H1 before anything wider.**\n   438\t- **Targets:** T-old-3-Q3 (FY2027 revenue), T-old-3-Q37 (FY2028 gross margin) and T-old-1-Q7 (2027 capex). These are three bets where\n   439\t  v7 kept the rejected rung in 3 of 3 re-sends and the arm without the previous conclusion moved in 3 of 3 (verified from\n   440\t  `probe/v7` and `probe/v7-noprev`).\n   441\t- **Controls:** two, seeded, from the controls that carry hidden evidence, were jointly supported in 3 of 3 v7 re-sends, and do not sit\n   442\t  on a target's bet: C-old-1-Q29 and C-old-3-Q15.\n   443\t- **Arms:** H1 against same-day v7, 3 repeats each, giving 30 answers. Every answer is blind-read with plants, against cards built from\n   444\t  the v7 text.\n   445\t- **Falsifier:**\n   446\t  - **passes** with at least 2 net target repairs jointly supported in at least 2 of 3 repeats, no control lost, and no added refusals\n   447\t    (a refusal counts as not supported);\n   448\t  - **inconclusive** if same-day v7 leaves fewer than 2 targets to repair.\n   449\t- **Not run:** the 48-case movement-only send, and the whole-window replay, until H1 shows a supported repair and clears the gold\n   450\t  guard.\n   451\t- **Dev only.** These cases motivated the design. A pass earns the end-to-end replay, not acceptance.\n   452\t- **$0 checks:** all 5 requests are rebuilt byte for byte; H1 changes only the heading; the stored answers pass the production check\n   453\t  under both arms.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md | sed -n '1,233p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# J2: the judge's next version, design for the council (2026-11-20, phase 1 steps 2–4)\n     2\t\n     3\t## What the census and the traces show\n     4\t- **The target.** 24 answers rejected by both readers (`census/CENSUS.md`). In about 16 of them the answer leaves the rung of its own\n     5\t  reference on a weak step. Main classes:\n     6\t  - O: overlapping amounts added (6);\n     7\t  - S: a figure of another scope taken as the value (6);\n     8\t  - E: an adjustment with no basis (5).\n     9\t- **The order of the answer (found at $0).**\n    10\t  - In all 48 stored answers of the probe set, the judge writes the fields in this order: `rung > reasons > cites > cites_views >\n    11\t    pushes > changed > why_changed > theme_pushes > reference > implied_outcome`. It chooses the rung first, and states the reference and\n    12\t    its own derivation last.\n    13\t  - The cause is code. `judging.schema_for` inserts `reference` and `implied_outcome` after `rung` in `required`. Its comment reads\n    14\t    \"after rung: the reference and the implied outcome are written before the reasons\". But it appends them to the end of `properties`,\n    15\t    and the model writes the fields in `properties` order.\n    16\t  - D260 requires both fields but names no output order (council A's correction). The order of work is the reader brief's: first the\n    17\t    reference, then what the evidence implies, then the comparison.\n    18\t  - It is a plausible mechanism for the arithmetic that lands just across a boundary (7 of 24), and not yet a proven one. J2a tests it\n    19\t    alone.\n    20\t- **The model's own account** (`poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/account/`, 24 continued conversations, $0.027). It names:\n    21\t  - \"State the implied figure … compare it with the rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one\" (9);\n    22\t  - \"carries the guided growth forward\" (6);\n    23\t  - \"rumour … in proportion to their specificity\" (3);\n    24\t  - \"The rung is your forecast\" (3);\n    25\t  - \"discounted by default\" (1);\n    26\t  - \"Missing confirmation alone…\" (1).\n    27\t\n    28\t  Two answers concede the error: old-2 Q9 says \"I inferred a Q4 figure without enough evidence\"; new-2 Q31 says \"moved … without new\n    29\t  evidence\".\n    30\t- **Re-send noise.** The 48 stored requests are re-sent 3 times unchanged (arm v7, running). A target that flips on the re-send alone\n    31\t  is sampler noise and is reported apart.\n    32\t\n    33\t## Prompt audit (judging-v7 against the judgement ruler's reader brief and D260/D263)\n    34\t| Failure class | Request sentence that produces it | The standard's line | Verdict |\n    35\t|---|---|---|---|\n    36\t| Rung chosen before the derivation (all classes) | The schema's field order: rung first, `reference` and `implied_outcome` last. Sentence 10 lists the rung first | Reader brief: \"first establish what the market expected … then ask what the evidence implies, then compare the two\". D260: \"the answer states the reference and the implied outcome\", written before the reasons | **Contradiction (code)** |\n    37\t| O, overlapping amounts added | \"derive the outcome … check it against the dependencies' conclusions (a part cannot exceed its whole …)\", applied only to dependencies, not to the judge's own sums | Reader brief and D260 (3): \"A part cannot exceed the whole it belongs to\"; \"the same claim … counts once\" | **Missing at its owner:** the identity is stated for dependencies, not for the judge's own arithmetic |\n    38\t| S, a figure of another scope as the value | \"a figure that spans several periods or products gives this question only its share\", which covers periods and products, not companies, measures, or balance against flow | Ruler v1 §2′, the stage 3/4 analyst standard B: \"期间和定义要对上\" (period and definition must match). Extraction standard: a proxy of one aggregate is evidence for the same question, never its value | **Narrower than the standard** |\n    39\t| E, an adjustment with no basis | \"move when it crosses one\", together with \"once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay\" | Reader brief: \"a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it\"; \"Lean cautious, catch the increment\" | **Missing:** the shift is meant to be quantified, and the prompt never says that an unquantified adjustment does not cross |\n    40\t| W, talks taken as a likely completion (2) | \"third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity\" | Same line in the reader brief | **Misread**, 2 cases. Not targeted now |\n    41\t| R, a rung's own condition misapplied (2) | — | The rung text | **Misread**, 2 cases. Not targeted now |\n    42\t\n    43\t## The arms (revised after council A, `council-a-codex.md`; one lever each; every sentence sourced)\n    44\t| Arm | Change | Source | Characters |\n    45\t|---|---|---|---|\n    46\t| v7 | None, re-sent as stored | Noise control | 4,924 |\n    47\t| **J2a** | **Schema order only:** `properties` written as `reference, implied_outcome, reasons, rung`, then the rest unchanged. The system text is v7's, byte for byte, so the arm isolates order (council A: changing the wording too would not) | The reader brief's order of work; the code's own stated intent | 4,924 |\n    48\t| **J2b** | J2a, plus a two-row check at the end of the system text, before the rung is chosen: (1) **overlap**: before adding, ask whether one amount contains all *or part* of the other, and count a shared part once (the examples are conditional on how the figures are reported); (2) **scope**: a figure of another quantity is an input to adjust or a proxy to name, never the value as it stands. Say how it was adjusted, or that it was used unadjusted as a proxy and why | (1) \"a part cannot exceed its whole\" (reader brief, D260); (2) analyst standard B, \"期间和定义要对上\", and the extraction standard's proxy line. The form follows AGENTS §6 (a check at the end) | 5,716. +792, reason: the closing-check form; nothing is cut, since each existing sentence carries a D260/D263 behaviour |\n    49\t\n    50\t**Dropped after council A:**\n    51\t- **The first draft's row (3)**, \"an adjustment no item quantifies does not cross a boundary\". It could block guided growth, the discount on\n    52\t  untested targets and forecasts based on specific non-numeric evidence, which are D263 behaviours of the frozen gold. That is a return to\n    53\t  D260's v5 stickiness.\n    54\t- **The first draft's examples**, which mirrored the failing cases. The examples now come from other domains: a company's guidance and its\n    55\t  reported quarter; a lender's loan book and its committed loans.\n    56\t\n    57\t## The probe and the decision rule (written before any J2 send; revised after council A)\n    58\t- **Cases.** 24 targets, plus 24 controls that both readers supported, of the same draw and type (seeded). All 48 stored requests\n    59\t  rebuild byte for byte. The rebuild check compares content (sha over sorted JSON). Key order comes from production's own\n    60\t  `build_request` and `schema_for`; the arms reorder only `properties`.\n    61\t- **Sends.** Each arm × 48 cases × 3 repeats, at about $0.003 a call. Ledger `phase1.json`, $3.\n    62\t- **Reading, complete** (council A):\n    63\t  - every repeat of every case in the three arms, v7 re-send, J2a and J2b: 432 cards, none sampled out, plus 3 plants confirmed by a\n    64\t    separate closed reader;\n    65\t  - blind, shuffled, both families (`mark_cards`: Codex with memories off, Claude), debate rounds 1 and 2;\n    66\t  - every card in the same form: the answer, then \"Reference / Implied outcome / Reasons\";\n    67\t  - Claude reads every target that flips beside its material, and every control lost.\n    68\t- **One decision rule for an arm against v7's re-send** (locked after council round 2, `council-b-codex.md`, before any J2 send). A\n    69\t  case is **jointly supported** when both readers' final marks are `supported`; a split counts as not supported.\n    70\t  - **(a) Targets:**\n    71\t    - the arm is jointly supported in at least 2 of 3 repeats on **at least 12 of 24** targets (a floor);\n    72\t    - paired by distinct question: (targets where the arm holds 2 of 3 and v7's re-send does not) minus (the reverse) is **at least 8**.\n    73\t  - **(b) Controls:** a control is lost when it is not jointly supported in at least 2 of 3 repeats, a split included. The arm loses no\n    74\t    more controls than v7's re-send.\n    75\t  - **(c) The frozen judgement gold** (gold-v1, `636c056d…`, the scorer's own rule, 51 requests of the r9-frozen-v108 draws, 3\n    76\t    repeats):\n    77\t    - the arm's mean agreement over its repeats is no lower than v7's re-send;\n    78\t    - every question whose agreement differs between the arms is read with its reasons.\n    79\t  - **(d) Production refusals** (`judging.check_answer` on every answer, against the request it answered) are counted per arm before\n    80\t    scoring. An arm refused more often than v7 is diagnosed before it passes.\n    81\t- **Archive key** (council round 2). `model.request_identity` sorts keys, so J2a's request has v7's archive key. A J2 run on a populated\n    82\t  archive would silently replay v7's answers.\n    83\t  - The probe calls the transport with no archive.\n    84\t  - The production-path check uses empty, separate archives per arm, and verifies the raw answer's field order.\n    85\t  - The build must give J2 a distinct request identity, for example its schema name.\n    86\t- **Falsifiers.**\n    87\t  - J2a moves no more targets than v7's re-send: order is not the mechanism.\n    88\t  - J2b fixes targets but fails (b) or (c): the check over-corrects. It stops there.\n    89\t- **Before the end-to-end slice: a production-path check** (council A).\n    90\t  - J2 and v7 run on identical inputs through `judging.round_of_judgements`, on a copy of one old store, over two consecutive days\n    91\t    with updates.\n    92\t  - It runs on the probe's questions and their dependants. J2 is patched into the harness; no package code changes.\n    93\t  - It checks: refusals by `check_answer`; the next day's request (the previous conclusion, the evidence shown from the citations);\n    94\t    dependants' consistency.\n    95\t- **The slice compares v7 and J2 on the same inputs.**\n    96\t  - Its arms are old reading with v7, old reading with J2, and c8 reading with J2.\n    97\t  - The first plan ran J2 in both arms; that is corrected in `PLAN.md`.\n    98\t\n    99\t## What this cannot show\n   100\t- **Opening, and the 17 matters never opened.** Phase 2.\n   101\t- **Effects across days.** A changed rung feeds the next day's previous conclusion. The end-to-end slice (plan step 7) shows those.\n   102\t- **A release verdict.** These cases are dev: they motivated the design. Acceptance is on a fresh window.\n   103\t\n   104\t## Out of scope for the council\n   105\tExtraction, opening, Steps 2–3, the frozen ruler's files, cost work.\n   106\t\n   107\t## Council rounds (Codex thread `01a1f7a7-e78a-7721-90a6-673bc85b6af6`, read-only, memories off)\n   108\t| Round | Verdict | What it found | What it taught |\n   109\t|---|---|---|---|\n   110\t| A (`council-a-codex.md`) | DISAGREE | J2a changed the order and the wording together; J2b's row 3 could bring back v5's stickiness, and its examples mirrored the failures; reading was not complete; the slice ran J2 in both arms | One lever per arm, literally. A blocking rule against \"unquantified\" moves conflicts with D263 behaviours of the frozen gold |\n   111\t| B (`council-b-codex.md`) | DISAGREE | The deck sampled same-rung answers. **J2a and v7 share an archive key** (`request_identity` sorts keys), so a populated archive replays v7. The loan-book example assumed one reporting convention. The rule was not paired | A change of key order alone is invisible to the archive's identity. Lock a paired rule by distinct question before any send |\n   112\t| C (`council-c-codex.md`) | DISAGREE | The refusal check read the stored final judgement as the previous one, giving false \"moved but changed is false\" flags. Coverage was not enforced | A check must run on the state before the answer it checks. Positive and negative controls: stored answers 0/48 refused, a cite mutant 48/48 |\n   113\t| D (`council-d-codex.md`) | DISAGREE | Invalid-rung answers fell out of the count. The replay preloaded production's whole day, which masked the theme queue | Start from production's own queue: 13 and 18 at the start, and the round adds 23 and 18, the same as old |\n   114\t| E (`council-e-codex.md`) | **CONSENSUS** | — | Send J2a and J2b (probe and gold, 3 repeats), score coverage and refusals, read the complete deck, then the production-path check under v7 and the passing arm |\n   115\t\n   116\t**Measured before the J2 sends ($0.60 spent):**\n   117\t- **v7 re-send, 48 cases × 3.**\n   118\t  - Targets: 21/24 reproduce the stored rung in all repeats; Q31 1→2 (3/3), old-2 Q16 1→3 (1/3), old-2 Q9 2→3 (3/3).\n   119\t  - Controls: 0/24 change. Refused: 0/144.\n   120\t- **v7 on the frozen gold, 51 × 3.** Agreement 45, 46 and 44 raw, 45, 46 and 42 strict (refused counted as not agreeing). Refused 3/153.\n   121\t  The stored scores are 15+15+16 = 46.\n   122\t- **Production-path replay, $0.** 72/72 requests are byte-identical to old's over 09-17 and 09-18, starting from production's queue,\n   123\t  with the same judged set.\n   124\t\n   125\t## Result 1: the final-request probe (2026-11-20, $1.21 spent of $3)\n   126\t| Arm | Targets moved off the stored rung in at least 2 of 3 repeats | Controls moved | Refused by the production check (of 144) |\n   127\t|---|---|---|---|\n   128\t| v7 re-send | 2 (Q31 1→2, old-2 Q9 2→3) | 0 | 0 |\n   129\t| J2a, order only | 2 (the same two) | 0 | 0 |\n   130\t| J2b, order plus check | 3 (the same two, plus old-2 Q16 1→3) | 0 | 0 |\n   131\t\n   132\t- **The registered falsifier fired at the rung level:** J2a moves no more targets than v7's re-send. J2b adds one.\n   133\t- **The derivations, read by Claude for 4 cases** (old-2 Q29, old-3 Q33, old-3 Q3, old-2 Q6), are unchanged under J2b:\n   134\t  - it still adds $25B and $18B;\n   135\t  - it still stacks the uplift;\n   136\t  - it still scales NVIDIA's $1.3T, now naming it a proxy.\n   137\t- **On M08 the material is ambiguous.** The items say \"$25 billion of equity investment commitments as of July 26\" and \"$18 billion\n   138\t  committed … for the remainder of FY2027\", with no stated relation. The readers' \"likely overlaps\" is an analyst's inference.\n   139\t- **The probe was confounded, and this was found before the slice, not at the end.**\n   140\t  - Every final request carries \"[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung N; reasons: …\", which is the rejected answer with its\n   141\t    whole derivation.\n   142\t  - The rejected rung had been held for a median of about 10 judgements: 1 to 46, and Q4 for 42.\n   143\t  - So the probe measures whether the judge overturns its own carried conclusion, not whether it would have chosen it.\n   144\t  - **The planned slice has the same flaw:** it starts from the 09-06 copy, with every earlier wrong rung carried in.\n   145\t- **The 432-card reading is deferred** (to be agreed). With the rungs unchanged, rule (a)'s floor of 12 needs the readers to support at\n   146\t  least 9 answers at a rung they rejected, on unchanged derivations. The reading cannot change the decision.\n   147\t\n   148\t## The fork probe (written before any send)\n   149\t- **Cases.** Per target, the stored judgement at which the rejected rung was first chosen and then held to the end. 6 are a question's\n   150\t  first judgement, with no previous conclusion. 18 are moves from another rung, where the previous conclusion is the rung before the\n   151\t  error. All 24 fork requests rebuild byte for byte.\n   152\t- **Arms.** v7, J2a and J2b, 3 repeats each: 216 calls, about $0.45.\n   153\t- **Metric (code).** A target \"repeats the rejected rung\" when its fork answer gives that rung in at least 2 of 3 repeats. Production\n   154\t  refusals are counted.\n   155\t- **Rule.**\n   156\t  - **If v7 at the fork repeats the rejected rung on 12 or more of 24**, the errors are the judge's, not a sample carried forward. An\n   157\t    arm passes the screen when it repeats on at least 8 fewer targets than v7, with no more refusals. Its fork answers and v7's are then\n   158\t    read blind by both families (216 cards plus plants) to confirm that the new rungs are supported.\n   159\t  - **If v7 at the fork repeats on 11 or fewer**, the errors are mostly one unlucky sample that the carried previous conclusion keeps.\n   160\t    The lever is then the anchor (how the previous conclusion is shown), not a reasoning check. J2a and J2b stop, and the anchor is\n   161\t    designed next.\n   162\t  - **If neither arm passes the screen while v7 repeats on 12 or more**, J2's levers do not fix these errors even without the anchor.\n   163\t    The J2 line stops and goes to Alex with this census.\n   164\t\n   165\t## Council F (`council-f-codex.md`, DISAGREE) and what follows\n   166\t- **The fork probe is dropped.** Its evidence differs from the final request's, and an error can predate the first use of the rejected\n   167\t  rung: old-2 Q29's earlier answer already adds $25B and $18B. The fork cannot isolate the anchor.\n   168\t- **The 432-card reading runs** before either arm is called passed or failed. Reasoning can be repaired at an unchanged rung (rule\n   169\t  (a)), and support can be lost at an unchanged rung (rule (b)). The deck was built on 2026-11-20: 432 answers plus 3 confirmed\n   170\t  plants, 0 invalid rungs.\n   171\t- **The anchor test** (written before its results).\n   172\t  - **Requests.** The same 48 final requests, sent without the previous conclusion (`without_previous`). Its \"[Previous conclusion,\n   173\t    for comparison only] …\" block goes, and so does the \"[Not shown: N more pieces … the previous conclusion carries them]\" line.\n   174\t  - **Arms.** v7-noprev and J2b-noprev, 3 repeats each (288 calls). J2a is left out: it equalled v7 on rungs.\n   175\t  - **Flag.** 34 of 48 requests had hidden pieces that the previous conclusion carried; without it they lose that information. Results\n   176\t    are reported apart for those 34 and for the 14 clean requests.\n   177\t  - **Metric (code).** Targets moved off the rejected rung in at least 2 of 3 repeats; controls moved off their supported rung in at\n   178\t    least 2 of 3. Each is compared with the same arm sent with the previous conclusion.\n   179\t  - **Reading.**\n   180\t    - If v7-noprev moves at least 8 more targets off the rejected rung than v7 does, and no more than 2 controls, the carried conclusion\n   181\t      holds the errors in place. The lever is then the anchor.\n   182\t    - If v7-noprev is within 3 targets of v7, the anchor is not what holds them: the errors are the judge's on this evidence.\n   183\t    - J2b-noprev against v7-noprev is J2b's effect without the anchor.\n   184\t    - Moved answers are read blind before any claim of improvement.\n   185\t- **The end-to-end test is redefined** (council F agrees). It is a judge-only replay of the whole window from 07-01, with old's links,\n   186\t  sources and relations fixed. Each arm builds its own conclusions, citations, theme effects and queue.\n   187\t  - It runs on the prodpath machinery, extended from two days to the whole window.\n   188\t  - The cost is about $2 per draw and arm, from archived tokens.\n   189\t  - It replaces the slice from the 09-06 copy, which carried every earlier wrong rung.\n   190\t\n   191\t## Result 2: the frozen judgement gold, rule (c) (51 requests × 3, scored by the frozen scorer's rule)\n   192\t| Arm | Agreement per repeat (of 51) | Mean | Strict (refused counted as not agreeing) | Refused (of 153) | Rule (c): mean no lower than v7's |\n   193\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   194\t| v7 re-send | 45, 46, 44 | 45.0 | 45, 46, 42 (44.3) | 3 | — |\n   195\t| J2a | 44, 44, 45 | 44.3 | 43, 44, 45 (44.0) | 1 | **missed** |\n   196\t| J2b | 44, 45, 45 | 44.7 | 44, 44, 44 (44.0) | 2 | **missed** |\n   197\t\n   198\t- **The questions that differ, read with their reasons.**\n   199\t  - Five are single-repeat flips on boundary questions: d1-q17 and d3-q17 (China share), d2-q08 (H200 authorization), d2-q22 and\n   200\t    d3-q22 (FY2028 revenue).\n   201\t  - **One is a consistent J2b regression: d3-q18**, Kyber NVL144 shipping by the end of 2027. The gold says No (the reported slip to\n   202\t    2028). v7 says No in 2 of 3 repeats; J2b says Yes in 3 of 3. J2b's reasons treat the slip report as \"one underlying source\", weigh\n   203\t    NVIDIA's denial, and name the NVL576 statement \"a proxy\". The scope row was read as a reason to discount specific third-party\n   204\t    evidence. That is the direction council A warned of.\n   205\t- **Both arms miss (c) as written.** The gaps are within one question of 51, inside v7's own repeat range of 44–46. The miss stands as\n   206\t  scored.\n   207\t\n   208\t## The whole-window production path (validated at $0)\n   209\t- `window old-2 replay`: a copy holding nothing before 07-01. Each day is fed old's links, evidence, sources, relations, opened\n   210\t  questions, closures and successions. The judge's own round runs from production's own queue.\n   211\t- Result over 82 days: the same judged set on 82 of 82 days, the same evidence in 1,616 of 1,616 requests, and 1,612 of 1,616 requests\n   212\t  byte-identical.\n   213\t- The 4 differences are retries. In old's run, a refused first answer was re-asked with \"The code returned the previous answer\n   214\t  because …\". The replay answers the first request with old's accepted retry answer, so no retry note is sent.\n   215\t- The machinery for the end-to-end judge test is ready.\n   216\t- **Cost correction.** The whole window is about 1,616 judge calls per draw, at about $0.002 each: **about $3.3 per draw and arm**, not\n   217\t  $2. The $2.04 figure was the 14-day window for two draws.\n   218\t\n   219\t## Result 3: the anchor test, v7 with and without the previous conclusion (the same 48 final requests, 3 repeats each)\n   220\t| Group | Role | n | Moved in at least 2 of 3: v7 | Moved: v7-noprev |\n   221\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   222\t| Clean (no hidden pieces) | targets | 8 | 1 | 3 |\n   223\t| Clean | controls | 6 | 0 | 1 |\n   224\t| Hidden pieces carried by the previous conclusion | targets | 16 | 1 | 11 |\n   225\t| Hidden pieces | controls | 18 | 0 | 1 |\n   226\t| **All** | **targets** | **24** | **2** | **14** |\n   227\t| **All** | **controls** | **24** | **0** | **2** |\n   228\t\n   229\t- **The registered reading fires:** 12 more targets moved (at least 8 needed), and 2 controls (at most 2 allowed). The carried\n   230\t  conclusion holds the errors in place, so the lever is the anchor, not a reasoning check.\n   231\t- **The direction of the moves (Claude's code read against both readers' named rung):**\n   232\t  - 2027 capex: 4→3 on all three draws' questions (the readers named rung 3);\n   233\t  - equity commitments (M08): 3→2 and 3→1 (readers: rungs 1–2);\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; sed -n '1,110p' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n\n> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n\n## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n\n**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n\nThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n\n- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n  nothing at all and were never judged.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n| Evidence | Content | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n\nAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n\n## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n\n| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n\n## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n\n- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n  system (D263).\n- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n  competition or pricing\".\n- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n  of the previous filing topic).\n- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n\n## 5. What to do\n\nStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n\n1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n     the existing rule).\n   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n\n## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n\n- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n  measured and reported apart.\n- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n  counted after.\n- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n    question\";\n  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n    or no question uses);\n  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n    ones that matter one by one.\n- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n      facts must not fall.\n  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n      what was measured.\n  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n\n## 7. Process (no drift)\n\n- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n  sentence, in both directions.\n- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n\n## 8. Steps and where we are\n\n| # | Step | Output | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n\n## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n\n- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n# Current state\n\n**Clean Steps 1–3, current (2026-11-11).** Records: Step 1, [round log](../clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md) §§16–47; Step 2, [Step 2 log](../clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §§12–19.\n- **Step 1 configuration: c8, adopted by Alex (D387).** It runs DeepSeek Flash at high effort, with a 64,000-token output cap; a\n  format failure or a cut answer is resent once (D380). It carries Alex's decisions D382–D385:\n  - figures are conditions;\n  - material statements with no thesis are kept without links;\n  - only big matters open a thesis;\n  - no target prices.\n\n  [PRD §12](../clean-arch/PRD.md) holds its system prompt, closing check and schema; a run with no candidate files sends exactly the\n  accepted request.\n- **Acceptance (D386, [protocol](../clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/ACCEPTANCE-PROTOCOL.md)).** 50 fresh articles that no run,\n  old or new, had used: news from 2026-09-05 to 09-07, a holiday weekend with no NVIDIA posts. One draw; two readers and a third on\n  disputes.\n  - helpful 132/139 (core 83/83);\n  - wrongly opened theses 1 of 10;\n  - search 141/143 available repeat pairs, or 141/145 for the whole system (one 11-article story supplies 55 pairs, and revisions are\n    barely exercised);\n  - clear wrong merges 1/57, plus 2 overlaps;\n  - detail retention 22 = 22. This is a storage comparison: every record keeps its own text. It is not a usability test.\n\n  Dev ×3 on the frozen golden-v5 (a lead): helpful 214, 211 and 212 of 234; search 98.9–100%; clear wrong merges about 1%. Step 1\n  cost for the fresh set plus dev ×3: $4.20 at published rates.\n- **Steps 2 and 3** are built in isolation (`clean-arch/step2`, `clean-arch/step3`): search, comparison, grouping and later linking.\n  They are not integrated with production storage. **Step 4 (judging) has not passed** on the clean path: its ladder step is built and\n  measured (four Stage A runs, lines missed by 1 to 3 answers; D426), so nothing yet shows the clean system can judge an investment\n  view on its own questions.\n- **Step 2 is closed and frozen (Alex, D427, 2026-11-11;** [Step 2 log](../clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §§14–19**).** It goes\n  to production only with the whole clean path, after Step 4 meets its lines; `tests/test_frozen_clean_step2.py` guards its files. Its three lines were met at the acceptance above (one draw; \"later version\"\n  relations barely exercised), and no miss of the seeded end-to-end test (clean 18, 18, 17 against production 17, 15, 16) has\n  been traced to it (the two single-draw misses were diagnosed on 2026-11-11, below: neither traces to Step 2). What happened since the close-out of 2026-11-08:\n  - A change to Jev's wording for \"later version\" relations failed its line (42 calls, 2026-11-09) and tuning stopped.\n  - One wrong relation was traced to the judge: it put NVIDIA's answer to the Kyber report under the FY2027 revenue question;\n    sent both ways, the conclusion did not differ ([result](../clean-arch/step2-edge-effect-2026-11-10/RESULT.md)).\n  - Alex stopped work on that relation (D423) and asked for a fix to the rule that gives a \"later version\" the older record's\n    questions. Measured on the three saved draws ([round log](../clean-arch/step2-revision-links-2026-11-10/LOG.md)), removing the\n    rule was withdrawn: of the 24 links it wrote, two blind reader families call 19 context and 3 evidence, and one of the 3 is a\n    company's answer that would otherwise be missing beside four reports (with it rung 2, 2, 2; without it 2, 3, 2). **The rule\n    stays (D424).**\n  - **Open, recorded:** Jev's \"later version\" relation (9 of 30 join different statements in draw 1); the context links it carries\n    (19 of 24); wrong groups (at the line on one draw; the unprompted reading test never run); the \"Unverified quote\" mark not\n    shown to Jev (3 of 188); not decided, whether a company's answer must sit under every question its report is filed under.\n  - **Reopens when** Step 4 yields judged views on the clean path and a miss, or something a reader of a thesis sees, traces to\n    one of these. The round's measurement kit (replay of a saved draw, exact simulation of a writer rule, blind packets with\n    controls, a judgement sent both ways) is kept in the two folders above.\n- **The clean system's untraced end-to-end misses, diagnosed (2026-11-11;** [round log](../clean-arch/e2e-misses-2026-11-11/LOG.md)**).**\n  The China share question in draw 3 and the FY2028 revenue question in draw 2 (seeded test, D392). Both estimates sit on a rung\n  line. China: the same window-end request, re-sent, gives the miss twice and the right answer once (the judge's own variation at the\n  1% line); no H200 price is in any request. FY2028: draw 2's window-end request gives the miss 3 of 3 and draw 3's the right answer 3\n  of 3; draw 2's lacks records draw 3's holds (a \"41% in calendar 2027\" figure Step 1 filed under FY2027 only, \"$1 trillion of\n  Blackwell and Rubin visibility\", five infrastructure-demand records), not isolated which matters; FY2028 is the question whose filed\n  statements differ most between draws (47% shared, median 74%). Neither traces to Step 2. Refuted on the way: that a refused answer\n  re-asked caused the China miss. Observed defect of the frozen judge: it names the export-controls theme as a dependency of the China\n  question, 42 of 65 refusals in six draws, and the re-ask changed the rung 8 times (PRD 11, E4). No change proposed; 18 sends, $0.043.\n- **Open defects, by product impact** (reflection council, `council/av-codex.md`):\n  1. **The Ohio-guarantee yes/no question is never covered** in the three dev draws; a broader credit-support amount thesis opens\n     instead. Diagnosed (round log §48): the model folds the unsigned guarantee into the open aggregate thesis because the request\n     uses \"covers\" both for \"is evidence for\" and for \"already asks this question\". The same clause sits under link clutter.\n  2. **A revenue-share thesis was opened** (Vera Rubin's share of data-center revenue), the class-A boundary of D382.\n  3. **Link clutter.** A blind recheck of the 34 disputed fresh links found 19 weak and 2 wrong; 10 accepted links mixed in as controls were all useful; the other 49 links were not rechecked.\n     The model's own account names topic or theme overlap and inference chains, and c8's closing check never reads back a link. This is\n     a likely cause, with no fix proven. Alex approved designing a protected link check through the council; a first draft was stopped\n     because it would drop founding premises and contrary facts.\n  4. **Unverified passages:** 3 of 188 fresh records and 1–3 per dev draw. The mark now travels into Step 2's records, and a reader sees the found source text or an \"Unverified quote\" label. Jev's comparison input still carries the model's text without the mark; changing it changes measured Step 2 behaviour and needs its own measurement.\n  5. **The lidar policy thesis** recurs at the boundary (one dev draw in three).\n- **History.** These earlier states are superseded:\n  - the 2026-11-07 takeover (K5 → K6b; [takeover log](../clean-arch/takeover-2026-11-07/LOG.md));\n  - the 2026-11-08 close-out (K6b test 32/42; Step 2 first lines; [Step 2 log](../clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §12);\n  - the unfiled fix round and the c1–c7 arms (round log §§1–39).\n- **No run is live.** The branch is committed through the D388 line (round log §§48–59).\n\n**Clean Step 1 source investigation (2026-11-07):** A [source-loss census](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md) traces two separate losses in the historical cleaner and records reproducible corpus observations. Offline candidate comparisons expose both recovered content and source-identity or contamination risks. A subsequent [full-page diagnostic](../clean-arch/STEP1-FULL-PAGE-RESULTS.md) completed 102 calls on 17 selected articles and independent source-backed review. It exposes examples of both missing or misattributed source text and model errors that persist despite the right text being present. A Codex–Claude council is reviewing the results and earlier attempts; no source-cleaning candidate is adopted, and the source corpus and production defaults are unchanged.\n\nAlex has authorized correcting the test-source cleaner and test data when their defects are confirmed, while the frozen production configuration and ruler remain intact. The cleaner switched 615 originally fed articles off; a replay reproduces all 615, and direct inspection confirms an actual article excluded because its 9,874-character body was flattened to one line. Other examples include a wrong-site shell, paywall and long challenge code, so the false-exclusion count and a general article-identification repair remain to be established. The [council regroup](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-REGROUP-COUNCIL.md) and [source-preservation design](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PRESERVATION-DESIGN.md) describe an isolated candidate checked against all 34,084 records, with a fake-answer actual-runner handoff and provenance display in the replay renderer. The [five source-control calls](../clean-arch/STEP1-CAPTURE-CONTROLS-RESULTS.md) completed and were source-reviewed; two items contain summary details absent from their attached quotations, and one source-visible risk explanation was not returned. This is selected-case evidence, not a general quality result. Broader source-quality comparison, citation completeness and actual writer integration remain open.\n\n**Clean architecture Step 1 (2026-11-06, isolated work on `clean-arch`):** Alex reopened work with the clean architecture. An experimental prepared-source handoff now preserves the archived page, prior cleaned text, source-role decisions and selected-line mapping without changing the original database; quotations for prepared items must also occur contiguously in the original captured page. Two seven-item runs are available in the [clean architecture PRD](../clean-arch/PRD.md), one in the earlier selection order and one in recorded page-timestamp order. The source selector is not a production service and source ordering is not established event-arrival ordering. The runs expose unresolved semantic problems: citations can omit supporting facts despite an exact location match; model output can change a benchmark into a deployment, reverse a backup relation, omit a named customer or assign an unstated currency. A blinded 84-call check-placement/wording comparison and a 27-call same-article citation-completion diagnostic did not give a clean semantic fix and are not adopted. The latter missed an omitted connectivity condition and a reversed backup relationship, and left six expected evidence reviews unaddressed. A further five-round Codex–Claude council produced a narrowly scoped Step 1 link candidate, independently checked for prompt-contract drift. A six-article, 36-call blind comparison completed; the candidate still produced an explicitly self-contradictory link and both arms consistently omitted the same Kyber report and company response. It is not adopted and has not changed the active PRD prompt. A trace confirms that both the Kyber report and open Kyber thesis were supplied, and the raw model answers omitted the report and denial; the reason is not established. See ../poc/data/clean-step1-kyber-trace-2026-11-06/TRACE.md. An isolated six-call comparison showed that hiding the Kyber thesis did not recover the report or denial in any draw. A second six-call comparison returned them in 3/3 draws with the original Kyber paragraph alone and 0/3 with the full article; length, position and surrounding content changed together, so the reason is not isolated. Excerpt outputs have source-attribution limitations. Neither experiment changed state or established a production fix. (Superseded: the next task then was a whole-article coverage approach; the current next task is under \"One next task\".) See ../clean-arch/STEP1-IMPLEMENTATION-COUNCIL.md. The earlier four-round council agreed that no tested semantic fix is ready; its next investigation is a source-backed review of the 49-item historical run and its 160 evidence records, with overlapping issue flags and separate article-level omissions, then a separate comparison of the seven-item replays. See ../clean-arch/STEP1-COUNCIL-REVIEW.md. Preserve relevant evidence while identifying the next intervention; production/frozen history remains as described below. See [Step 1 run log](../clean-arch/STEP1-RUN-LOG.md) and [English reading page](../clean-arch/architecture.html).\n\n**Sealed (D379, 2026-11-06):** Alex asked to clean up and seal this version while he re-plans the system design. Work stops here and is sealed as it is: main's working tree is committed on branch `seal/2026-11-06` (tag `sealed-2026-11-06`) and merged into `main` on Alex's word (PR #10); the sealed split and two side experiments are pushed on their own branches (D379). No PRD 11 experiment is open, and the E1 next task below is withdrawn. The next task is Alex's re-planned system design.\n\n**E1 diagnosed (2026-11-06, Alex chose E1):** of 57 helpful matters frozen production wrote and then dropped, two readers agree on 17 link misses (4 core), 3 opening misses (1 core) and 24 small matters the standard itself drops; 10 are disputed on the fallback clause and 3 were quote refusals. Opening is not the experiment. The core of the link misses is the model reading a question's period, population or named configuration as a boundary; only 8 of the 13 window draws it dropped were lost to the product. No production change; $0.02 of probes. My recommendation was to fold that narrow change into the next extraction version; withdrawn by the seal (D379). Details: `docs/research/2026-11-06-e1-no-home-drops/README.md`, PRD 11 E1.\n\n**Split extraction sealed as a failed experiment (D378, 2026-11-06):** the split (a new-question call, an evidence call and record preparation) was benchmarked against the frozen poc-v108 and failed. Its stop line fired: helpful matters 156, 156, 159 against 166, 170, 162, with about 15% higher cost. One screened revision kept more noise than production. It is sealed in the worktree `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` (tag `split-extraction-failed-sealed-2026-11-06`), and nothing of it is in main. What it taught is written as proposed production experiments in `docs/prd/11-production-experiments-from-split.md`: E1 no-home helpful drops, E2 duplicate questions, E3 ladder checks with D186, E4 judge refusals, E5 D203's revision day, E6 the measurement kit. Each opens only on Alex's word.\n\n**Question-catalog limit removed (D377, 2026-11-05):** Alex also directed removal of the separate 150 KB catalog rejection. The research reader in this worktree now has neither the catalog nor evidence byte limit; it retains full scoped questions, historical boundaries, dependencies and evidence. A portable 326,996-byte catalog test returns all 400 new questions and expands evidence both after reading the catalog and directly; the old 150 KB guard fails the same test in a scratch copy. The saved 69-question pilot catalog and all 69 saved evidence expansions match their original content hashes. Details: `docs/research/2026-11-05-catalog-limit/`. The full suite remains pending until the paid seven-day run no longer competes for resources. The run remains in its isolated worktree and does not call this research reader.\n\n**Evidence reader limit removed (D376, 2026-11-05):** Alex directed removal of the reader's 1 MB evidence-expansion rejection. In this worktree, `research_context.Reader.expand` now returns the entire evidence payload without that cap, while the catalog's separate limit remained at that checkpoint; D377 subsequently removed it. On the saved pilot ledger, all 69 individual question expansions, the four-question 1,931,225-byte pilot request and the entire 3,454,076-byte catalog produce the exact same content as the pre-change high-limit baseline; the portable oversized-ledger test passes and fails when the old cap is restored in a scratch copy. Details and snapshot-specific measurements are in `docs/research/2026-11-05-reader-limit/`. The seven-day diagnostic runs in `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/poc/data/split-fresh-seven-day-2026-11-05-v2/` with its pinned code; it does not call this research reader. Read its status through its `snapshot.sh`; do not edit its package code while it is live. This change is not an acceptance of seven-day extraction or judgement quality.\n\n**Start-state correction (2026-11-05):** Alex requires a research library established from real material available before the test start, then extraction against that state. The prior empty-library diagnostic cannot represent this case. Our own mistakes and the current 68-question provenance audit are in `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/own-mistakes-2026-11-05.zh.md`. Two new A-only calls were run on 2026-08-29 articles using the 68-question prior-day snapshot; both proposed the same aggregate supply-commitments topic. A source/context review judged it distinct from the existing specific SK Hynix question. A third A-only call on an author-body excerpt about DLSS 5, already tracked by v22, passed; the prompt also restricts software updates, so the reason is not established. No writer or dedup ran; source-history completeness remains unverified, and this is not scored acceptance. Details: ../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/start-state-diagnostic-2026-11-05.zh.md.\n\n**Measurement correction (2026-11-05):** Alex clarified that a gold-worthy topic already in the supplied library should not open again; an absent worthy topic should open. The earlier simple-A claim of a \"missed\" new topic was not scored against that rule and is withdrawn as a correctness conclusion. In the actual experiment, a Kyber topic was shown and passing follows Alex's topic-level rule; whether its exact release question captures the shipping milestone remains unverified. The experimental pre-article snapshots differ from the replay's day-start snapshots; 9 of 10 inspected examples differ. The current uncommitted replay default does not build a cold-start library; committed main HEAD still uses the old seed and startup filing path. Historical lexical coverage has a confirmed unrelated-topic match. The frozen gold and past scores remain unchanged. Audit: `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/measurement-context-scrub-2026-11-05.zh.md`.\n\n**Simple new-question diagnostic (2026-11-05):** At Alex's direction, an isolated prompt returned new questions plus article evidence without asking for product database fields. Four previously seen real dev articles each ran once: three returned one candidate, one returned none, all four quotes matched their source, and the gold-listed GPU backstop amount question was not proposed. Whether that omission is a new-topic miss depends on whether the existing library covers the topic. Two other candidates require source and independent-significance review; the actual experimental snapshot already had a Kyber topic, so its pass follows Alex's topic-level rule; whether its exact release question captures the shipping milestone remains unverified. This is not a production or full-pipeline acceptance. Details: `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/simple-new-question-results.zh.md`.\n\n**Split prompt structure v3 read diagnostic (2026-11-05):** One real reading-stage call on a previously seen article found the revenue/margin guidance and Trainium scale but omitted the gold China revenue/market-loss matter. The prewritten stop condition was triggered and the linking call was not sent. This is one draw and not a production or end-to-end result. See `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/structure-v3-read-result.zh.md`. D374 removes the model sampling field from active settings and default configs; historical config files and archives retain their original bytes.\n\n**Split prompt structure v2 small test (2026-11-05):** Thirty calls on two previously seen golden-v5 dev articles completed in `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review`. The blind model reader found complete helpful matters in the fresh control versus new A+B of 6/19 vs 10/19, 11/19 vs 7/19, and 9/19 vs 12/19. The new arm also retained more gold not-kept matters in each draw; B missed useful margin guidance and one answer reused IDs, with a demonstrated matching collision. This is not a production acceptance. See `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/structure-v2-results.zh.md` and `reports/2026-11-05-parallel-structure-v2.html`.\n\n**Split extraction small test (2026-11-05):** At Alex's request, the isolated `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` worktree ran two real golden-v5 articles once through each split lane. An independent model reader found 7 of 19 helpful gold matters fully retained, two partial, two written then dropped, eight missed, and one retained gold not-kept matter. Three saved v108 draws on these articles retained 7, 9 and 12 of 19, but used 22 seeded questions while the new test used 8/10-question snapshots; these are not directly comparable. The experiment remains unaccepted; see `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/small-scored-test-20261105.md` and `reports/2026-11-05-split-small-test.html`.\n\nAs of November 5, 2026; decisions through D374. [Decisions](DECISIONS.md) holds what is decided now; the [design page](system-design.html) and the [module PRDs](prd/README.md) hold what the code must do; the [README](../README.md) holds startup, build and run instructions.\n\n## Future evidence scoring (D372)\n\nAlex decided future gold scoring should measure helpful versus not helpful evidence without requiring separate scores for core and supporting. Use frozen golden-v5 as-is: combine core and supporting as helpful, treat not-kept as unhelpful, and keep optional entries neutral. A counting report has not yet been implemented. Historical scores retain their original definitions. D373 removes the stop caused by a separate concern about three question-gold entries: keep the gold unchanged, continue scoring and disclose the concern; see `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/ruler-conflict-2026-11-05.zh.md`.\n\n## Latest decision (D369)\n\nAlex decided in D369 to capture core and supporting evidence without a strict boundary and in D370 to remove the 22 preset starting questions and separate earnings-report import. The replay entry now defaults to `poc-v118` (D374), refuses old seeded ledgers and baseline configurations, and continues to read existing non-seeded questions; earnings releases remain normal source items. This change has local contract tests but has not passed end-to-end product acceptance. The frozen historical rulers and results remain unchanged.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '2559,2591p;3049,3088p;3685,3714p;4871,4918p;6589,6631p;9108,9155p;9383,9441p;10007,10320p' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"## D198 · 2026-10-27 · A judgement reads the evidence in view, not everything in force\n\n- Alex, 2026-10-27, on the finding that evidence in force grows without bound (v6, NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center revenue: 574 pieces after six weeks, 415 never cited by any of its 33 judgements; 40 of 316 judge requests over 30k tokens, max 67k; judging 38% of spend and rising; the run's stall came from enumerating 300+ ids): \"这是什么问题你深度思考之后改\".\n- What the problem is (design: `docs/research/2026-10-27-evidence-in-view/design.md`): two mechanisms. (1) Extraction links wide: `role-v14.txt` says \"every statement and number is, by default, evidence linked to a shown thesis\" and also \"link only to the thesis it directly concerns; omit statements with no thesis\"; the model attaches every business mention to the broadest thesis (2.5 pieces per item on v6; a liquid-cooling note, a SuperPOD purchase, a rack R&D story). Not changed here; the prompt audit is reported to Alex as the next slice. (2) Judging-v4 reads everything in force every time (\"Rejudge from all evidence currently in force\"); an analyst keeps what his view rests on and reads what arrived.\n- Decided: from judging-v5 (`poc-v30`) a judgement of a thesis is shown, of its evidence in force, the arrivals (pieces linked after the thesis's fifth most recent judgement, READINGS = 5; everything when it has fewer) and the basis (pieces cited by one of its last ten judgements, BASIS = 10). The rest stays in force in the ledger; the request names the count (\"[Not shown: N more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\"); the answer may cite and push only shown ids, checked against the request that was sent (`judging.evidence_shown_in`, as the theme block is). Nothing leaves the ledger; the repeat / replace / new rules, \"in force\", the rejudgement triggers, eligibility, the theme block and dependencies are unchanged. Gated on the prompt version: v4 ledgers and configurations read in force as before.\n- Measured ($0, `census.py`, two ledgers): among judgements after a thesis's first, the rule would have hidden 1.5% (nvda-3m) and 1.1% (r5) of the citations the judge actually made (22 of 325 and 7 of 261 judgements lose one cite, mostly third-party risk mentions listed in a \"pull the other way\" sentence); shown mean 95 and 109 pieces where in force averaged 225 and 178. Tighter rules hid 16% (cited last time + new since) or kept growing (ever cited + three readings). Arm beside control (`ab.py`, $0.03): v6, v11, v22 from the same ledger state, v4 fresh against v5: the same rung and `changed` on all three, reasons consistent, request tokens 76,476 → 12,516, 53,151 → 11,204, 25,806 → 8,425. Tests: `tests/test_evidence_in_view.py` (4, red first); suite 199.\n- Applied to the three-month run at a day boundary: 2026-08-18 finished under `poc-v29` on the serial path (the parallel runner refuses a half-done day after a code change), `poc-v30` from 2026-08-19. Prompt `judging-v5` is `judging-v4` with the first rule reworded (2,264 → 2,437 characters; the growth is the sentence that says what is not shown and who carries it).\n- Also in this slice, from the stall at 2026-08-07: a code-refused judgement answer is retired from the archive's lookup (`model.Archive.retire`; the row stays; schema-refused and malformed rows already carried a suffix), so a resume re-samples instead of replaying the refused answer; and the request's closing line names a closed set that exists (\"cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis\"; \"a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views\"): 4 of the run's 5 retried first answers had put theme ids into cites_views.\n\n## D199 · 2026-10-27 · One extraction call stays; its gate rule becomes decidable and the empty-answer exit goes (v16, poc-v32)\n\n- Alex, on the instability trace: \"不这个一次一定是可以搞过的 → 读明白，抽取 … 这一定是prompt 的问题，你如果再拆成两步这个消耗的luna calls 太多了\"; then \"你要反思自己的错误，然后下去改然后马上测试啊，不要一直diverge scope\". The two-call design (`docs/research/2026-10-27-two-step-extraction/design.md`, two review rounds) is recorded and not built.\n- Found ([log](research/2026-10-27-single-call/log.md)): the run's 493 empty extraction answers in 1,553 calls (32%; 65% of the first week's items) were the model following the written rules: \"every statement is, by default, evidence\" against \"link only to the thesis it directly concerns … omit statements with no thesis\", decided once per page, so a customer, partner or product page was omitted whole (asked directly, the model names that rule: a national lab buying Vera Rubin CPU servers \"describes a customer project, not NVIDIA's reported sales\"). The five-send benchmarks before this were sent with an alphabetised schema (the archive stores sorted keys; production sends the dict as built) and overstated the empties; in production order no send of three articles was empty. A second mistake stopped the first week test: code edited while the parallel runner was mid-day.\n- Decided: extraction v16 (`role-v16.txt`: one rule, \"a statement is evidence to each shown thesis whose answer its fact or figure makes more or less likely\", with the customer/supplier/competitor/regulator and policy cases and the omit cases; the closing line \"Return empty arrays only for a page with no business content\", `extraction.EMPTY_LINE_FROM`; v12's admission reasons, `V12_REASON_VERSIONS`). `poc-v32.json` is the configuration. Measured on the same 98 items of 2026-07-01..07 against the paused run (v12, whole pages): empty handovers 65% → 32%, the model's own empties 55 → 8, evidence per item 1.1 → 2.7, candidates 9 → 11, week cost $0.22. v17 (`poc-v33`, an account per mandatory paragraph in the same call) held the piece set across sends (A3: Jaccard 0.6–0.8 against 0.4–0.5) but tripled the pieces with court-case minutiae and opened 5 theses where v16 opened 11, at twice the extraction and matching cost: refuted for production, kept as the record of the shape.\n- The three-month run restarts from 2026-07-01 with poc-v32 on the cleaned database (D256) in `poc/data/english-v32-nvda-3m-2026-10-27/`; the poc-v29/v30 run stays paused and untouched. Open, named: links read widely (every piece to three or four theses; D198's \"links wide\"), candidates for product and gaming news (no thesis list entry fits them), the cold start's \"no thesis fits\" drops.\n\n- Amendment, 2026-10-27 23:20: Alex stopped the restarted run (\"我不是要你看下step 1抽取到底行不行？\") and asked for the prompt to be read and fixed, with examples (\"什么叫重要陈述，你也没给例子\"). Arms B1–B6 ([log](research/2026-10-27-single-call/log.md) §10–17) rewrote the role as the model reads it: evidence first; four kinds of material statement with examples; a walk over the whole page (\"a page with eight such statements yields eight evidence items\"); a reject list with examples; links decided statement by statement; a rumour bears on a thesis as a confirmed statement does; T8's reading block narrowed to forecasts, ratings, targets and cited figures. Extraction v18 (`role-v18.txt`, `categories-v18.json`, `poc-v34.json`) supersedes v16 as the candidate configuration: on six articles sent five times each every material statement is present in every send; at scale (243 items of the run, one send each) 2% of answers are empty against 32% under v12 and 7–8 material statements come out per page, of which the theses that exist absorb 18% in the first week and 48% in the last. v17 and the two-call design stay refuted and unbuilt. The restarted run (`english-v32-nvda-3m-2026-10-27`, $0.215, 119 items) is paused; whether a run resumes, and with poc-v34, is Alex's call.\n\n## D200 · 2026-10-27 · Extraction v18 (`poc-v34`) is the production default\n\n- Alex, after the step 1 verdict (D199 amendment): \"is step one and step two done? … whatever latest fixed version that you have, it just needs to be on production for these two. Do it, and then we move to step three and four.\"\n- Decided: `poc/config/poc-v34.json` (extraction T*-v18; matching, collision judge and judging as `poc-v30`) is the replay default (`scripts/replay.py --config`, README). Evidence: the arms B3–B6 and the two extraction-only scale tests in the [log](research/2026-10-27-single-call/log.md) §12–17 (243 items, 4 empty answers against 32% under v12, 7–8 material statements per page), recounted from the scratch archive before this decision.\n- Acceptance on the product's own path: one week (2026-07-01..07, NVDA, cleaned database) replayed through extraction, matching and judging with `poc-v34` in `poc/data/english-v34-nvda-week-2026-10-27/`, compared with the v16 week (`compare_week.py`). The result is appended to the log; a defect found there is diagnosed under §1 before any further change.\n- Step 2 on the review page (the complete retrieval entry point of D250, main branch) is verified as a component and has no production consumer until step 3 (question selection) exists; nothing of it goes to production in this decision. The paused runs (`english-v29/v30`, `english-v32`) stay paused; whether a three-month run resumes with `poc-v34` is Alex's call.\n- Found while switching the default, before the acceptance run: `extraction.ACCOUNT_FROM = 17` made v18 send v17's per-paragraph account shape in production, while arms B3–B6 and both scale tests were sent with the flat shape (`arm.py` restores the stored request and replaces only the role, the closing line and T8's reading block). The harness bytes were not the production bytes (AGENTS §5). Fixed at the owner: `ACCOUNT_VERSIONS = (17,)` and `extraction.accounts(version)`; test `test_v18_sends_the_flat_answer_shape…` (red on the old code, green after); the production `poc-v34` request now equals the scale test's bytes for all 243 items (system, schema keys and evidence item, user text from the item header). Suite 205 pass; preflight exit 0.\n\n## D201 · 2026-10-27 · Steps 3 and 4: Jev gives opinions only, the library keeps sources and research arguments, a new question needs an independent investment judgement; gold first, small scope, root cause before any change\n\n- Alex, to the three choices on the review page (`docs/research/2026-10-27-pipeline-review/index.html`, main branch): \"我都同意但一样你的黄金标准要有然后自己下去改和量化测试，先小范围验证可行，深度解读错误的trace不要乱做test和乱改一定要知道问根因\".\n- Decided: (1) Jev's output is an opinion with a score (same statement / revision / different; direct result / business lead / background / unrelated / uncertain); whether a source is kept, merged or placed is decided by the analysis step that reads the complete old and new content. (2) The library keeps both the source records (immutable, for tracing) and the research argument each question carries, updated as a new version with what changed, its supporting and opposing sources, whether they are independent, and its limitations; the judgement reads the argument and its sources, not only the flat evidence list. (3) A new question opens only on an independent investment judgement with the full admission contract; a new product, a new figure or a narrower scope alone does not open one.\n- Method, Alex's: a written gold before the first send (reference answers for every case, held-out cases included), one small scope (about 20 incoming records at their historical library state) measured against a fresh control of the current path, quantified per case; a failure is traced to its raw answer and its mechanism before any test or change; no tuning on the cases that were read.\n- This decision changes the thesis-library PRD (06-views: the argument record and its writer/reader) and the matching PRD (Jev as opinion); the PRD text follows the measured build, not before it. No production change and no paid call in this decision itself.\n- Acceptance result ([log §18](research/2026-10-27-single-call/log.md)): the v18 week through the whole path, 98 items, 361 calls, none failed, $0.22 (equal to the v16 week). The model left 4 answers empty against 8; 222 evidence rows against 213; 9 theses against 11, the two missing being v16's noise theses (Xeon 6 host CPU, Intel 18A wafers). Items that end with no evidence rise from 32% to 51% because v18 writes 506 statements with empty links where v16 stretched them to the nearest financial thesis, and the code drops them (D42); read on the eight largest items, every extra v16 piece was such a stretch. The one real loss is the cold start (the CNBC Kyber-delay statements of 07-05 dropped a day before the Kyber question opens) and 14 capex pieces pointing at theme theses the extraction request does not show: both on D199's open list, neither new. v18 is accepted and stays the default.\n## D260 · 2026-11-02 · A judgement is a forecast against a reference: the rung the evidence in force makes most likely, judged against what the market expects; the answer states the reference and the implied outcome (judging-v6)\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-02, asked whether the judge's stance should stay an absolute-band lookup (the current reading) or be judged relative to expectations: \"门槛没有标准，按照你作为 hedge fund analyst 的认识来定\". Decided by Claude under that delegation: the definition below is Claude's analyst judgement, recorded as such, not Alex's words.\n- Mechanism (arm 4 draw 1, all 356 judgements read, `research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/review-2026-11-02.md` §7 and `proof-judging-v6.txt`): 8 of 314 later judgements moved a rung and 320 of 356 reasons say \"remains\"; the FY2027 total revenue question was judged 12 times at neutral while its upward pushes grew from 1 to 35, every reason \"the $392 billion estimate is inside the band … does not establish $400 billion\"; the gross-margin judgement wrote \"leans toward rung 2 but does not warrant moving\"; the 2027 hyperscaler-capex theme stayed below the band management's own forecast names because the forecast \"is not fact\". The judging-v5 prompt asked the judge to \"be precise about what is established\" and said only fact can establish a rung: the model read \"established\" as the bar for leaving a rung and the consensus figure as the anchor. A second cause stays open: the ladder's neutral band is written at opening without naming what it compares against (PRD 06 ladder rule 2 asks for it); that is the extraction's rule and is not changed here.\n- Decided, the analyst's rule: (1) the rung is a forecast, the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. (2) The reference is what the market expects: consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline; a consensus or guidance figure alone keeps the judgement at the reference's rung and, once other evidence has arrived, is never the reason to stay. (3) The judge derives the outcome the evidence implies (reported figures and guidance give a run-rate; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it), checks it against the dependencies' conclusions (a part cannot exceed its whole; a figure spanning several periods or products gives the question only its share), compares it with the rung boundaries and moves when it crosses one. (4) Weight follows tier and novelty: fact fixes what is known; party statements are the strongest forecast input; third-party views and rumours move the forecast in proportion to their specificity; a repeat, another outlet or an opinion with no new fact or calculation moves nothing. (5) The answer states `reference` and `implied_outcome` as required fields from `judging-v6` (`judging.schema_for`; code refuses an empty one; stored beside the rung in `judgements.reference` / `implied_outcome`; a ledger written before v6 gains the columns at its first write), so the D3 stance gold can read the reasoning, not only the rung. Written where the judgement is specified: PRD 06 State rules (`docs/system-design/new-facts.js` STATE_RULES, the generated block) and this record.\n- Proof (`proof-judging-v6.txt`, `proof-judging-v6b.txt`: the last stored judgement request of 14 theses of arm 4 draw 1 re-sent once each under v6 and read beside the stored v5 answers, about a cent): every answer names the reference and derives the implied outcome. Six rungs moved against the stored ones: non-GAAP gross margin 3 → 2 (Q1 reported 75% and Q2 guided 75% sit on the rung-2 boundary), 2027 hyperscaler capex 4 → 5 (management's own \"more than $1 trillion\"), China accelerator share 4 → 5 (share \"fallen to zero\"), China H200 authorization No → Yes (a reported plan to permit named firms and reported progress in reviews, six months before the deadline), Data Center revenue 2 → 3 ($355–360 billion derived from Q1 and the total-revenue proxy, below the $360 billion boundary), Blackwell+Rubin revenue 2 → 1 in the first send by annualising a two-year \"$1 trillion visibility\" figure to $545 billion, a part above its whole: clause (3)'s consistency check was added for it and the re-send gives rung 2 ($320–360 billion, a share of the Data Center segment). Total revenue stays at 3 with the arithmetic shown (H1 $172.6 billion from Q1 and the Q2 guide; $400 billion needs more than $227 billion in H2). Prompt 2,453 → 3,782 characters: the request defined neither what a rung means nor a reference and its \"established\" wording forced the defect; the two sentences that carried it were cut. Configuration `poc-v90` (poc-v89 with judging-v6); the default stays `poc-v69`. Tests: `tests/test_stance_fields.py`. Measured by re-sent stored requests, not by an arm (Alex: \"不要再跑这些有的没的臂了\").\n\n## D261 · 2026-11-02 · A ruler or gold that is built and reviewed is never changed again\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-02, while the judgement ruler (D3) was being built: \"你的尺子做出来和金标真的不能再动了，已经造成了3天的各种漂移，非常的严重，我需要你记录到我们的决策和原则里面，我们做完的review过的尺子金标不能再动了\".\n- The drift he names, counted: the extraction ruler went from the first standard (D229, 2026-10-29) to the signed page version 3 (D243, the same day) to version 5 (D250, 2026-10-31), the gold was re-derived from version 3 to version 5 with 62 recorded changes, and every score between D248 and 2026-10-31 had been read against the version-3 gold (the \"假的测量\" of 2026-10-31); the judgement step had no ruler at all until today. Three days of numbers that cannot be compared with each other.\n- Decided (his words; written at the decision it governs, AGENTS §7.1): once a ruler and its gold have been reviewed (readers who did not write them read them against the decisions; Alex signed), neither the definition nor the gold changes again. A defect found later is a dated correction record beside the gold (`corrections-*.json`), printed with every score and never merged in. A new definition is a new version that only his decision opens, with its own gold derived from scratch; old scores stay as they were and are never compared with the new. A ruler under construction is edited only until that review, and nothing is scored against it before.\n- What is locked by this record: the extraction standard page (`docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html`, version 3 signed D243, with D250's four boundaries and counting unit) and `golden-v5/` (sha256 in `golden-v5.sha256`): no version 6; the loop's desk ruler (`research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/README.md`, D1–D3). What is still under construction and locks at his review: the judgement ruler `research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md`, its reader brief, question list and gold `gold-v1.json` (the sign-off is a separate record).\n\n## D262 · 2026-11-02 · The judgement ruler (D3) and its gold are fixed: the gold is reader B's answers; frozen at sha256 636c056d1e4252fa…\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-02, after reading the two readers' reasoning on every disagreement (readers A and B differed on 3 of 23 questions by range or yes/no and on 12 of 20 direction labels; the system disagreed with the draft on the total revenue and Vera CPU questions in every draw): \"那就不要理A了；我们就go with B 好吧，定下尺子金表\".\n- Fixed by that decision: the ruler `research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md` (unit: a gold question at the window end 2026-07-14; material: golden-v5's 309 items by sha; a numeric question agrees when the gold's implied range overlaps the band of the rung the judge chose, a yes/no question when the answers match; the two null baselines printed with every score); the question list and the must_open mapping as built (`questions-v1.json`, 22 seeds + the Ohio guarantee); the gold `gold-v1.json` = reader B's 23 answers verbatim (B: a fresh-context Claude reader, model-authored, 3 questions marked no material: CoWoS capacity, semiconductor growth, SK Telecom), sha256 `636c056d1e4252fa759660803067e6ccd67a81067b012905e918aa9dec4005f2` in `gold-v1.sha256` and in `score_judgement.py`, which refuses any other. Reader A (Codex) stays beside it as the control record (`reader-A.json`, `reconcile-v1.md`): its distance from B is the stick's own noise. Claude's two proposals of the afternoon (a three-level score when readers split; toss-up questions excluded) were not adopted: one range per question, every question counts. From here D261 applies: nothing in the ruler or the gold changes; a defect is a dated record in `corrections-v1.json`.\n- First measurement on the frozen gold (`score-r4-identity.txt`, arm 4, three draws, judging-v5): judged questions agreeing 13/19, 15/18, 12/17; the lookup baseline (the rung containing the gold's reference figure) 9/15, 8/14, 8/14; always-neutral 5/11, 5/10, 5/10. Disagreeing in every draw: FY2027 total revenue (gold $400–435 billion, judge neutral by consensus lookup) and Vera CPU revenue (gold $8–18 billion, judge the top rung on management's $20 billion at face value); in two of three: the financing-commitments total (gold $25–60 billion, judge $10–20 billion). Never judged in any draw: NVIDIA's own capex (q12); the operating margin in two draws (q10): evidence never reached them, counted apart as \"no judgement\". The new judge (`judging-v6`, D260) has no run to score yet; a run on `poc-v90` is Alex's call.\n\n## D263 · 2026-11-02 · The judge is written to the frozen gold's own reasoning (judging-v7, poc-v91); a gap to the gold is closed in the judge, not re-measured\n\n- Alex, after the first score on the frozen gold was reported as the system being \"wrong\" on some questions: \"不是啊什么叫错？我不是和你说了就按照claude的标准来就好了嘛\". The gold is Claude's (reader B's) standard by D262; a disagreement is the distance between the judge and that standard, to be closed in the judge's rule, not a verdict to be measured again.\n- What reader B does that judging-v6 did not, read from B's answers on the questions that differed (`judgement-gold/reconcile-v1.md`, `gold-v1.json`): carries the guided growth forward rather than annualising the last quarter flat (Data Center, total revenue); discounts a management target for a product with no shipments by default (Vera CPU: two selling quarters, four million units at $5,000, the company's own choice of another CPU); sizes an announced but unsized commitment from its disclosed comparable (the Firmus backstop from Sharon's); derives a figure no one states from the accounting identities in the evidence (capex from operating less free cash flow); keeps linked conclusions consistent (China share against the H200 authorization theme). Written into `judging-v7.txt` as five clauses (v6 3,782 → 4,924 characters; reason: each clause is a behaviour of the frozen standard the judge lacked), named by `poc-v91`; schema and code unchanged.\n- Proof on the stored requests of the questions whose arm-4 judgement differed from the gold (`proof-judging-v7a.txt`, `proof-judging-v7.txt`; one send each, cents): total revenue rung 3 → 2 (about $421 billion from the guided growth on FY2026's base; gold $400–435 billion), Data Center 3 → 2 (about $372 billion; gold $370–400 billion), Vera CPU 1 → 2 ($12 to below $20 billion, the target discounted for the volume not in hand; gold $8–18 billion), DRAM prices 3 → 2 (about +15%; gold +10–35%), financing total stays 4 ($33.5–39.8 billion; gold $25–60 billion), the Ohio guarantee stays Yes; China share stays below 1% in draw 1, consistent with that draw's H200 theme judged No (the gold has the authorization likely and the share 1–8%; the consistency clause ties the share to the theme, so it moves with it). Six of the seven re-sent questions now land in the gold's band; the end-to-end agreement under judging-v7 needs a run on `poc-v91`, which is Alex's call. A placement gap seen on the way: the Vera CPU question never held the fall-launch statement (filed under the Rubin launch questions), so the judge could not count selling quarters from evidence; the default-discount clause covers it, the filing is a D2 class to count.\n\n## D264 · 2026-11-02 · The extraction reads the day's open questions with their takes lines and files the statements itself (extraction v72, poc-v92): the one-decider architecture Alex chose is built and measured against poc-v91 on the frozen ruler\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-02 evening, after asking why the open library was never adopted as the extraction's context (\"你为什么架构上没有采纳我说的开的库作为step 2 context\"; the answer traced the chain from his 10-31 instruction through the one-draw v82 replay I judged \"不采用\" myself to the D256 proposal left waiting): \"我们非常专注的验证这个架构选择：我们有金标和尺子frozen可以比；我们这个架构设计的核心就是透过适当的Prompt 和引入已经开的题，和题的叙述来决定抽取；现在最新的prompt有修改了很多小毛病，你看是不是用最新最好的prompt + v72这边告诉模型怎么用读取开题库的prompt 来做新的实验prompt。充分理解我的ask然后get it worked out\". This is his choice of the one-decider architecture (review-2026-11-02.md §4 way (i), the D256 proposal) and of its base prompt.\n- Built (design: `research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/design-v72.md`, the four tables of AGENTS §2, predictions frozen at sha256 `aec1bb40…` in `predictions-v72.sha256` before any send): extraction version 72, named by `poc/config/poc-v92.json` (= poc-v91 with T*-v72 and no placement setting). The request shows the questions open at the start of the item's day (D251's snapshot) each with the code-written takes line the placer read (`placement.question_lines`, one writer for both requests); the schema defines the evidence field `links` by the same rule (`placement.FILING_RULE`, the placer's v4 system-prompt rule restated beside the field: the narrowest open question whose takes line covers the statement, whoever says it, with or without a period or amount, plus the answer's own candidates, else empty); `role-v72.txt` is `role-v66.txt` with its first paragraph pointing at [Open questions] and the links field (the latest extraction prompt on the frozen extraction gold: v53 → v65 gold-rejected kept per draw about 109 → 59 with core and supporting flat, v66 = v65 + R3's boundary, `r3-final-scores-Y6.txt`); `categories-v72.json` and `review-v72.txt` are v66's. The extraction's links file each statement: one linked to an open question is filed there, one linked only to its candidates follows them, one linked to nothing is dropped as 'no home' (D209; the handover's reason is the same word, so `e2e_funnel` and `dropped_census` read it); no placement call exists for v72 (`extraction.one_decider`, `places` ends at v71). v71's `new_in_it` and `moves` fields are not asked (`MOVES_FIELDS_VERSIONS`). Settled by me under the delegation of 2026-11-01, and said so: the candidate rules stay v66's (propose when the item raises it; code owns identity and folds a duplicate of an open question, D258), not v71's \"only for a matter no open question covers\", which halved the candidates and the homes in v82; it is one sentence to tighten if the questions ruler says so.\n- Two wirings of D251's snapshot were wrong and are fixed in the same change, each with a test that fails on its mutant (`tests/test_one_decider.py`): (1) before the first completed day the snapshot was empty, so the seeds (seq 0, D253) were invisible to every first-day item while the placer of poc-v91 saw them (`store.day_start_seq` is 0 before the first completed day); (2) the takes line prints a question's group names and aliases, which D257's alias merges change within a day, so the request bytes would have changed mid-day, the parallel day would have re-sent every waiting request, and two items of one day would have seen different lists (`store.groups_of_view_at` reads memberships and aliases at the snapshot's seq). Version gates: v53's and v71's request bytes are unchanged (tests); 274 tests pass; `scripts/preflight.py` over arm 4 draw 1 replays 243 stored answers through the current checks with the same 22 refusals as before (`preflight-v72-prep.txt`).\n- Proof before the run (cents; `harness/resend_extract_v72.py`, `proof-v72-links.txt`: the four rack-delay articles of 2026-07-05/06 rebuilt under v72 from arm 4 draw 1's day-start snapshots, three sends each; the stored outcome of the same run as the control). The statements that name nothing (\"Our roadmap is intact\", the scrapped backup plan, NVL72x2 back-to-back racks), which the placer dropped or filed under same-day duplicate questions in every arm since 10-31, are filed under the seed question v18 (Rubin Ultra / Kyber NVL144 shipping) in 3/3 sends of the SemiAnalysis article (both statements), 3/3 of the Tom's Hardware article (NVL72x2; the denial 2/3 with one send's quote refused), 3/3 of the Cramer article (to v18, v2 or v31 by send), while in the Asian-suppliers article the NVL72x2 statement went once to no home and twice to the revenue questions (v1/v2, v2/v20: a sink link; the Kyber question v33 the control's placer used was opened that same day and is outside the snapshot). Zero candidates in 27 sends over nine matched articles against the control's four, all four Kyber duplicates of v18 (the list shown does the deduplication the matching step did after the fact). Request size 22–38K characters (27–44 open questions) against the control's mean 19.8K.\n- The measurement, launched 2026-11-02 18:05 at his \"get it worked out\": `arms/r6-one-decider-v92/d1..3` against `arms/r6-control-v91/d1..3` (the acceptance run he stopped at 17:27, now the control, since arm 4 and v92 are separated by D258, D259 and D263 as well), 2026-07-01..14, seeds v2, eight extraction workers each, cap $3 a draw; scored on the frozen ruler (`e2e_funnel.py`, `score_judgement.py`), regressions first, every number, in `log.md` row 12 and `design-v72.md` §8. Whether poc-v92 becomes the default is his call after the numbers.\n- Measured (six draws finished 19:40; `e2e-funnel-r6-v72.txt`, `judgement-gold/score-r6-*`, `matter-states-r6-v72.txt`, `probe-v72-sink.txt`; three-draw means, v91 → v92). Regressions first: core matters reaching judgement 76.3 → 73.7 of 86 (predicted ≥ +2, the null is ±2), core dropped for no home 4.0 → 5.7, article-opened questions 11.7 → 3.0 (candidates 65 → 6.7: the list shown suppresses proposals even with the candidate rule unchanged; must_open 28/28 and may_open 40/42 in both arms because the seeds cover them, the Ohio guarantee being the one visible loss), supporting not extracted 25.3 → 46.0. Held: supporting reaching 94.0 → 90.0 (at the −4 line), noise reaching 89.2 → 56.9 (−36%), judgement agreement 17/19, 17/18, 17/19 → 15/18, 16/17, 17/18 against lookup 8–9/14–15, cost $1.15 → $0.88 a draw with model time −36% and wall clock 95 → 46 minutes for three draws, zero re-sent requests. Two mechanisms, both in sentences and neither in the architecture: (1) the sink is back: 60–62% of links on the three company-wide revenue questions against v91's 34–40%, 190–208 statements a draw under both total and Data Center revenue against 5–6; the model's own account in three of three cases cites the figure-line takes clause D259 added (\"what makes that figure more or less likely: the demand, supply, competition and pricing behind it\") and concedes the links field's \"narrowest only\" should have excluded the broader question or the link altogether; (2) role-v66's end-of-request check removes 21 supporting matters a draw that v53 keeps (20 matters extracted in v91 in at least two draws and in v92 in at most one: third parties' actions and views, named hires and partners, product milestones, standard §5.1 kinds 2–3), a loss the extraction bench's \"supporting in at least two draws\" count had hidden. Verdict: as built, v72 is neither better nor worse than v91 on the desk's counts and is cleaner, cheaper and faster; it does not become the default and no further arm is sent before the two mechanisms are fixed at their owners with $0 proofs: the root/driver pair collapsed to the narrower question by code (identity, D207's rule), the figure-line clause reworded and proved on re-sent stored v92 requests, and the base text (v53 against v66 under the architecture) put to Alex.\n\n## D265 · 2026-11-02 · Extraction v72 / poc-v92 is the version: the default configuration, the base every later change builds on, the control of every later measurement; D207's narrowest rule is enforced in code\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-02 evening, on the measured v72: \"for the current version that you just fixed, I think it's highly probable that it's going to be the candidate. So, clean up the versioning. Like, whatever we just did, I think this is going to be the latest version. Use this, and don't drift again. With that, start to experiment the next steps that you talked about on what you need to do to fix the problems that you have discovered that still could be enhanced.\" The base-text question of D264 (v53 against v66) is thereby answered: the version is v72 as measured (role-v66's text under the architecture); its supporting-recall loss is fixed inside it, not by reverting its base.\n- Versioning from here: `poc/config/poc-v92.json` is the default (`scripts/replay.py`, the default-entry test `test_replay_script_defaults_run_the_default_jev_configuration_with_sixteen_and_eight_workers`, the README name it; `poc-v69` stays as the record of the placement architecture). A change to an extraction text is a new number (v73, v74 …: its own role, categories and review files, byte-identical where the text is unchanged) and a new `poc` configuration built from v92; a code rule applies under every configuration and changes no number. Every later measurement names poc-v92 as its control, three draws each, on the frozen ruler (§7.1); the 2026-11-02 draws `arms/r6-one-decider-v92/d1..3` are its baseline (`e2e-funnel-r6-v72.txt`). Nothing of the request shape (the day-start snapshot with takes lines, the links definition, no placement call) changes again without a decision in his words.\n- The first fix, code, $0 (the sink of D264): `extraction.narrowest_links`, applied in `file_reading` to every filing from v20 on: a statement linked to a question and to a question that question depends on (the ledger's `dependencies`, the seeds' root-and-driver pairs, transitively) keeps the narrower one only, which is D207's \"narrowest question only\" made an identity rule where the ledger knows the hierarchy. Replayed over the three v92 ledgers: links 967/921/907 → removed 302/310/285 (31–34%), no statement left without a link (the rule keeps the narrower); the three revenue questions' share and the per-question piles after the rule are printed in `design-v72.md` §9. Test `test_a_statement_filed_under_a_driver_and_its_root_keeps_the_driver_only`.\n## D354 · 2026-11-03 · Steps 3 and 4 (opening and matching questions, judging them) are frozen as `poc-v108` runs them, after three draws on the frozen extraction\n\n- Alex, after the extraction was frozen (D274): \"now that the extraction stage is done and frozen, we freeze that as well\"; on the plan to\n  run the whole line on the frozen extraction and freeze steps 3 and 4 if their numbers did not get worse: \"好你跑\"; then \"那你跑了吧，我们赶紧确认step 3,4 可以freeze\".\n- The run (`research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/log.md` row 29): `poc-v108` with the 22 frozen seeds over 2026-07-01..07-14, three draws at once\n  from a pinned worktree, predictions and freeze conditions written before the send (`predictions-r9-frozen-v108.md`, sha256 beside it).\n  All four conditions hold: every draw finished with no halted day; must-open questions 28/28 in every draw; the judgement agrees with the\n  frozen gold on 15/17, 15/17 and 16/17 judged questions (control `poc-v94`: 16/18, 16/19, 16/18) and no question that agreed in all three\n  control draws disagrees in all three new ones; core matters reaching judgement average 77.7 (control 74.3) and estimated noise reaching\n  judgement 49.5 (bound 51).\n- Frozen: the collision judge's prompt `collision-v2`, the judge's prompt `judging-v7`, the matching prompt the configuration names\n  (`matching-v3`), the judge's answer schema and the three questions the Jev matcher is asked; `tests/test_frozen_questions_and_judgement.py`\n  pins them (a one-character change to a prompt or to a matcher question fails it, both mutants checked). The configuration itself is\n  pinned by `tests/test_frozen_extraction_prompt.py`. A change is a new version only Alex opens.\n- Known defects, recorded and not fixed: (1) the collision judge splits one bet on a fine detail: the seed H200 question and an identical\n  candidate were judged distinct (\"any\" against \"public\" authorization) in one draw; ten re-sends of the stored request gave the same\n  question 8 times, so about one encounter in five opens a duplicate; (2) the frozen extraction proposed the lidar-legislation question the\n  gold rules out in one draw of three; (3) the operating-margin question (gold q10) had one or two pieces in each control draw and none in\n  any new draw, so it was never judged; (4) the root-revenue fallback of the frozen extraction raised the total-revenue question's share of\n  all links from 9–12% to 23–24%, and the narrower questions lose the evidence that answers them: Broadcom's AI-chip revenue ($10.8bn in Q2,\n  $16.0bn guided for Q3), the gold's basis for custom-chip spending (q16), was filed under that question in two of three control draws and in\n  none of the frozen ones (it went to the total-revenue, data-center and accelerator-share questions), and the judge then chose below $40bn\n  three times against the gold's $60–85bn; defect (3) is the same class. Over 14 days the judge requests stayed under 18,500 tokens, but a longer window is where evidence bloat\n  appeared before (the three-month run stopped and sealed at 2026-08-28). (5) `scripts/preflight.py` cannot judge the one-decider\n  architecture (D264): it does not rebuild the questions open at the time, so every stored link reads as \"links to nothing shown\".\n\n## D355 · 2026-11-04 · The filing topic closes without a change: filing is about 85% right; the remaining misses are recorded as known defects\n\n- Alex opened the topic on 2026-11-03 (\"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\"),\n  set the method on 2026-11-04 (\"We need to validate the hypothesis in a very simple way and to make sure that it works on a high level before we\n## D439 · 2026-11-13 · While Alex is not active, the council decides by consensus and the loop iterates to the PRD's production goals\n\n- **Alex, 2026-11-13** (unprompted, after a recap of the goal): \"把这个goal 改成更多的要求council in the loop 来自行的根据数据和目标做决策。我接下来不会active,需要你有问题的时候就fire council 然后和claude 掰扯直到你们能达到共识为止再下去执行，并仔细的测量，自己看trace 然后把每一轮log 做学习总结，不要饭一样的错，词学迭代完成我们的PRD生产目标\".\n- **Decided (his words):** while he is not active, every choice or next step that would have gone to him goes to the council: an independent read-only Codex review and Claude argue it on the data, the PRD and its quantified goals until they reach a consensus; the consensus is recorded as a decision citing D439, executed, measured, every trace read by Claude itself, and every round logged with a learning summary so the same mistake is not made twice; the loop iterates until the PRD's production goals are met.\n- **Settled by Claude (wording and limits, not confirmed by Alex):** the standing limits of AGENTS.md stay (the frozen ruler never changes; nothing pushed, deployed, published or committed unasked; credentials and provider caps untouched). Opening a new version of a piece frozen for \"a new version only Alex opens\" is a council decision under D439; the frozen ruler is not. A disagreement the council cannot settle is recorded with both positions and the cheaper, reversible course is taken. **Spending ceiling:** paid model calls in this period stay within $40 at published rates in total, each paid step with its own ceiling in its council-agreed design; this figure is Claude's setting, not Alex's, and is reported in every status update.\n- **Where written:** AGENTS.md, \"Authority and scope\" (the D439 bullet) and §7.3 \"Own the loop\"; `docs/CURRENT_STATE.md`; the memory file `feedback-council-decides-while-alex-away.md`. The session goal itself is set by Alex's `/goal` command; the replacement text is given to him in the reply.\n- **Scope:** all modules Alex has taken up; the current work is clean Step 1 (gates 2 and 3 of `clean-arch/step1-diagnosis-2026-11-12/COMBINED-PLAN.md`), then the Step 4 judgement faults that kept Stage B from passing.\n\n## D440 · 2026-11-13 · Gate 2 failed; a memory diagnostic decides whether c15yc goes on (council consensus under D439)\n\n- **Council** (D439; Alex not active): an independent Codex member and Claude argued four rounds (`clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/mj-*`). Agreed facts: gate 2 failed under its declared rules (two stop lines on the memory article); the link line measured the wrong thing (c8's request returns an uncovered statement with empty links); the 3-of-9 against 0-of-9 memory-thesis difference is real and its cause is not established. The council's first position was to keep c8; Claude's was a larger re-send. The consensus, after Claude adopted the council's changes (a margin fixed in advance, a three-way result in which inconclusive is not a pass, retained statements decisive, an exercised-control rule, a named and simulation-validated interval): \"Council position: consensus on the rule in mj-challenge-2.md as amended by mj-challenge-3.md\" (`mj-codex-4.md`).\n- **Decided:** the diagnostic in `clean-arch/step1-diagnosis-2026-11-12/gate2/MEMORY-DIAGNOSTIC.md` runs (108 sends, ceiling $2.0); its rule is `gate2/memory_rule.py`, frozen by sha256. c15yc goes on to gate 3's design only on \"proceed\" for both decisive outcomes; otherwise c8 stays and the council decides the next step. The gate-2 failure stays recorded.\n- **Amended 2026-11-13 (council round mj-5, `council/mj-codex-5.md`: \"Council position: consensus on (a)\"):** the 11 jobs the $2.0 ceiling never sent are sent alone, same requests, under a $1.2 completion ceiling (a recorded departure from D440's step ceiling; the $40 total unchanged), before any diagnostic answer is read; the combined sample must hold 18 per arm per draw, or the diagnostic is inconclusive.\n\n## D441 · 2026-11-13 · The memory diagnostic does not let c15yc go on: the year change stops, c8 stays (rule fixed by council consensus, D440)\n\n- **Result** (`clean-arch/step1-diagnosis-2026-11-12/gate2/MEMORY-RESULT.md`): memory-price thesis 17 of 54 (c15yc) against 28 of 54 (c8), difference -20.4 points, 90% interval (-34.6, -4.8): inconclusive under the frozen rule; retention 49 against 49: proceed. The rule required both to proceed, so c15yc does not go on.\n- **Decided (by the rule the council fixed before the send, D440; no new judgement):** the combined Step 1 candidate c15yc stops; c8 stays in force. The year change moved its error to a neighbouring case (capex years separated, the memory question pushed from the ruler's 2027-against-2026 shape to 2026), so under D391 it is not tuned further on these articles. Change 2 alone (the scoped capacity counterexample) is not carried as its own version without a council decision: its measured target without change 1 is one doubtful aggregate in 729 c8 calls.\n- **Next:** the council chooses the next step toward the PRD's production goal (D439).\n\n## D442 · 2026-11-13 · Next step: D431's same-day end-to-end comparison, run as a diagnostic (council consensus under D439)\n\n- **Council** (`clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/mk-*`): Claude's reflection written first (`mk-my-reflection.md`); an independent Codex member investigated cold and reached the same recommendation (`mk-codex.md`: \"Run the bounded D431 comparison as a diagnostic, then choose the repair from the final-answer losses\"), correcting two of Claude's statements (the comparison does not by itself identify the largest cause; Stage B also missed its last-judgement line on the strictest reading). Consensus: \"Council position: consensus on the next step in mk-challenge-1.md\" (`mk-codex-2.md`).\n- **Decided:** the next step is D431's same-day comparison as a diagnostic, never an acceptance and never waiving Step 4's failed lines: the clean path (c8, frozen Steps 2–3, the ladder step as it ran in the fourth Stage A, the frozen judge) against production as it now runs (poc-v116, no preset questions), the 14-day window, three draws each, the seven graded questions reported one by one, every lost answer read back through the opened question, the evidence it received and the judgement. Before any send: the comparison, matching rules, stop conditions and ceiling frozen in a design document; both paths' requests verified at no cost by replay; a component-by-component cost estimate from stored runs; a council review of the design until a round finds nothing serious.\n- **Correction, 2026-11-13:** \"production as it now runs\" is `poc-v118` (D370, D374, the replay default; `scripts/replay.py` refuses `poc-v116`, which carries the retired baseline block). D442's \"poc-v116\" above is a factual error of mine; the decision means v118, as D431 says (\"production as it now runs\").\n\n## D443 · 2026-11-13 · Shape B': an exploratory census of final-answer losses, the stored clean chain against fresh production v118 draws (council consensus under D439)\n\n- **Council** (`clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ml-*`, five rounds in one resumed session): version 1 was not ready (shape B departed from D442's same day; no production reader; incomplete outcomes; no $0 replay; an unenforced ceiling); each round's findings were answered with code and results (a production record reader that reproduces the frozen scorer on 69 outcomes; an evidence-visibility reader validated against an independent build, with cutoff, cross-view and missing-call cases; preflight per check; a complete outcome and cause order with \"unresolved\" and \"not judged\" placed before fact causes; an enforced shared budget transport with ten asserted checks and five caught mutants, whose first run caught a real ledger race). Consensus: \"Council position: consensus on shape B' (DESIGN.md version 4): send the one-day slice\" (`ml-codex-5.md`), with the $5 ceiling stated as conditional on the input and output bounds and the comparison exploratory (different days).\n- **Decided:** run `clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/DESIGN.md` version 4: the one-day slice first (2026-07-01, run cap $0.25), reviewed before the three draws; all production runs under one $5.00 shared ledger; D442's same-day comparison (shape A) stays owed for D427's acceptance.\n\n## D444 · 2026-11-13 · Three fresh production draws under a corrected ledger, replacing the stopped ones (council consensus under D439)\n\n- **What happened:** the three draws of D443 stopped on 2026-07-12/13 (of 14) when the $5 shared ledger was exhausted: the agreed Jev settlement (each call at its whole three-attempt byte-bound reservation, about 84,000 input tokens a call against about 3,300 billed) charged the ledger $4.999 while the archive recorded $0.98 for the three draws ($0.994 with the slice). The slice had shown the same ratio; I did not compare before the draws (my process error).\n- **Council** (`clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ml-codex-7.md` to `ml-codex-9.md`): resuming in place needed a recovery proof (`resume_check.py` could not fake a valid judgement for views opened on the stopped day); three fresh draws avoid it. Jev now settles its whole charge at three times its reported input tokens (a hidden retry is the same request), uncapped so that an excess sets the overrun flag; zero, missing or invalid usage settles at the whole reservation; the retry check goes through the real `jev.transport` with a fake network failure (`check_budget.py` 12 of 12; mutants caught). Consensus: \"Council position: consensus on three fresh draws as in ml-challenge-7.md and ml-challenge-8.md\".\n- **Decided:** three fresh 14-day draws from empty (`draw-q1..q3`), the same day; a new shared ledger with ceiling $3.00, conditional on the reservation and retry-billing bounds (an overrun stops later sends but cannot undo a charge); run caps $1.00. The stopped p1–p3 are kept as records, unused; their recorded cost ($0.98) and ledger charge ($4.999) are reported separately. This replaces D443's $5 shared limit for these draws.\n\n## D445 · 2026-11-13 · Next: a $0 source-to-answer trace of the clean path's 17 graded opening losses, memory first (council consensus under D439)\n\n- **Census result** (`clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/RESULT.md`): on the graded questions both systems agree with reader B on one or two a draw (production 3, clean 4; two unresolved each); no eligible judgement disagrees; every loss is at opening. A measuring-stick concern (q23: D430's same deadline against the standard's 18-month range) is recorded under D373, scoring unchanged.\n- **Council** (`clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/mm-*`): Claude's lean (event deadlines by the standard's rules in code) was withdrawn on the council's evidence (the product-node rule is not a general rule of the standard; the regulatory default needs interpretation; q08 also fails on scope; the clean path's deadline losses are 2 of 15). Consensus on a $0 trace, as amended by the council: every one of the clean path's 17 graded opening losses (9 not opened, 6 not eligible, 2 unresolved, kept apart) traced along its path (article and request → raw answer → checks → opened question → matching → final question and judgement) to the first point where the ruler's question is lost, or \"unknown\"; scope, period, deadline and part-of-total recorded independently; \"absent from the window\" only after searching the window for the matter, labelled by what was checked; each product question marked as breaching the standard, reasonable but not comparable, or unresolved; the 108 gate-2 memory sends used as a separate diagnostic; mechanisms ranked by their demonstrated effect, the sample's size and uncertainty disclosed; no repair proposed until the trace identifies a cause; memory (q05) first.\n\n## D446 · 2026-11-13 · A $0 audit of a narrow typed-unit contract on the stored losses and correct controls (council consensus under D439)\n\n- **Trace** (`clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/TRACE.md`): no single mechanism explains most of the clean path's 16 graded opening losses; 7 certainly and 2 possibly breach a rule of the question's unit the frozen standard states; two sentence-level attempts on these classes failed (D441, D391). Accounting corrected on the council's findings: the reconciled breach count, and both unresolved counts in `RESULT.md` (by the design's order, and by the decisive reading, disclosed as a departure).\n- **Council** (`council/mm-codex-3.md`, `mm-codex-4.md`): not a new Step 1 version yet, and not Step 4's lines directly (D426 stopped tuning on the 44 theses; the evidence does not show Step 4 is the highest-impact repair). Consensus: audit, at no cost, whether typed identity fields could expose a wrong question without rejecting a correct one, under the contract in `AUDIT-CONTRACT.md` (amended by the council: a check date and its basis for quantities and events; signed and undertaken may overlap; missing values are unknown, never reconstructed from the ruler), with its stop rule.\n\n## D447 · 2026-11-13 · The typed-unit line stops; next a reviewed $0 design for wiring the ladder step end to end and its diagnostic same-day run (council consensus under D439)\n## D543 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the cold reflection on the lean path: D518 stands with its reasons, the choice of system is not made in Alex's place, and no investigation is opened (reflection council consensus)\n\n- **The council** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-2/`): Claude's own reflection written first\n  (`own-reflection.md`); a fresh independent Codex session reading the whole record cold (`handoff-cold.md`, `cold-codex-1.md`);\n  one challenge round (`cold-challenge-1.md`, `cold-codex-2.md`). Both readings, made apart, reached the same place.\n- **Why the path did not reach its goal (agreed):** the fresh week mixed a difference of product policy with a comparison of\n  quality (three of the old system's four covered matters are questions Alex's rule forbids the new system, D505); the evidence\n  was too thin to rank answers (one run a system, seven answers against fifteen, readers' bars apart); the work followed each\n  newly exposed defect instead of asking whether resolving it would select a system (the dependency stretch is the clearest\n  case); and the council often corrected work already framed on a premise that the stored request, an earlier decision or the\n  order of events contradicted.\n- **Conclusions marked as weaker than they look:** \"no eligible judgement disagrees\" in the July comparison (few were eligible);\n  \"36 of 438\" (repeated daily readings on a few question paths, not an error rate); the three unsupported answers with a\n  missing stored figure (two matters, cause not shown); the new system's three confirmed missing pairs (a minimum; the one test\n  was inconclusive); the dependency connection (a conditional replay; it also removes seven filing links).\n- **Decided, citing D439:** D518 stands, restated with its reasons: the old system stays the default and both systems stay,\n  for continuity and because the new system's answer-choice step has not passed its tests and it is not integrated with\n  production storage. The quality of the two systems' answers is not ranked and **the choice of a foundation is not made in\n  Alex's place** (the reviewer withdrew \"carry the old system forward provisionally\" as readable for such a choice). No\n  investigation is opened: no bounded test on the record is likely to change the choice at a justified cost, and no narrow\n  defect is reopened to postpone it. The two problems that do not depend on the choice stay recorded: related facts reaching\n  the question that needs them (no common cause or tested general fix among the known cases), and the judge's amounts with\n  nothing sizing them (what the product shows is Alex's choice). The pages were corrected where a cold reader could be misled\n  (the advice's reasons; \"the one test that could decide\"; a stale \"waiting on Alex\" and an unsourced spend figure on the\n  status page; \"three small mismatches\"; \"36 of 438\" without its qualification). A new step worth a decision returns to the\n  council under D439.\n\n## D544 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the work toward production carries forward on the new system; the old system stays the running default until the new one passes its gates (council consensus; D543's deferral of the choice is withdrawn)\n\n- **Why D543 was reopened the same day:** its consensus left the path's own question (which system carries forward) to Alex.\n  His words in D439 give that job to the council (\"自行的根据数据和目标做决策 ... 掰扯直到你们能达到共识为止再下去执行 ... 持续迭代完成我们的PRD生产目标\";\n  D509: \"下来你自行按照我的准则操作\"), and where it cannot settle a question it takes the cheaper, reversible course; it does not hand\n  the question back. Claude had argued the independent reviewer out of a position; that was Claude's error, recorded here.\n- **The council** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-2/cold-challenge-2.md`, `cold-codex-3.md`): the evidence set\n  out by Alex's own criteria; Claude's position; the reviewer's decision with two qualifications.\n- **Decided, citing D439:** **the work toward production goes on the new system (the rebuild under `clean-arch/`). The old\n  system stays the running default until the new one passes its gates; both remain; nothing that runs is switched, deleted or\n  deployed by this decision.** It does not establish that the new system's answers are better. Reasons: Alex had reopened work\n  on the rebuild (2026-11-06) and D439's scope names its steps; its treatment of the company's financial figures follows the\n  product rule he last confirmed (D505); the fresh week gives no demonstrated reason to abandon it and shows fewer trivial or\n  duplicate questions in that one run; the old system's known defects sit in frozen pieces whose fixes are each a new version.\n- **Qualifications kept:** D505's rule was given for the new system; whether it applies to the old one stays open and is not\n  claimed. The rebuild's cost and time to production are unknown: its step that writes answer choices has not met its lines on\n  total only as its base, the share is the question.\n  - This settles the scope question four Codex-family auditors raised (D653 rounds 3 to 5).\n- **Next:**\n  - an independent audit of the exact passage (narrow-d436 v5.1) against this ruling and the request;\n  - the paired exposed verification, tightened per council round 5: a material defect marked by either reviewer blocks a pass; u3/clean-11 checks only\n    that the total is not asked; u2/clean-10 is reported, not deciding; the scoring and spending paths are exercised at $0 before the send.\n- **Scope:** the clean path's ladder step (Step 4a); the frozen request is unchanged until a measured version is adopted.\n\n## D655 · 2026-11-17 · Council consensus (D439, D654): send the paired test of the narrow passage v5.5 on astra, shared cap $3.60\n\n- **Basis:**\n  - Alex's \"你开始做\" on D653's next step and his D654 ruling (\"立通用规则\");\n  - about $6 of testing authorized (\"你测试吧\"), of which about $4.34 is left.\n- **The passage** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/edit.json`, version 5.5):\n  - D654's three shapes and its share counter-case in D654's own words, two examples a shape from industries outside the 44;\n  - one pointer to the same exception in the closing check, and one in the `no_ladder` description;\n  - +1,528 characters across the three places, o200k system tokens 2,031 to 2,294, nothing cut (`cut_review`).\n- **Audits:** fresh blind auditors of both families each round, v5.1 to v5.5. Both families read v5.5 SOUND (`audit-v55-codex.md`,\n  `audit-v55-claude.md`). The inputs were frozen read-only per round from v5.4 on, after two Claude audits were voided by my replacing files mid-read.\n- **The test** (`paired_probe.py`), astra on the frozen request against the edited one, interleaved:\n  - targets acc/clean-8, acc/clean-9 and pool-17, 3 passes each;\n  - u3/clean-10 (3 passes) and u2/clean-10 (2) are reported, not deciding. The scope rule: a case D654's words do not plainly name is reported. The\n    two families split three times on u3/clean-10's easing form.\n  - 5 decline controls and the share control u3/clean-11, 2 passes each;\n  - 52 sends.\n- **Verdict, fixed before the send:**\n  - any lost edited answer gives FAIL;\n  - fewer than 5 of 9 frozen target declines, or declines on fewer than 2 targets, gives INCONCLUSIVE;\n  - PASS needs:\n    - every edited target send written, with no hard-line fault from either family;\n    - no material defect (wrong side, invented measure, second side dropped) from either source reviewer;\n    - every edited control a decline neither family faults;\n    - each share send does not ask the total;\n  - due-day defects are reported, not deciding;\n  - a stopped draw or a missed planted control gives NOT MEASURED.\n- **Code:** 17 tests at $0 (the verdict paths, and a dry run of all six draws and the reading with stored astra answers). No mutant was run: the\n  machine's guard refused scratch mirrors earlier.\n- **Council:** rounds 4 to 6c on thread 01a1e8ee-437f-74a3-ad0a-8edcbde0e3b3.\n  - Round 5 disagreed and sent the scope question to Alex (D654).\n  - Round 6 corrected two verdict rules and the budget wording.\n  - Round 6b corrected the verdict order.\n  - Round 6c: \"CONSENSUS: send the paired test under the shared $3.60 cap … a pass is evidence on this exposed test, not a Gate 1 pass or proof of\n    general performance.\"\n- **Cost:** estimated about $2.15 with the system text cached and about $3.2 uncached. These come from stored mean tokens and are not bounds.\n- **Next:**\n  - the reading by both families with the planted controls, and two source reviewers;\n  - third readers on disputes, with the prewritten strict counting kept in the verdict;\n  - every trace read by Claude;\n## D664 · 2026-11-17 · Alex: no budget needed for the comparison; D658 waits; the council determines whether the six lines still make sense and what decides readiness\n\n- **Asked** (after D663):\n  1. Should the six lines (D400) still block release, or be reported while readiness is decided by the product comparison, with \"beats\" set in\n     numbers?\n  2. May D658's new reference wait?\n  3. The comparison's budget.\n- **Alex:** \"3. no need for budget, 2. wait, 1. what are the rules, does it make sense now run your council to determine\".\n- **Decided:**\n  - The product comparison needs no budget set in advance. Each run still carries its own spend guard, and its price is reported. Provider caps\n    are untouched.\n  - D658 waits; design v8 is kept, and nothing is run.\n  - Question 1 goes to a council, which determines it and records its consensus with his words, as an exercise of the authority he gave. The\n    six lines were explained to him in plain words.\n\n## D665 · 2026-11-17 · Council (D664, Alex's \"run your council to determine\"): the six lines stop blocking release; one product comparison against the running pipeline decides readiness, its verdict written before the first send (council consensus)\n\n- **Council** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-10/`):\n  - Claude's own position, written first (`own-position.md`);\n  - the Codex reading (`codex-1.md`, thread 01a1ebfe-4f67-7c72-af87-7830055e0f09, memories off);\n  - the Claude-family reading (`claude-1.md`);\n  - challenge rounds (`codex-2.md`: disagree, four corrections; `codex-3.md`: \"CONSENSUS\", with one sentence added).\n- **Why the six lines stop blocking:**\n  - D400 wrote them for \"this first measurement\" and told Alex they \"不能证明它比老系统好\". D548, a council decision, made them gate 1.\n  - D560 found no measured link between them and the final answer.\n  - Their zero-fault forms are a poor target at the measured luna-era rates (D623): zero faults in 131 sends has even odds only near 0.5% a send.\n  - Reader agreement on disputed points is 21 of 70 and 29 of 53, and draws move from 7 failures to 1 (D650).\n- **Decided:** as written in `clean-arch/PRD.md` §1.5, which now owns the rule.\n  1. **The product comparison is the release decision.**\n     - poc-v118 is replayed against the hashed new system (astra-frozen; v5.5 excluded). Both start from empty over 07-01 to 07-19, three draws\n       each, and are scored on unread weeks from 07-20 up to 09-20, as far as the full exposure census supports. The exclusion list is frozen.\n     - **The main quantity:** big matters served, paired per matter.\n     - **Verdicts:**\n       - **Practical lead:** a − b ≥ max(3, 15% of N) and a ≥ 2b. Every guardrail subgroup must meet its frozen minimum and pass under both split\n         countings.\n       - **Practical loss:** the mirror, or a guardrail breach confirmed under both countings.\n       - **No practical winner, too small or invalid**, as written.\n     - **Guardrails:** unsupported shares (figure and yes/no apart) and the not-worth-tracking share, each within 10 points of the old system's.\n       D428's limits are printed beside them.\n     - **Illustrative only:** a sign test and an interval.\n     - **Frozen and hashed before the first send:** the matter list (blind), the credit rules (one cutoff per matter, the grouping of repeats,\n       what counts as the same question), the controls, the versions and the rule.\n  2. **What changes, explicitly:**\n     - D548's use of D400's six lines as a release gate is superseded. The lines are reported on every ladder run.\n     - The parts code decides stay hard: figure ranges meet end to end; the favourable end is stable; the due day is consistent at D400's 90%.\n     - D431's frozen-reference reading of 07-01 to 07-14 is still produced and printed beside the comparison, not deciding. D665 supersedes D431\n       on that point only.\n     - Step 2's three checks stay as D427 and D431 set them.\n     - Gate 2's unsupported lines become the guardrails, and its control line waits (D658).\n     - Gate 4 folds in.\n     - Gate 5 waits for a practical lead and Alex's word.\n     - The absolute floors stay proposals, reported beside.\n  3. **Back to Alex,** before any release engineering resumes: a practical lead (with the split cards for his spot check), a practical loss at\n     once, no practical winner, too small or invalid, or no verdict by 2026-12-01.\n- **Next:**\n  - the comparison's design at $0: the exposure census, the frozen matter list and credit rules, the cards and controls, the replay plan and\n    its spend guard;\n  - council review;\n  - then a send on Alex's word. No budget is required (D664).\n## D676 · 2026-11-19 · The release comparison: the new system loses for two known mechanisms; keep the old system; the outcome was computable before the run (council consensus)\n\n- **Alex:** \"did you measure wrong or what … you go deep into the entire trace and logic and run council loop to reason on this\n  comparison and the runs of the two system\".\n- **Council:**\n  - an independent cold Codex read (`docs/research/2026-11-19-comparison-review/council-a-codex.md`);\n  - Claude's reflection, written first (`claude-reflection.md`);\n  - one challenge round (`council-b-codex.md`).\n- **Agreed:**\n  - **The measurement is sound for the products as configured.**\n    - The matter list was built blind from the articles.\n    - The mapping was blind with third readers.\n    - The controls pass, 13 of 13.\n    - The old system's served cards hold on the ones read.\n    - No new question was mapped out wrongly.\n    - Codex found the old system's Anthropic serve generous; without it b is 6 or 7, still a loss.\n  - **Mechanism 1, by design (D382, D384, D505):** the company's own figures are not questions. 7 of the 32 matters are such figures,\n    and the old system serves 4 to 6 of them. By D384, five other companies' standalone figures are also outside the new system's\n    rules, so its ceiling is 20 of 32 matters.\n  - **Mechanism 2, a hand-off defect known since D432:**\n    - Step 1 opens bundled questions (two years, or several outcomes), and the frozen ladder refuses them. new-2 lost 27 of 60 and new-3\n      19 of 60.\n    - Among them are four-hyperscaler capex (M04) and the Anthropic investment (M17) in both runs, and Hugging Face (M19) in new-2.\n    - The judge uses pass 1's ladders; new-3's capex question got a valid ladder only on pass 3.\n  - **The ceiling ($0 check):** with every dropped key-matter question repaired and served, new is 5–6 against old 10, a = 0, b = 4–5:\n    no practical winner, never a lead. Only NVIDIA's own figures (M06, M10, M11, M18) remain.\n  - **Process failure:** the attainable verdict was computable in minutes before the run, from the frozen list, D505, D432 and the D489\n    week. Neither the council (D665–D667) nor Claude computed it (AGENTS §7.2); two days and about $145 measured a knowable result.\n- **Decided (council consensus):**\n  - The old system stays for release.\n  - No further comparison run.\n  - The new path continues only on Alex's word, and only after two things: his D382 call for such a comparison, and a cents-level test\n    of the one-outcome hand-off repair on the stored no-ladder cases.\n  - Before any future paid comparison, its attainable verdict is computed first.\n\n## D677 · 2026-11-19 · The release comparison's goal loop is closed; the old system stays; no paid work continues (council consensus, D439/D544)\n\n- **Context:** Alex's goal (D674) is to keep tackling until the comparison passes, without asking him. He has not answered the closing\n  question. D544 says the council decides reversibly, not \"Alex's choice\".\n- **Facts:**\n  - A pass needs at least 9 of 14 rule-allowed matters only the new system serves, under D382; it serves 0–1 today.\n  - The one-outcome probe failed its line 1.\n  - The $0 census found three loss points that no wording fix reaches.\n  - A verdict needs a full comparison, about $110, above D439's $40 ceiling.\n  - Two decisions stand: D493 (no further Step 1 request change) and D505 (figures are not questions).\n- **Council:** `docs/research/2026-11-19-comparison-review/council-c-codex.md`: \"CONSENSUS: (a) Close the comparison loop, keep the old\n  system running, and stop paid work … Reconsider if Alex reverses D505 or funds a full run. Before spending, show a path to passing the\n  frozen criteria.\"\n- **Decided:**\n  - The loop is closed and the old system stays.\n  - No paid work continues.\n  - **Reversal conditions:** Alex reverses D505, or funds a full run. Either way, a written path to passing the frozen criteria comes\n    first.\n\n## D678 · 2026-11-19 · Merge design (council consensus): the old system opens questions, the clean system reads and organises evidence, the same judge answers; a 12-article test awaits Alex's approval\n\n- **Alex:** \"I think you should run council to determine how to merge the best of both worlds, and come up with a small scale test to\n  validate\".\n- **Council:** Claude's proposal (written first), Codex's cold independent design and one challenge round, which ended in consensus.\n- **The design:** `docs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/MERGE-PLAN.md`.\n  - Opening and answer choices come from the old system, and a refused proposal stays out of the open list.\n  - Reading, earlier versions, repeats and unlinked facts come from clean Steps 1–3.\n  - Judging stays the frozen judge.\n  - NVIDIA's own figures follow D382/D505 unless Alex makes an exception. The ceilings are 6 and 10 of 32.\n  - The main risk is meaning drift.\n- **The test:**\n  - 11 frozen articles for M04 and M17, plus 1 control, against old-2's and old-3's question lists;\n  - 4 judge answers, blind-read;\n  - 5 pass lines, written beforehand;\n  - $0.33–0.42.\n  - It waits for Alex's approval, since D677 holds paid sends.\n\n## D679 · 2026-11-19 · Alex lifts D677's paid-send stop for the merge test only; the test runs on 70 articles, two old draws, two judging arms, under a $5 ledger\n\n- **Alex (the session goal):** \"write the plan, execute the plan, and don't do full blown runs, validate it works based on metrics we\n  meassure for quality\".\n- **The plan:** `docs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/EXECUTION-PLAN.md`, revised by council rounds c and d (`council-c-codex.md`,\n  `council-d-codex.md`).\n- **The article set.** It became the 70 window articles that either system tied to M04 and M17, plus the census and the control.\n  - My first count read the census numbers as positions in `kept-articles.json`. They are numbers in `census/numbering.json`. Codex\n    caught it.\n  - The same misreading put custom-chip and DRAM theses into a first list of 126.\n- **Per-article open lists** were agreed in round d: the old questions open when the article was read.\n- **The judging copy.** A 09-06 cutoff copy of each old store, with the frozen judge restricted to the target questions on one daily\n  schedule for both arms (hybrid: c8's filing; control: old's own evidence).\n- **Checks before any send ($0):**\n  - c8's request, rebuilt, equals the stored c8 request byte for byte.\n  - The control arm, answered with old's archived answers on old's schedule, rebuilt 25 of 25 capex requests byte for byte, and all 22\n    Anthropic requests with the same evidence. Their only difference is the theme block: other questions held at 09-06.\n- **Lines** P1, P2, P3 and P5 hold in both draws, plus a validity line V; P4 is diagnostic only.\n- **Ceiling:** $5 on a new ledger. The estimate is about $1.4 for c8 and about $0.2 for judging. This is within D439's $40.\n- **Also recorded:** PATH-TO-PASS's census C named new-2's clean-26 and clean-7, and new-3's clean-29, clean-6 and clean-12, as capex\n  theses. They are custom-chip and memory theses, so census C's capex reading is to be re-checked before it is relied on.\n\n\n## D680 · 2026-11-19 · Merge test closed (council e): delivery verified, the registered test did not pass (P2's proxy clause and V missed)\n\n- **Ran under D679**, for $2.13 of a $5 ledger: 140 c8 sends, 98 judge calls in two arms, 15 noise re-sends, and two blind reader\n  families on 20 cards with 5 controls.\n- **Held:**\n  - P1, delivery, 4/4: the hybrid's final requests carry c8's four-company $720–745B item and the Reuters anchor-talks items;\n  - P3, talks stay talks, 2/2;\n  - P5, both readers support all 4 hybrid answers, settled in round 0;\n  - P4 (diagnostic): 0 NVIDIA-figure proposals of 7;\n  - the reader controls: 3 plants unsupported, 2 controls supported.\n- **Missed:**\n  - **P2's second clause.** Total capex is used for data-center capex without being named a proxy; old misses it the same way.\n  - **V.** The control's v47 was unsupported. Its 09-16 request, re-sent, gave rungs 2, 1, 2 against old's 2, 2, 2. The requests differ in\n    the theme block and the previous wording, so a reconstruction effect is not ruled out.\n- **What the answers rest on.** Capex rests partly on 09-06 history: 26–27 c8 items against 16–20 history items in the final requests.\n  The Anthropic answers rest wholly on c8's filing.\n- **Errors of mine** (caught by Codex):\n  - the census numbering misread;\n  - a first article list with off-matter theses;\n  - a judging copy refused by the store's append-only triggers.\n- **Closed with no further sends.** The next step is Alex's.\n- Record: `docs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/RESULT.md`, `council-e-codex.md`.\n\n## D681 · 2026-11-20 · Alex opens the hybrid system's build and test; phase 1 is a new judge version (J2) for the judge's reasoning errors\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-20, after the merge test (D680) and the finding that the old system loses on the 32 matters at opening and in the judge's\n  reasoning, not in reading: \"不是聊了半天就是要合并两边最好的来提升系统吗？\"; then, on the proposal to fix the judge's reasoning errors\n  first as a new judge version, with a council review and a test of a few cents: \"可以啊，你现在开始做hybrid system 的构建和测试，不要犯了和做新系统一样的错误到最后才发现问题\".\n- Decided (his words): the hybrid system is built and tested. The judge (judging-v7, frozen by D354) may get a new version, J2, measured\n  against v7. The frozen ruler is unchanged.\n- The plan (`docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/PLAN.md`) guards against the new system's mistakes:\n  - a change is accepted by its effect on final answers, read by blind readers;\n  - an end-to-end slice runs in week 1;\n  - the attainable verdict is computed first;\n  - every line has a source;\n  - the design uses the stored dev failures, and acceptance is on a fresh window.\n- Attainable on the 32 matters (computed first):\n  - the judge fix can add M08 and M14 to old's 10;\n  - a release lead needs opening (phase 2);\n  - with D382 applied to the hybrid's figure questions, the hybrid loses by construction. That choice is Alex's.\n- First findings ($0, 2026-11-20):\n  - 24 answers rejected by both readers. In about 16 of them the answer leaves its own reference's rung on a weak step: overlapping\n    amounts added (6), a figure of another scope taken as the value (6), an adjustment with no basis (5).\n  - The judge writes the rung first and the reference and implied outcome last, in all 48 stored answers read. `schema_for` appends\n    the D260 stance fields to the end of the schema's properties, against its own comment: D260's intended order never ran.\n- Correction: the merge-test report cited a planted reader control (C140) as an old-2 answer. Corrected in its `RESULT.md`.\n- Record: `docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/` (PLAN.md, census/, DESIGN-J2.md).\n\n## D682 · 2026-11-20 · Under D681 (council): J2a and J2b retired; the carried previous conclusion is the lead mechanism; a separating test on complete evidence\n\n- Council thread `01a1f7a7…`, rounds A to G (`docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/council-*-codex.md`), consensus at E for the sends and at G for\n  what follows.\n- **Measured** on the 48 stored final requests (24 targets rejected by both readers, 24 supported controls), 3 repeats each:\n  - **J2a (schema order only):** moves the same 2 targets as v7's re-send; the falsifier fired.\n  - **J2b (order plus overlap and scope check):** 3 targets, with unchanged derivations.\n  - **Frozen judgement gold:** v7 45.0, J2a 44.3, J2b 44.7 of 51. Rule (c) is missed by both; J2b regresses d3-q18.\n  - **Production-check refusals:** 0 of 144 in every arm.\n- **The anchor.**\n  - Every final request carries \"[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung N; reasons: …\", the rejected derivation itself, held\n    for a median of 8.5 judgements.\n  - Sent without it: v7 moves 14 of 24 targets (2 with it) and 2 of 24 controls, mostly toward the rung the readers named. J2b without\n    it moves 16.\n  - It is confounded: in 34 of 48 requests, the previous conclusion also carries hidden pieces (D198).\n- **Decided (council):**\n  - retire J2a and J2b;\n  - separate the anchor from the hidden pieces on complete evidence: 21 cases with 1 to 100 hidden pieces, every piece in force shown\n    (each request first rebuilt byte for byte by `build_prompt` on the store as it stood, 48/48), with and without the previous\n    conclusion, and a screen written before the send;\n  - the end-to-end test of a judge change is a judge-only replay of the whole window from 07-01 with old's links fixed. It is validated\n    at $0: 82/82 days with the same judged set, and 1,612 of 1,616 requests byte-identical (the 4 others are retries);\n  - a new $2 ledger for the separating test, inside D439's $40.\n- **For Alex after the separation:** whether to change how the previous conclusion is presented (within D198), or what it carries\n  (a change to D198).\n- Record: `docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md`, results 1–3 and councils F–G.\n\n## D683 · 2026-11-20 · Under D681/D682 (council H): the removal of the previous conclusion is not accepted; one presentation-only test goes to Alex\n\n- **Measured** (`docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md`, results 4 to 7):\n  - **The 432-card blind reading:** J2a holds 2 of 24 targets and J2b 4 of 24, against a locked floor of 12 and a net of 8. Both are retired\n    by the rule. v7's own re-send loses 3 of 24 controls, which is the readers' noise.\n  - **The separating test** (13 targets, 8 controls, every piece in force shown):\n    - with the previous conclusion, 0 targets move;\n    - without it, 8 move toward the readers' rung, and 0 controls;\n    - blind reading: net 4 jointly supported repairs (two 2027 capex, total CPU, FY2028 gross margin) against the 5 required, so the\n      screen does not pass. No control was lost and none was refused. The M08 answers moved from rung 3 to 2, where the readers say 1.\n  - **Frozen gold without the previous conclusion:** 44.3 against v7's 45.0, with d3-q17 (China share) regressed.\n- **Decided (council H, consensus):**\n  - the carried previous conclusion is the mechanism that holds rejected rungs;\n  - removing it is not accepted;\n  - one recommendation goes to Alex: keep the previous conclusion and its reasons intact (what D198 carries), and change only its\n    heading to \"Earlier assessment and carried evidence. Derive today's forecast afresh.\";\n  - **one test**, on fresh, frozen cases (13 targets, 8 controls), 3 paired repeats against v7, every answer blind-read. Screen: net\n    repairs of at least 5, no control lost, no added refusals, and frozen gold at least v7's, with d3-q17 agreeing 3 of 3;\n  - the whole-window replay waits until a candidate clears the screen;\n  - card C100 (M08) is to be rechecked independently; the recorded score stands until then.\n- Spend: phase 1 $2.4 of $3, and the separating test $0.28 of the $2 `phase1b` ledger.\n\n## D684 · 2026-11-20 · Under D683: Alex approves the presentation-only test and hands the iteration to Claude (\"那你自己持续迭代\")\n\n- **Alex, 2026-11-20**, in reply to the request for approval of council H's one test: \"那你自己持续迭代\".\n- **Decided:**\n  - the presentation-only test runs on fresh cases, as council H wrote it;\n  - Claude continues the phase-1 loop on its own: council before every send, a council round on every result, every trace read by\n    Claude, every round logged. The limits of D439 stand: the frozen ruler and the frozen production files never change, nothing is\n    pushed, deployed or committed unasked, credentials and caps are untouched, and paid spend stays inside D439's $40;\n  - D382 (company-figure questions in the hybrid) stays open for Alex; the judge's phase does not depend on it.\n- **The fresh pool, written before any reading** (`fresh_test.py pool`, `DESIGN-J2.md`, \"The fresh test\"): each question's latest\n  judgement on or before 2026-09-06 in the 5 draws, excluding the 73 question-and-draw pairs whose answers the design read. 116 answers,\n  all rebuilt byte for byte under the frozen judge.\n\n## D685 · 2026-11-20 · Under D684 (councils I–K): the fresh test cannot be fed; H1 fails its diagnostic; the judge line pauses within D198; phase 2 (opening) starts\n\n- **The fresh census** (`DESIGN-J2.md`, \"The fresh census and council J\"): 116 answers as of 09-06, read blind by both families.\n  - Once the bets the design had read were excluded (council I), 27 fresh bets gave 1 target against the 8 required. The selection\n    refused itself, and nothing was sent.\n  - On this corpus, the judge's errors sit in about ten big numeric bets, all of which the design has read.\n- **H1** (the heading only, council H's recommendation) was tested in council J's diagnostic: three motivating targets, two controls,\n  3 repeats against same-day v7, 32 cards blind-read with 2 plants.\n  - Result: **0 net repairs against 2 required.** No control was lost, no refusals, and both plants were caught. **H1 is retired.**\n  - By code, it kept the rejected rung in 8 of 9 target answers. The carried derivation, not its label, holds the rung.\n  - Spend: $0.08 from `phase1b`, which now stands at $0.36 of $2.\n- **Reflection council K** (a cold read, consensus with Claude's reflection apart from one point):\n  - Four angles on the judge have failed. Every remaining lever changes D198.\n  - The judge's ceiling on the 32 matters is M08 and M14.\n  - **Decided:**\n    - D198 stays in place, and its options are not taken to Alex yet;\n    - **phase 2 starts**: a $0 census of the 17 matters old never opened (where each fell out, and whether the evidence then available\n      could support an answer), and the attainable gain under each company-figure policy (D382 open);\n    - **falsifier:** if even the generous ceiling, the two judge repairs included, is below 5 more matters, the plan changes to address\n      the constraint that sets that ceiling. Otherwise the strongest opening fix is tested on final answers against D665's line:\n      5 net gains, and at least twice as many wins as losses.\n  - Claude's reflection had proposed taking D198's options to Alex now. Council K: not before the census shows what the hybrid can\n    gain. Claude agrees.\n- **Errors of mine:**\n  - I read 7% at 09-06 against 17% at 09-20 as \"errors accumulate\". The two pools are chosen differently, so this is withdrawn\n    (council J).\n  - The first fresh pool held questions the design had read in other draws (council I).\n\n## D686 · 2026-11-20 · Under D685 (council L): the opening census; D382 goes to Alex first\n\n- **Census** (`docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/OPENING-CENSUS.md`, $0). The 16 matters old never opens fall into three causes:\n  - **A. Merged into a held question, or a variant of one** (the scoring list is finer than D197's key and D248), 6 matters:\n    five-company capex into four-company capex; the U.S. and EU review stages into one clearance question; capacity commitments into\n    credit support.\n  - **B. The library's subject is NVIDIA only**, 5 matters: Broadcom, CoreWeave, Alphabet, Marvell.\n  - **C. Never proposed, though D248 allows a separate question**, 5 matters, plus M05 (2026 semiconductor sales) in 2 of 3 draws:\n    FY2028 data-center revenue, the Ohio guarantee amount, the $125B residual-value support, the Hugging Face obligation, and DRAM\n    prices.\n- **Ceiling.**\n  - Within the standing decisions there are gross gains only: the judge +2 (not reached), and cause C up to +6, which needs a new\n    version of the frozen extraction (D274).\n  - D665's lead needs 5 net gains and at least twice as many wins as losses, which a gross count does not show.\n  - D382 (company figures as evidence only) would cost 5 or 6 matters old serves.\n- **Decided (council L, Claude agreeing):**\n  - D382 goes to Alex first, with the recommendation to keep a company's own figure as a forecast when it is material and separately\n    answerable;\n  - then one opening change is tested on final answers against old: three paired runs, blind readers, the frozen 32 matters, D665's\n    line, after a $0 check that 5 net gains are attainable.\n- **Error of mine:** the first census version claimed the facts were seen in every case, and put M07, M12 and M20 under \"merged\",\n  though no candidate had been proposed. Both were corrected after council L.\n\n## D687 · 2026-11-20 · Alex: in the hybrid, a company's own material figure gets its own question\n\n- Alex, 2026-11-20, asked whether in the hybrid NVIDIA's own financial figures (data-center revenue, gross margin, total revenue)\n  should get questions and forecasts of their own, or serve only as evidence to other questions, with the recommendation (council L\n  and Claude) to give one its own question when it is material and separately answerable: \"单独开题吧\".\n- **Decided:** in the hybrid, a company's own material, separately answerable figure opens its own question, as poc-v118's opening\n  already does. The hybrid keeps old's opening on this point. D382, D384 and D505 stay in force for the clean path and do not bind the\n  hybrid. This is the exception D678 left to Alex.\n- **Effect:** no code changes, since the hybrid opens questions with old's pieces. The company-figure matters old serves (M06, M10,\n  M11, M18) and those it serves in one draw (M09, M30) stay within the hybrid's reach.\n\n## D688 · 2026-11-20 · Under D687 (council M): no credible path to a D665 lead through opening changes alone; to Alex, keep old and pause the hybrid's release line\n\n- **Council M** (`docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/council-m-*`; Codex thread 01a1f7a7, memories off):\n  - round 1: CONSENSUS on deferring any release test, with corrections;\n  - round 2: DISAGREE, reclassifying M27 and recommending option 2;\n  - round 3: CONSENSUS, with one wording change, taken here.\n- **The attainable check, $0** (`OPENING-CENSUS.md`, last two sections).\n  - Old's per-draw served counts: 3 of 3 for M11, M13, M15, M18 and M31; 2 of 3 for M04 (counting 1), M06, M10, M17 and M19; 1 of\n    3 for M05, M09 and M25, and for M30 under counting 2.\n  - Noise alone costs an estimated 1.4 of the 2-of-3 matters.\n  - Within D687 and Alex's standing rules, the concrete opening misses are M20 and M05:\n    - M20: a $125B unsigned obligation that old folds into the total in 3 of 3 draws, against D388/D389;\n    - M05: a theme quantity refused once under a free name.\n  - M09, M30 and M25 are coin flips.\n  - Not misses under the rules:\n    - M12: a signed deal under a plan tracked by amount (D248 boundary 1, D388);\n    - M27: separately answerable in principle, but its public settlement source is unproven;\n    - M07: a distinct segment forecast, but the evidence does not establish a missed opening;\n    - M16: allowed by the standard, but its wording lever failed in D481 and leaves the ceiling until a different mechanism has\n      evidence behind it.\n  - What binds: the list counts finer than D248/D388 on 9 matters, the library's NVIDIA-only subject on 5, and D198 for the judge's 2.\n- **Decided (council, Claude agreeing):**\n  - The census found no credible path to a D665 lead through opening changes alone. Five gains with zero losses would pass on paper,\n    but only M20 and M05 are concrete, and the noise losses are an estimate.\n  - No opening test and no M20 account probe are sent for release.\n  - The release bar stays.\n- **To Alex, one recommendation:**\n  - Keep poc-v118 as production and pause the hybrid's release line. The judge and reading fixes remain quality work.\n  - Questions on key figures of other research-list companies are offered only if he wants those forecasts for their own value.\n  - D543 applies, since another test would leave the decision unchanged.\n- **Errors of mine, both corrected before council M closed:**\n  - the first attainable check counted M12, M27 and M16 as gains within the rules without reading D248 boundary 1, D388 and D481;\n  - the census section listed the categories without the decisions that bound them (AGENTS §2.4).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/PLAN.md docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/OPENING-CENSUS.md docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/CENSUS.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/PLAN.md docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/OPENING-CENSUS.md docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/CENSUS.md'","aggregated_output":"# Hybrid system: build and test plan (2026-11-20, D681)\n\n**Alex, 2026-11-20:** \"可以啊，你现在开始做hybrid system 的构建和测试，不要犯了和做新系统一样的错误到最后才发现问题\". This was his reply to\nthe proposal to fix, first, the old system's judge reasoning errors as a new judge version: a council review first, then a test of a few\ncents (`docs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/RESULT.md`, \"Where the old system loses\").\n\n## The new system's mistakes, and the guard for each\n| Mistake | Where it showed | Guard in this plan |\n|---|---|---|\n| Steps were tuned on their own scores. The hand-off broke and was found at the end: Step 1's questions refused by the ladder, 19–27 of 60 (D432) | the release comparison, D676 | Every change is accepted only by its effect on final answers, read by blind readers on the analyst matter list. An end-to-end slice runs in week 1, not at the end |\n| The attainable verdict was not computed: $145 spent on a knowable result | D676 | The ceilings are below, computed before any send |\n| A policy removed matters by construction (company figures, D382), and it was known only at the end | D676 | Alex decides the figure questions for the hybrid now (below) |\n| Lines with no source, or that the change could not move (the merge test's P2) | D680 | Every line names its source and the change that can move it |\n| Choices made on the test set, and fix-and-rerun loops | council-loop traps | Designed on the stored failures of 09-07..09-20 (dev). Accepted once on a window the design never saw, with its matter list built blind before any hybrid output is read |\n\n## What the hybrid is\n| Piece | From | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Opening questions and their answer choices | Old (poc-v118) | Unchanged |\n| Reading articles | Old extraction (control arm) or clean Step 1 c8 (hybrid arm) | c8's effect on final answers is measured end to end, not assumed. On the 32 matters, old's losses are not reading losses |\n| Judging | Frozen judging-v7, then a new version (J2) | The change; Alex opened it on 2026-11-20 |\n| Clean Steps 2–3 | Not in the first build | Added only if a measured loss calls for them |\n\n## Attainable, on the frozen 32 matters (from the stored readings, $0)\n- **Old serves 10.**\n- **The judge fix can add M08 and M14.** Old opened each in 3 of 3 draws, and the readers rejected every answer for reasoning:\n  - adding amounts that overlap;\n  - a cumulative estimate taken for an outstanding balance;\n  - a five-company or company-forecast figure taken for a four-company answer.\n- **M30 (+1)** only if company figures are kept.\n- **M05, M09 and M25** (served in 1 of 3 draws) and the **17 never opened** move only with opening, not the judge.\n- **The release lead** (D665: a − b ≥ 5 and a ≥ 2b against old) is out of reach for the judge fix alone, with a ≤ 3. The judge fix is a\n  quality gain. A release needs phase 2, opening.\n- **If D382 applies to the hybrid** (no company-figure questions), old keeps 5–6 matters the hybrid cannot serve, and the hybrid loses by\n  construction. Hence Alex's decision now.\n\n## Phase 1: the judge (J2)\n1. **$0 census.** Every answer of the 5 stored draws that the readers rejected or split, among the questions rated worth tracking. The\n   same frozen judge answered old-1..3 and new-2..3. Each is classified by mechanism, from the readers' reasons and the judge's own:\n   - overlap or arithmetic;\n   - range against the answer's own estimate;\n   - scope (other companies, year or measure);\n   - source weight (a company's own forecast over independent ones);\n   - missing material;\n   - the boundary between two ranges;\n   - other.\n\n   The largest class is the target.\n2. **Mechanism.** The whole judge request is read for 10 cases. The model's own account is taken on up to 10, at a cent each.\n3. **Design.** A prompt audit of judging-v7 against the judgement ruler (`ruler-v1.md`) and the standards. The levers are structural\n   first, for example a required estimate field whose chosen range code checks. Every sentence names its source, or goes to Alex.\n4. **Council A** on the design: an independent Codex read, then challenge rounds to consensus.\n5. **$0 checks.**\n   - Stored requests are rebuilt byte for byte.\n   - A synthetic answer passes every code check.\n   - J2 sits beside judging-v7. The production default and the frozen tests stay unchanged, apart from the code that admits a second\n     version.\n6. **A probe of cents.**\n   - The stored final requests of the failing cases are re-sent with J2, 3 times each.\n   - As many supported answers are re-sent as controls, which must not regress.\n   - Blind readers mark them, mixed with the archived answers.\n   - **Pass:** the decision rule in `DESIGN-J2.md` (targets, controls and the frozen gold, each against v7's re-send).\n7. **A production-path check, then the end-to-end test in week 1** (revised after councils A and F).\n   - The production path is verified at $0: a two-day replay of old-2 gives 72/72 requests byte-identical to old's, starting from\n     production's own queue.\n   - The end-to-end test of a judge change is a **judge-only replay of the whole window from 07-01**, with old's links, sources and\n     relations fixed. Each arm, v7 and the candidate, builds its own conclusions, citations, theme effects and queue.\n   - A slice from a 09-06 copy would carry every earlier wrong rung into both arms (council F).\n   - Readers mark every answer at 09-20. The 32 matters are counted as dev.\n   - Cost: about $2 per draw and arm, from archived tokens. The c8 reading arm is a separate later test.\n8. **Acceptance** on a fresh window, run once on Alex's word.\n\n## Phase 2: opening\n- A census of why the 17 matters were never opened, after phase 1's end-to-end slice. It is not designed now.\n- **Status (2026-11-20):**\n  - The census ran (D686).\n  - Alex kept company-figure questions in the hybrid (D687).\n  - Council M found no credible path to D665's lead through opening changes alone (D688), so no opening test is sent.\n  - Waiting on Alex: the recommendation is to keep poc-v118 as production and pause the hybrid's release line.\n\n## Limits\n- The frozen ruler is unchanged.\n- No paid send before the council and the $0 checks.\n- Phase 1 through step 6 costs about $1. Step 7's estimate is written before it runs.\n- Nothing is pushed or deployed.\n# Phase 2, step 1: why the old system never opens 16 of the 32 matters ($0, D685)\n\n**Source:** `opening_census.py`, written to `poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/opening-census.json`. For each matter's articles in each old\ndraw it records:\n- the candidate questions the extraction proposed, and what matching did with each;\n- where the evidence from those articles was linked;\n- the mapping readers' verdict (`reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/map-L*-*.txt`).\n\nClaude read every row.\n\n## The answer\nNone of the 16 is lost because the old system failed to see the facts. In every draw it wrote evidence from those articles, between 2\nand 138 items a matter, and linked most of it to questions it holds. There are three causes, and **two of them are design rules Alex\nset.**\n\n| Cause | Matters | What happens | Rule behind it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. One bet, one question.** The scoring list splits a bet that the system keeps as one question | M02, M03, M24 (five-company capex, total and data-center), M26, M28 (the EU and U.S. review stages of the Hugging Face deal), M27 (the Hugging Face obligation amount), M12 (the Ohio lease-guarantee amount), M20 ($125B residual-value support), M32 (neocloud capacity commitments), M07 (FY2028 data-center revenue) | The extraction proposes the variant, and matching merges it into the held question. old-1 proposed \"top five hyperscalers' capex\" 8 times; each merged into the four-company question. Otherwise the variant's evidence goes to the held question: the Ohio guarantee's to \"will NVIDIA sign the guarantee\", the $125B's and the capacity commitments' to \"credit support, guarantees and capacity backstops\", FY2028 data-center revenue's to FY2028 total revenue. The mapping readers mark these \"broader\" or \"missed\": old-2 Q43 joins the U.S. and EU clearances by 2027-06-30, and the list asks for each review stage at 2027-01-31 | D248, \"one bet, one question\" (Alex). The comparison's frozen matter list (D671) is finer than D248 for these 10 |\n| **B. The library's subject is NVIDIA only** | M01, M22 (Broadcom's AI revenue, FY2027 and FY2026), M23 (CoreWeave's revenue), M29 (Marvell's revenue), M21 (Alphabet's capex) | Every request says \"[Subjects] The objects this item concerns: NVDA\". The prompt puts a company's own figure under that company's ticker. The old draws proposed 0 candidates with any company object other than NVDA. The evidence went to NVIDIA's revenue questions | The corpus tags NVDA as the only subject. Broadcom, CoreWeave and Alphabet are on the research list (`poc/universe.json`); Marvell is not. D187: a company's own quantity is a thesis under that company |\n| **C. A theme proposed under the wrong name, or not proposed** | M16 (DRAM prices in 2027); M05 (2026 semiconductor sales, served in 1 of 3 draws) | M05: old-2 proposed it under the object \"Semiconductor sales worldwide\", and the research-list check refused it. old-3 used `semiconductor-cycle` and opened it. old-1 did not propose it. M16 was proposed in no draw | Within the standing decisions |\n\n## The attainable ceiling (council K's falsifier)\n- **Within the standing decisions:**\n  - the judge, +2 (M08 and M14), not reached by any tested change;\n  - cause C, up to +2 (M05 steadied, M16);\n  - the matters served in only 1 of 3 draws (M09, M25, M30), whose share is uncertain.\n\n  **That is about 4 to 6 at best, every piece uncertain, against D665's lead of 5.** The hybrid cannot be shown better than old\n  without one of Alex's rules changing.\n- **The generous ceiling:** if cause A were opened as separate questions (+10) or cause B's companies were tagged (+5), it is well\n  above 5. So the falsifier does not fire as worded. **The constraints that set the ceiling are D248 and the library's NVIDIA-only\n  subject, both Alex's**, together with D382 (company figures: 7 of the 32 are company figures, and old serves 5 or 6 of them).\n- **The concern is recorded, as D373 requires, and the frozen list is not changed.** For cause A, the comparison's matter list is\n  finer than D248. Splitting five-company from four-company capex, or the EU stage of a review from the U.S. stage, is the\n  \"wordings, deadlines, milestones and periods of one bet\" that D248 keeps as one question.\n\n## Council L (`council-l-codex.md`, DISAGREE) and the corrected classes\n**Verified against the trace.** In every draw, M07, M12 and M20 had no candidate proposed. They were not merged; they were never\nproposed. D248 allows them as separate questions:\n- FY2028 data-center revenue can move apart from total revenue;\n- the guarantee's year-end amount can move apart from whether it is signed;\n- the $125B support is one agreement, which an aggregate balance cannot settle.\n\nM24 and M27 were not proposed either.\n\n**Withdrawn:** \"none of the 16 is lost because the old system failed to see the facts\". The trace counts evidence and links by article.\nIt cannot show that the decisive facts were kept.\n\n| Cause (corrected) | Matters | Within the standing decisions? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Merged into a held question, or a variant of one** (code-owned shared-quantity key, D197; one bet, D248) | M02, M03, M24 (five-company capex variants); M26, M28 (old-2 opened one joint U.S.-and-EU clearance question); M32 (old-2 merged it into \"credit support, guarantees and capacity backstops\") | No: the list is finer than the key |\n| **B. Library subject is NVIDIA only** | M01, M22 (Broadcom), M23 (CoreWeave), M21 (Alphabet), M29 (Marvell, not on the research list) | No: a scope choice |\n| **C. Never proposed, though D248 allows a separate question** | M07, M12, M20, M27, M16; also M05, refused once under a wrong name and not proposed once | Yes. A fix is a new version of the frozen extraction (D274), which only Alex or the council opens |\n\n**The ceiling, counted the way D665 counts it.**\n- The named opportunities within the standing decisions are gross gains:\n  - the judge, +2, not reached;\n  - cause C, up to +6, which needs a new extraction version;\n  - the 1-of-3 matters, uncertain.\n- D665 needs 5 net gains and at least twice as many wins as losses. A gross count does not establish that.\n- Before any send, the test must show that 5 net gains are attainable.\n\n**First question for Alex (council L): D382.** Should a company's own figure (NVIDIA's revenue, margin and segments) get its own\nforecast in the hybrid, or serve only as evidence?\n- 7 of the 32 matters are company figures, and old serves 5 or 6 of them.\n- Under D382, the hybrid loses those by construction, so it cannot beat old whatever opening does.\n- Recommendation (council L and Claude): keep a company figure as its own forecast when it is material and separately answerable.\n\n## After D687: the attainable check before any opening test ($0)\n**Alex (D687, \"单独开题吧\"):** in the hybrid a company's own material figure keeps its own question, as old's opening does.\n\n**How old serves each matter, per draw.** \"Served\" follows the PRD's rule: a covering question both reader families mark worth\ntracking, with its latest answer marked supported, in at least 2 of 3 draws. Counts below are from `deck/readings.json` and\n`mapping-out/mapping.json`, under counting 1 and counting 2:\n- 3 of 3: M11, M13, M15, M18, M31; M04 under counting 2.\n- 2 of 3: M04 under counting 1, M06, M10, M17, M19.\n- 1 of 3: M05, M09, M25; M30 under counting 2.\n\n**Noise alone, before any change.** Suppose a hybrid with old's opening repeats old's per-draw rates:\n- A matter served in 2 of 3 draws stays served with probability 20/27, so each of the five is lost with probability about 0.26. That\n  is about 1.3 losses.\n- A matter served in 1 of 3 draws becomes served with probability 7/27, about 0.26 each. That is about 0.8 to 1 gains.\n\n**The 22 matters old does not serve, by what it would take.** Each mechanism was read in the stored requests and answers of old-1..3:\n\n| Mechanism | Matters | Within D687 and the standing rules? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **The judge answers a held question wrongly** | M08, M14 | Only by changing D198 (Alex's); the line is paused (D685) |\n| **An obligation's held question in one form absorbs a statement that, by the frozen prompt's own `binding_obligation` rule, calls for the other form** | M12: the Ohio guarantee was agreed (\"agreed last month to provide a guarantee of up to $105 billion\"), so its amount at the next annual filing is a question; old keeps linking to \"will NVIDIA sign the guarantee\" and to the aggregate credit support. M27: the $12.9B Hugging Face acquisition was agreed, and its amount is a question; old holds only completion. M20: \"may also put up as much as $125 billion in residual-value support\" is not yet signed, so it is a yes/no question; old links it to the aggregate | Yes. It is a misread of the frozen prompt's rule, not yet confirmed by the model's own account. A fix is a new extraction version (D274) |\n| **A theme quantity proposed under a free name, or not proposed** | M05: refused once as \"Semiconductor sales worldwide\" while the request lists `semiconductor-cycle`; not proposed once | Yes, coin flip |\n| **A sub-line or a framing variant, opened in 1 draw of 3** | M09 (networking revenue), M30 (Vera CPU revenue): the prompt says \"Not every reported or guided line\". M25: opened in all 3 draws, under different framings and deadlines | Partly. Steadying M09 and M30 means widening which lines open, against D248's granularity |\n| **Outside the prompt's four reasons, or no figure stated** | M16: a DRAM contract price change is not an aggregate quantity of named companies. M07: the 16 articles state no FY2028 data-center figure; old holds FY2028 total revenue and FY2027 data-center revenue, with data center 92% of sales | No: needs a wider opening rule or D248 |\n| **The list is finer than D248** | M02, M03, M24: five against four hyperscalers; the prompt says \"A different population … is the same question\". M26, M28: the review stages. M32 | No (D373 concern recorded) |\n| **Library subject is NVIDIA only** | M01, M21, M22, M23, M29 | No: library scope, Alex's |\n\n**The arithmetic, against D665 (a − b ≥ 5, a ≥ 2b):**\n- **Best case within the rules:** 3 obligation-form gains, plus M05, plus M09, M30 and M25 made steady, gives 7 gross with no loss.\n  That passes on paper, but only if every one converts in 2 of 3 draws.\n- **Expected:**\n  - The 3 obligation forms, at an assumed 0.6 per draw after a fix, give 0.65 each in 2 of 3 draws: about 2.\n  - M05 adds about 0.65.\n  - M09, M30 and M25 stay near 0.26 each: about 0.8.\n  - So a is about 3.4, against b of about 1.4 from noise, plus whatever a new extraction version breaks.\n  - The expected net is about +2, and a \"no practical winner\" verdict is the likely outcome.\n- **Conclusion (Claude, before council M):** the falsifier \"5 net gains attainable\" holds only in the best case. The expected result of\n  an opening test within the standing rules cannot decide release. What binds is the matter list's granularity against D248 (6 to 8\n  matters) and the library's NVIDIA-only subject (5 matters), both Alex's.\n\n## Council M (D688): the corrected attainable check\nClaude's first attainable check, above, counted M12, M27 and M16 as gains within the rules. After reading the decisions, and after\ncouncil M's three rounds (`council-m-*`), the corrected classes are:\n\n| Matter | Corrected class | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| M12 | Not a miss: a signed deal under a plan tracked by amount is evidence for the total (M31, served 3 of 3) | D248 boundary 1, D388 |\n| M27 | Separately answerable in principle (a deal closing in May 2027 answers M19 and still leaves an obligation at January year-end), but its public settlement source is unproven; out of the gains | Council M round 2 |\n| M07 | A distinct segment forecast, but the 16 articles state no FY2028 data-center figure; a missed opening is not established | Council M round 2 |\n| M16 | Allowed by the signed standard (memory contract prices), but the wording lever failed (0 memory candidates in 45 sends); out of the ceiling until a different mechanism has evidence | D480, D481 |\n| M20 | A real miss: a $125B unsigned obligation keeps its own signing question; old folds it into the total, and old-1's Q29 (platform agreements by August 15) does not settle it | D388, D389 |\n| M05 | A real miss, one draw in three: proposed under a free name and refused | research-list check |\n\n**Decided (D688):** the census found no credible path to a D665 lead through opening changes alone. No opening test and no M20 account\nprobe are sent for release. To Alex: keep poc-v118 as production and pause the hybrid's release line.\n# Phase 1, step 1: census of the judge's rejected answers ($0, read by Claude, 2026-11-20)\n\n**Scope.** 49 answers in the 5 stored draws of the release comparison that the readers rejected or split, among questions rated worth\ntracking (`rejected-answers.md`). The frozen judging-v7 gave all of them. Planted reader controls are excluded: they were filtered by the\ndeck key's role, `product` only.\n\n## Two populations\n| Group | Count | What it is |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Reader-threshold splits** (Claude supported, Codex insufficient) | 23 | Mostly deadline yes/no questions answered with the status quo (\"still open\", \"not passed\"). Codex asks for a base rate that the material does not hold; Claude accepts the status quo. This is the families' known threshold gap, not a judge fault agreed by both. **Not the target** |\n| **Genuine splits** (supported against unsupported) | 2 | #13 France, #16 the Groq-licensed China chip |\n| **Rejected by both readers** (unsupported or insufficient on both sides) | 24 | **The target** |\n\n## The 24, by mechanism (one primary class each)\n| Class | Count | Cases (number in `rejected-answers.md`) | Example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **O: adds amounts that overlap, or counts a factor twice** | 6 | 32, 40 (Grace's trailing revenue added to a \"total CPU\" outlook); 36, 45 ($18B added to a $25B balance that likely holds it); 44 (an above-consensus uplift stacked on an above-consensus run-rate); 47 (memory costs already inside guidance) | old-3 Q3: $446B revenue by stacking uplifts, where consensus and run-rate sit in rung 2 |\n| **S: a figure that measures something else** | 6 | 31, 38, 49 (five companies' total capex and NVIDIA's own forecast for four companies' data-center capex); 28 (a cumulative investment estimate for an outstanding balance); 5 (a lease NVIDIA holds as lessee counted as a guarantee); 8 (a partner outside the question's four) | old-2 Q6: NVIDIA's $1.3T top-five forecast scaled by a 2026 share, against $1.08T consensus for five |\n| **E: an estimate or adjustment with no basis** | 5 | 39 (+46% sequential Q4); 48 (an arbitrary trim to just below $40B); 4 (28% with no denominator); 17 (a sum $0.1B under a boundary, from approximate parts); 1 (one state, with none qualifying) | old-3 Q41: guidance points above $40B; trimmed to $38–39B on unquantified risk |\n| **W: reports of talks taken as a likely completion** | 2 | 30, 42 | old-3 Q23: early-stage financing talks turn the status-quo \"No\" into \"Yes\" |\n| **R: a rung's definition misapplied** | 2 | 12, 20 (rung 2 needs a formal decision after the opening date; the reported delay predates it) | new-2 Q5 Kyber |\n| **M: too little material, default chosen** | 3 | 6, 15 (both readers: too thin); 11 (status quo against rich material) | — |\n\n## What cuts across the classes\n- **The answer leaves the reference's rung on a weak step of its own, in about 16 of 24.** The reference is the consensus, guidance or\n  status quo that the judge itself names first. Cases: 5, 8, 12, 17, 20, 28, 30, 31, 32, 38, 40, 42, 44, 47, 48 and 49. The readers'\n  rejections say, in effect, that the reference rung was right and the step off it is not justified.\n- **The step lands just across a boundary in 7:** 5, 17, 32, 40, 44, 48 and 49. Examples are $24.9B against $25B, \"just above $25B\",\n  \"just above $1.1T\" and $38–39B against $40B.\n- **The step off is asymmetric.** The same answers refuse to move on unquantified downside (\"does not quantify a reduction sufficient to\n  cross\"), yet move on unquantified upside (31, 38, 44, 49), and the reverse in 47 and 48.\n- **On the 32 matters:** M08 (36, 45, 28) is O or S, and M14 (31, 38, 49) is S. Both are in the target's two largest classes.\n\n## Not yet established (next step: mechanism)\n- Which sentence of judging-v7 lets the answer leave the reference on its own arithmetic, and whether the request defines overlap,\n  scope or the boundary.\n- Whether the judge's own account names the same sentence.\n- Whether a re-send gives the same answer. The judge is a sampler: the 24 are one draw each, though M08 and M14 recur across draws.\n\n## Errors found while doing this\n- My report to Alex on 2026-11-19 cited an old-2 answer that \"chose $75–100B against its own estimate of about $53B\". That card, C140,\n  is a planted reader control. The real old-2 answer, C153, chose $50–75B and was rejected for overlap. Corrected in the merge test's\n  `RESULT.md` and in `PLAN.md`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/council-*-codex.md docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/reflection-k-*.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/council-*-codex.md docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/reflection-k-*.md'","aggregated_output":"**Hold J2 before sending.** Putting the reasoning first is worth testing. J2b could make the judge ignore valid estimates, and the probe could miss that regression.\n\n- **Census.** I recounted 23 supported/insufficient splits, two supported/unsupported splits and 24 rejections by both readers. Five checks against the stored requests: **#4** repeats 28% without a denominator, an estimation error. **#6** has one tariff rumor and no usable timing evidence, so it tests how the judge handles missing material. **#12** uses a July 5 report to predict a later formal decision without explaining why that decision is likely. **#17** reasonably favors disclosed consideration over a media aggregate, but approximate inputs cannot securely place $24.9B below $25B. **#44** applies an uplift measured against consensus to a different revenue base. Also, setting aside **#41** hides a date error both readers noticed: six weeks versus roughly fourteen. [CENSUS.md:7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/CENSUS.md:7) · [rejected-answers.md:397](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/rejected-answers.md:397)\n\n- **Order.** Raw answers C003, C127 and C030 all put the forecast first and the derivation last, as did all 48. The schema appends the reasoning fields to `properties`. D260 requires both fields but specifies no output order. The reader brief specifies reasoning order. Early commitment could explain the arithmetic, but J2a changes both schema order and wording. Its result cannot isolate order. The archive hash also ignores key order. [judging.py:54](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:54) · [DECISIONS-log.md:3053](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3053) · [model.py:245](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/model.py:245)\n\n- **J2b.** Checking before choosing follows the brief. The overlap rule misses partial overlap, and its examples closely mirror the failures. The scope rule’s first sentence conflicts with using quarters, comparables and accounting inputs to derive the requested figure. The second sentence preserves proxies. Requiring an item to quantify the adjustment can block guided growth, discounts on untested targets and forecasts based on specific nonnumeric evidence. That risks restoring v5’s reluctance to move. [J2b.txt:12](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/arms/J2b.txt:12) · [DECISIONS-log.md:3072](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3072)\n\n- **Probe.** Twelve repairs are possible on paper. In the partial v7 results, three of 27 target answers changed range, all on one question. Support still needs blind reading of every repeat, including unchanged ranges. The plan allows zero lost controls, the design two. I rebuilt all 51 July requests by archive hash, including one from July 13. Use the frozen score alongside per-question reasons. July omits the unjudged capex question, and the probe skips production validation. [DESIGN-J2.md:53](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md:53) · [judge_probe.py:185](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:185)\n\n- **Plan.** Before the full slice, run one production-path comparison with v7 and J2 on identical inputs across two updates. Check whether changed citations alter what the next judgement sees and whether a changed conclusion reaches its dependants. The planned slice uses J2 in both arms. [PLAN.md:63](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/PLAN.md:63) · [judging.py:266](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:266)\n\nDISAGREE: Isolate answer order, preserve valid estimates, and require complete blind reading plus an early production-path comparison before advancing J2.**Hold the J2 sends.** The deck builder still samples eight unchanged-range answers per arm. That can hide better or worse reasoning within the same range. Require all 432 repeats to be accounted for, and count production refusals before scoring. [judge_probe.py:330](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:330)\n\n- **J2a.** I verified byte-for-byte prompt equality and that only the schema property order changes. J2a and v7 share an archive key, so a production check using a populated archive can silently replay v7. Start with empty, separate archives and verify the raw answer order. [model.py:245](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/model.py:245)\n- **J2b.** Row 2 allows comparable sizing, accounting identities and guided growth. Qualify “say how you adjusted it” so an unadjusted proxy can be explained without inventing a conversion. Row 1’s loan-book example assumes undrawn commitments are already in the balance. Make that conditional on what the lender reports. [J2b.txt:12](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/arms/J2b.txt:12)\n- **Pass rule.** Keep 12/24 as a practical floor. In the 45 v7 target repeats I checked, four changed range. The only case with repeat changes still had a forecast below 0.5 GW paired with the 0.5–1.5 GW answer. Range changes cannot establish the v7 repair rate. Require a predeclared paired improvement by distinct question after blind reading. Count controls that drop from joint support to a split as losses too. [DESIGN-J2.md:68](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md:68) · [v7 repeat:21](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/probe/v7/T-new-2-Q31-3.json:21)\n- **Production check.** The three-arm slice fixes the comparison. Choose a two-day case where an older citation controls what remains visible and a changed conclusion actually queues a dependant. [PLAN.md:63](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/PLAN.md:63) · [judging.py:41](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:41)\n\nDISAGREE: Make the deck complete, protect the comparison from cached answers, and lock the improvement rule before sending.**Hold J2. The refusal check misclassified four of 117 stored v7 re-sends.** It reads the archived final judgement as the previous one. All three Q31 re-sends stayed at the range shown in their request, but the harness flagged them as moves. That inflates v7’s refusal count and makes rule (d) easier for J2 to pass. Validate against the state immediately before each request. [judge_probe.py:329](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:329) · [judging.py:226](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:226)\n\nThe deck no longer samples, but it reads whatever files exist without checking for each expected arm, case and repeat. The gold probe can also credit an answer production would refuse for a bad citation. Require complete coverage and count refusals before scoring both sets. [judge_probe.py:287](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:287) · [judge_probe.py:349](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:349)\n\nJ2b’s revised wording and the archive plan resolve my earlier objections.\n\nDISAGREE: Fix refusal checks and verify complete coverage before sending J2.**Two gaps remain.** I reproduced 0/144 v7 probe refusals, 3/153 gold refusals, and strict gold agreement of 45, 46 and 42 out of 51. The coverage check rejects missing files. [judge_probe.py:336](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:336)\n\n- **The deck skips a production refusal.** I set one stored answer’s range number to `0`. Production refused it, but the deck puts it in a separate list before recording refusals. The reader output omits that list. Count it as both refused and unsupported. [judge_probe.py:412](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:412)\n- **The replay masks the queue behavior.** All 72 request texts matched. On September 18, though, production started with 18 questions and judged 36. The harness preloads all 36, so it would still judge `v11` if the theme failed to queue it. Start with the 18 questions production used and verify that `v11` is added. [judge_probe.py:535](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:535) · [round.py:64](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/round.py:64)\n\nDISAGREE: Count invalid answers before sending J2, and verify theme-triggered scheduling before the paid production-path check.CONSENSUS: send J2a and J2b (probe and gold, 3 repeats), score coverage and refusals, read the complete deck, then the production-path check under v7 and the passing arm.**The carried answer is a plausible influence. The fork cannot isolate it.**\n\n- **Anchor.** I checked old-3 Q3, old-2 Q29 and old-2 Q6. Each final request repeats the disputed calculation. Across all 24, 21 carry the rejected range, two carry a different range and one has no prior answer. The stored runs held the final range for 1–46 judgements, median 8.5, including the fork. [forkcases.json:270](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/forkcases.json:270)\n- **Reading.** Defer the 432 cards only if Result 1 stays undecided. Rule (a) counts supported reasoning, so nine same-range repairs could get J2b to 12. Rule (b) also needs reading because an unchanged range can lose support. Four reviewed cases establish neither result. [DESIGN-J2.md:125](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md:125)\n- **Fork.** Eighteen forks show different evidence from the final request. Q29’s earlier answer already adds $25B and $18B. The first use of a range need not be the first error. Twelve recurrences and eight fewer are screening cutoffs. Eleven cannot establish bad luck, and eight moves can land on equally unsupported answers. To test the anchor, pair identical final requests with and without the previous conclusion, flag hidden evidence, and blind-read against the actual request. The current deck builder uses final-request cards. [judge_probe.py:322](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:322) · [judge_probe.py:623](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:623)\n- **Slice.** Yes. Compare v7 and the candidate from July 1 with old links fixed. Let each judge build its own conclusions, citations, theme effects and queue. That tests whether mistakes accumulate before September 6. [PLAN.md:63](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/PLAN.md:63)\n\nDISAGREE: Test the previous conclusion on identical evidence before attributing the result to anchoring, and read the final deck before declaring either arm passed or failed.**Stop J2a and J2b as candidates. Result 3 shows that removing the previous answer changes forecasts. It leaves open whether those changes improve them.**\n\n- **Result 3.** The code keeps every visible item and removes the prior answer and the notice about omitted evidence. Ten of the 12 extra target moves came from requests with omitted evidence. Among the seven targets with a previous answer and nothing hidden, moves rose from one to three. Q29 moved in all three repeats, yet two still added the disputed $25B and $18B. [judge_probe.py:129](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:129)\n- **Next test.** Use (a). The cap of 100 hidden items gives 13 targets and eight controls. Restore only evidence in force at the original cutoff. Send three paired repeats with and without the prior answer. Compare full and clipped evidence in the no-prior arms to test what restoring evidence changes. Predeclare a screen of five net target repairs, jointly supported in two of three repeats, with no lost controls or extra refusals. Rung-only also discards whatever information the prior reasons carried.\n- **D198.** Alex can choose to keep the same prior content and change its presentation, approve a separate summary of omitted evidence with the prior forecast removed, or approve showing the full evidence. The first preserves D198. The latter two change how omitted evidence is carried and need a new decision from Alex or the council under D439 while he is away. [DECISIONS-log.md:2559](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2559)\n- **Reading.** Yes, for diagnosis. Include all repeats of moved cases and controls, plus a preselected matched unmoved sample. Shuffle them with stored answers and v7 re-sends, showing what each answer actually received. Finish the 432-card reading for the record. Neither arm can recover rule (c) through that reading. On d3-q18, J2b picked Yes three times and gave weight to the NVL576 timeline when asked about NVL144. [DESIGN-J2.md:191](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md:191)\n\nDISAGREE: Retire both J2 arms and test the previous answer on identical, complete evidence before changing D198.**Both J2 failures stand. One reader call could change the separating test by a point, and the frozen gold regression stands independently.**\n\n- **Verdicts.** I reproduced 2/24 for J2a, 4/24 for J2b, and four net repairs against five with no controls lost in the separating test. The close call is C100. The archived filing gives a $25B balance, and the reporting gives $18B committed for the rest of the year. Neither establishes that all $18B comes out of that balance before filing. Both readers leaned on that assumption when rejecting the $20–25B forecast. Recheck C100 independently against those sources and keep the recorded score until adjudication. The China-share miss is real against the frozen 1–8% range, although the request contains conflicting H200 conclusions and the answers still weigh both. [C100](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/deck-full/C100.txt:83) · [DESIGN-J2.md:280](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md:280)\n- **One recommendation for Alex.** Keep the previous forecast and its reasons intact. The block already follows the evidence. Change only its heading to “Earlier assessment and carried evidence. Derive today’s forecast afresh.” This preserves what D198 carries. [judging.py:156](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:156)\n- **One test.** Freeze 13 fresh targets and eight matched controls. Send three paired repeats against same-day v7 and blind-read all answers. Require five net repairs jointly supported in two of three repeats, zero supported controls lost, and no extra refusals. Require frozen-gold agreement at least as high as v7’s, with d3-q17 agreeing three times and its reasons checked against both H200 conclusions.\n- **Skip the full-window replay** until a candidate clears that screen. Replaying retired arms or tuning again on exposed cases would leave the decision unchanged.\n\nCONSENSUS: Ask Alex to approve one presentation-only test on fresh cases, with the previous reasoning intact and d3-q17 protected.**Hold the send. The pool contains questions the design already saw, and the scorer can credit an answer production would refuse.**\n\n- **Pool.** I found five exact question repeats and nine cases mapped to previously exposed matters, with some overlap. For example, pooled old-1 Q8 is the same FY2027 Data Center revenue question as exposed old-2 Q9. Exclude exposed matters across draws and count each remaining matter once. The 114/116 unchanged forecasts suit a test of how prior answers carry forward. They limit what the result says about forecasts in general. Paired v7 re-sends help control for selection on rejected answers. [fresh_test.py:83](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/fresh_test.py:83) · [freshpool.json:99](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/freshpool.json:99)\n- **Selection.** Both ceilings are reasonable as preset screening ratios on distinct matters. At eight targets, the repair bar rounds to four. The code can proceed below eight or with too few controls. Enforce both prerequisites before sending. [fresh_test.py:240](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/fresh_test.py:240)\n- **Wording.** H1 is faithful. It retains the previous forecast and reasons, and changes only their heading. H2’s aim is sourced in PRD 06, although “this evidence” could be read to omit dependencies and themes. Q31’s account explains why it thought it should have stayed after it moved. That single account gives too little reason to send H2. [06-views.md:19](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/06-views.md:19) · [Q31 account](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/account/T-new-2-Q31.json:19)\n- **Screen.** Using identical cards preserves blinding. Count every production refusal as unsupported and require the reader controls to pass. Keep forecast moves descriptive. Reasoning can improve within the same range, and v7 can move on a re-send. A whole-window replay before H1 clears the screen would serve no decision. [fresh_test.py:382](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/fresh_test.py:382)\n\nDISAGREE: Freeze a genuinely fresh set and fix scoring before sending H1 against v7.**Send a five-case H1 diagnostic. The fresh test correctly stopped at one target.**\n\n- **Census.** Of the five jointly rejected, worth-tracking answers on September 6, four were exposed numeric answers and one was the fresh H200 question. OpenAI financing was marked duplicate. The pool still has five fresh, worth-tracking numeric bets, including 2026 hyperscaler capex and H100 rental prices. None was jointly rejected. The September 6 pool excludes cases based on what the design saw on September 20, so 7% versus 17% cannot establish that errors accumulate. [Census marks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/marks-fresh-census.json:1372) · [Selection code](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/fresh_test.py:240)\n- **Next send.** Use old-3 Q3, old-3 Q37 and old-1 Q7. They cover three distinct bets where v7 kept the rejected answer 3/3 and removing the prior answer moved it 3/3. Add two previously supported controls with carried evidence. Send three repeats each under H1 and same-day v7, then blind-read all 30 answers. [DESIGN-J2.md:219](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md:219)\n- **Falsifier.** Require two net target repairs jointly supported in at least two repeats, no lost controls and no extra refusals. If v7 leaves fewer than two repairable targets, call the diagnostic inconclusive. Eight extra range changes is the wrong bar. A changed range can still be unsupported, and a sound derivation can keep the same range.\n- **Skip the 48-case movement-only send.** A whole-window replay also waits until H1 shows a supported repair and clears the planned regression guard.\n\nCONSENSUS: Run one small, blinded H1 diagnostic before spending on a wider replay.**Fix the census before treating its ceiling as a product limit.** Some missing questions fit Alex’s existing rules.\n\n- **Causes.** The five-company capex candidates really merged under D197. M07, M12 and M20 had no matching proposal in the trace. FY2028 Data Center revenue can diverge from total revenue, and signing a guarantee can diverge from its year-end balance. D248 allows separate questions when an analyst would bet on each outcome. M20 asks whether a specific agreement gets signed. An aggregate support balance cannot settle it. The trace counts links by article, so it cannot establish that the decisive facts were kept. [Opening trace](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/opening-census.json:830) · [D248](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2959)\n- **Company scope.** NVIDIA-only tagging explains four misses involving companies already on the research list. Marvell also needs admission to that list. D187 assigns a company’s figures to that company. It does not require NVIDIA-only coverage. [D187](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2480)\n- **Ceiling.** The named opportunities total up to seven gross gains, or five with the judge paused. The generous scenario can clear five arithmetically. Neither establishes a lead: D665 requires five net gains and at least twice as many wins as losses. [OPENING-CENSUS.md:22](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/OPENING-CENSUS.md:22) · [D665](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9414)\n- **Ask Alex about D382 first.** Should company figures serve only as evidence, or can a material, separately answerable figure get its own forecast when an analyst can explain why it matters? Recommend the latter to preserve useful forecasts the old system already serves. Then compare one opening change against old in three paired runs, with blind readers scoring supported final answers on the frozen 32 matters under D665. Check that five net gains are attainable before sending.\n\nDISAGREE: Correct the cause counts, settle which company forecasts the hybrid should serve, and test one opening change on final answers.**Skip a release comparison. Diagnose the opening misses that the existing rules already cover.**\n\n- **Obligations.** M20 is the strongest case. All three draws kept the possible $125B support and proposed no signing question. A material unsigned commitment can have its own signing question beside an aggregate balance. Old-1’s broader signing question needs checking because it may already cover the same decision. For M12, the source reports an agreed guarantee of up to $105B. D248 permits the programme total to absorb a signed deal within that programme. A separate Ohio amount needs an independently meaningful balance that the annual filing can settle. M27’s source reports a definitive agreement. The signed standard explicitly includes sized, incomplete acquisitions, although v74i says “equity stake.” The $12.9B deal price alone does not establish a separately disclosed year-end obligation. [D248](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2959) · [Opening standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:278)\n- **Other openings.** M16’s category is already allowed. The standard explicitly lists memory contract prices. M07 also needs no guidance figure to qualify as a main driver. Whether the available evidence supports the specific forecasts is a separate question. M05’s refusal identifies a concrete naming problem. [Opening standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:276) · [Frozen prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/prompts/role-v74i.txt:13)\n- **Arithmetic.** I reproduced the per-draw counts. Under Claude’s assumptions, the two countings give expected net gains of about **1.8 and 2.3** before regressions. Those are scenarios based on three draws and an assumed repair rate. With zero losses, any five of seven gains clear the count threshold. Seven gains can absorb two losses. The evidence supports deferring the release test. [Census arithmetic](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/OPENING-CENSUS.md:96) · [D665](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9414)\n- **Next step.** Narrow (a) to confirmed omissions. Check the source, the existing question and what would publicly settle the proposed question. Then ask for the model’s account where a distinct, answerable question was missed. Stop the wording work if existing coverage, missing evidence or an unresolvable amount explains the omission. A repeated, identifiable misread earns a small quality test. Expanding coverage to already-listed companies is a legitimate choice for Alex. The frozen list’s granularity is a separate concern.\n\nCONSENSUS: Defer the release comparison, narrow diagnosis to verified misses, and recompute the ceiling before proposing broader company coverage.**Keep old in production and pause the hybrid release line.** No opening send currently earns its cost. I agree with that exit, with two corrections to the census.\n\n- **M12.** The signed Ohio guarantee belongs under the tracked AI-cloud total unless the annual filing gives analysts a separately meaningful Ohio balance. D210 specifies how to ask about an obligation’s amount. D248 and D388 determine whether it gets a separate question. The trace establishes no need for one here. [D248](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2959) · [D388](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4176)\n- **M27.** A deal that closes in May 2027 can satisfy M19 and still have an outstanding obligation at January year-end. A delay or termination also does not establish that $12.9B remains payable. Classify M27 as **separately answerable in principle, with the public settlement source unproven**. Leave it out of the attainable gains for now. [Frozen matters](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/census/matters.json:722)\n- **M07 and M16.** M07’s segment forecast is distinct from total revenue. The lack of a stated figure does not bar it, but the qualitative demand evidence does not establish a missed opening. M16 remains allowed. Exclude the failed wording change from the ceiling until a different mechanism has evidence behind it. [Frozen prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/prompts/role-v74i.txt:13) · [D481](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:5441)\n- **M20.** I meant old-1 **Q29 (`v29`)**. The stored $125B statement links directly to it. Q29 asks about platform agreements by August 15. Those agreements could be signed without the specific residual-value support M20 asks about by March 31. The broader question does not settle it. [Old-1 Q29](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/library-L6.json:11863)\n\n**Recommend option 2 to Alex.** Five speculative gains with zero losses can pass mathematically. The two concrete opportunities cannot carry that result. Drop the M20 account probe for release purposes. Widen company coverage only if Alex wants those forecasts for their own value. D543 supports closing the release investigation when another test would leave the decision unchanged. [D543](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:6604)\n\nDISAGREE**Keep poc-v118 in production and pause the hybrid release line.** D688 needs one wording change. Say “the census found no credible path to a D665 lead through opening changes alone.” Five gains with zero losses would pass on paper, but only M20 and M05 are concrete opportunities, and the roughly 1.4 losses are an estimate.\n\nNo opening test or M20 account probe is warranted for release. Keep the judge and reading fixes as quality work. Offer broader company coverage only if Alex wants those forecasts for their own value. Keep the release bar.\n\nCONSENSUS# Reflection council K (cold read, read-only, $0)\n\nYou are reviewing a research line cold. Read in this order:\n1. `docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/PLAN.md`;\n2. `docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md`, all of it;\n3. `docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/CENSUS.md`;\n4. `poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/diag-score-H1.json`;\n5. `docs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/RESULT.md`, the section \"Where the old system loses on the 32 matters\";\n6. in `docs/DECISIONS-log.md`, the records D198, D382, D439 and D681–D684 (search `## D198`, and so on).\n\n**The goal (Alex, D681):** build and test a hybrid of the old and new systems that serves an analyst better than the old one. Problems\nmust be found early, not at the end.\n\n**Answer, in under 350 words:**\n1. **The lessons**, at the level of mechanism.\n2. **Which conclusions are weaker than they look.**\n3. **The remaining problems, ranked by product impact on the 32 matters.**\n4. **The one next angle and its falsifier.** Choose between:\n   - continuing the judge line within D198, for example placing the previous conclusion before the evidence;\n   - taking D198's options to Alex;\n   - starting phase 2, the census of the 17 never-opened matters;\n   - something else.\n\n   Say what result would change the plan.\n\n**Out of scope:**\n- changing the frozen ruler or gold;\n- the c8 reading arm;\n- anything that pushes, deploys or commits.\n\nEnd with `CONSENSUS-READY: <one sentence>` or `OPEN: <the disagreement>`.\n# Reflection K: Claude's own, written before the cold reader's (2026-11-20)\n\n**Facts.**\n- **H1 (heading only)** fails council J's diagnostic: 0 net repairs against 2 required. v7 left 3 targets to repair, so the result is\n  conclusive. No control was lost, no refusals, and both plants were caught. By code, H1 kept the rejected rung in 8 of 9 target\n  answers; same-day v7 kept it in 7 of 9. The label does not matter. The content of the carried conclusion does: with it, 0 of 13\n  targets moved even on complete evidence; without it, 8 of 13 moved.\n- **Four angles on the judge have failed:** J2a (schema order), J2b (a check), the removal of the previous conclusion (screen missed by\n  one, gold 44.3 against 45.0), and H1.\n\n**Lessons, at the level of mechanism.**\n1. The anchor is the carried derivation itself. A label or an instruction around it does not loosen it.\n2. **Every remaining lever changes D198, which is Alex's decision.** The levers are:\n   - show the rung without the derivation;\n   - replace the derivation with a summary of the omitted evidence;\n   - show all evidence.\n\n   \"All evidence\" cannot scale: one question carries 2,264 pieces in force. That is why D198 clips.\n3. **The fresh census shows where the judge's errors sit.** They are in about ten big numeric bets, and the design has read all of\n   them. Yes/no completion questions are mostly right. Any judge change can be tested freshly only on a new window.\n\n**Weaker than they look.**\n- The 4 net repairs without the previous conclusion are dev.\n- Gold 44.3 against 45.0 sits inside v7's own range of 44–46.\n- C100 is not yet rechecked.\n- The judge's attainable gain on the 32 matters is M08 and M14 at most, so +2 matters.\n\n**Remaining problems, by product impact (PLAN, \"Attainable\").**\n1. **Opening:** 17 of 32 matters are never opened, and 3 more are opened in only 1 of 3 draws. This is the release's gap.\n2. **D382** (company-figure questions): 5 to 6 matters, open with Alex.\n3. **The judge:** at most 2 matters, and only through a change to D198.\n\n**The next angle.**\n- Close the judge's anchor line within D198. Take D198's three options to Alex as a choice with a recommendation, not as a work order.\n- Start phase 2 now: the $0 census of why the 17 matters were never opened.\n- One cheap within-D198 lever remains untested: the previous conclusion placed before the evidence instead of last. Run it only if\n  the cold reader finds a mechanism that a label lacked. Otherwise it is another corner (D543).\nStart phase 2 with a $0 review of the 17 matters the old system never turned into questions.\n\n**Lessons.** The judge can make a mistake once and repeat it for days. For example, it added $25B and $18B without establishing whether they overlapped, then carried that reasoning forward. With identical full evidence, removing the prior answer changed the chosen range in 8 of 13 rejected cases. Keeping it changed zero. Test whether readers support the answer against same-day repeats, then replay from day one so each version builds its own history.\n\n**Weaker conclusions.** Only four of those eight moves earned both readers’ support, one short of the threshold. Two were the same capex question in different draws. M08 still failed, and removing the prior answer hurt the China-share answer on the frozen check. Changing output order and adding reasoning checks both failed their screens. H1’s new heading repaired zero of three cases selected to give it a chance. Moving the prior answer earlier remains untested. The fresh pool yielded only one eligible failure, so it could not test whether a fix generalizes.\n\n**Impact on the 32 matters.** Opening ranks first. Seventeen never opened, and M05, M09 and M25 were served in only one draw each. The unresolved company-figure policy could put five or six matters the old system serves out of reach. Judge reasoning cost M08 and M14. M30 is a possible gain if company figures remain eligible.\n\n**Next angle and falsifier.** Trace where each unopened matter first fell out and whether the evidence available then could support an answer. Count the attainable gains under each figure policy. If even the generous ceiling, including both possible judge repairs, is below five additional matters, change the plan to address the constraint that sets that ceiling. Otherwise, test the strongest opening fix on final answers, requiring five net gains and at least twice as many wins as losses.\n\nCONSENSUS-READY: Start the opening census and keep D198 in place while the census establishes what the hybrid can realistically gain.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/RESULT.md; nl -ba src/hivemind/views/judging.py | sed -n '1,275p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/RESULT.md; nl -ba src/hivemind/views/judging.py | sed -n '1,275p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Merge test result (D679), 2026-11-19\n     2\t\n     3\t**Verdict, by the lines written before any send: the registered test did not pass.** It missed two lines (council e,\n     4\t`council-e-codex.md`, consensus).\n     5\t- **P2 missed on its second clause.** Both hybrid capex answers use total capex to answer a data-center capex question without naming it a\n     6\t  proxy. The plan wrote that clause as a higher bar than old met, and old's answers miss it too. It is the frozen judge's behaviour, not\n     7\t  something the merge's wiring added, but it is a miss.\n     8\t- **V missed.** Both readers rejected the rebuilt control's v47 answer. Three re-sends of its 09-16 request (rungs 2, 1, 2) show that the\n     9\t  request can give either answer. They cannot set the odds or rule out an effect of the reconstruction: the theme block and the\n    10\t  previous-answer wording differ.\n    11\t\n    12\t**What did hold:**\n    13\t- P1, delivery: verified on all 4 primary questions in both draws.\n    14\t- P3, talks stay talks: 2/2.\n    15\t- P5: both readers support all 4 hybrid answers.\n    16\t- P4 (diagnostic): 0 standalone NVIDIA-figure questions proposed.\n    17\t- The capex answers' scope: no five-company or 2027 figure was taken as the four-company 2026 answer. This is P2's first clause.\n    18\t\n    19\t**What it shows, and what it does not:**\n    20\t- The wiring (c8 filing under the old questions) delivers the matters' statements to the judge. On the two Anthropic questions, which\n    21\t  opened in the window, it carries the whole answer.\n    22\t- It does not show that the merge answers better than the old system. Old's answers to these questions were already supported, and the\n    23\t  capex band was already set by 09-06 history.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Spend:** $2.13 of the $5 ledger.\n    26\t- 140 c8 sends: about $1.75.\n    27\t- Judging two arms × 2 draws, 98 calls: about $0.34.\n    28\t- 15 noise re-sends: about $0.04.\n    29\t\n    30\t**Closed** at that, per the council. No further sends.\n    31\t\n    32\t## Answers at 09-20\n    33\t| Draw / question | Old (archived) | Control (old evidence, rebuilt) | **Hybrid (c8 filing)** |\n    34\t|---|---|---|---|\n    35\t| old-2 v5: four hyperscalers' 2026 data-center capex | rung 4 ($700–800B), supported | rung 4, supported | **rung 4, supported** |\n    36\t| old-2 v40: NVIDIA $10B Anthropic IPO anchor by 09-20 | No, supported | No, supported | **No, supported** |\n    37\t| old-3 v6: same as v5 | rung 4, supported | rung 4, supported | **rung 4, supported** |\n    38\t| old-3 v47: anchor agreed by 09-20 | No, supported | **Yes, unsupported** (both readers) | **No, supported** |\n    39\t| old-3 v48: anchor agreed by 2027-03-14 (negative control) | rung 1, unsupported; worth split | rung 1, unsupported; worth split | rung 1; answer split (Codex unsupported, Claude supported) |\n    40\t\n    41\t- **Readers:** Codex and Claude, blind under shuffled labels, with the comparison's card form and brief.\n    42\t- **Their controls:** the 3 plants were marked unsupported and the 2 supported controls supported, by both readers. A repeated card\n    43\t  (diagnostic C016 is the old v6 card) was marked the same.\n    44\t- **All primary marks settled in round 0.** Two debate marks, both on v48's worth, stayed void: Codex's reasons went over the\n    45\t  instrument's 40-word limit, which was left unchanged.\n    46\t\n    47\t## The lines, read by Claude in the traces\n    48\t| Line | Result | Evidence |\n    49\t|---|---|---|\n    50\t| P1 Delivery | 4/4 | The final requests of hybrid v5 and v6 carry c8's 09-14 Zacks item: \"the four top hyperscalers … roughly $720 billion to $745 billion … 2026\". Hybrid v40 and v47 carry c8's Reuters items: considering, or in advanced talks, on the $10B anchor (c8 tiered them `rumour`) |\n    51\t| P2 No meaning drift | **Missed** on its second clause; held on its first | First clause held: \"the nearly $800 billion … figures cover five companies, including Oracle, so they cannot be assigned in full to the four-company total\". Second clause missed: total capex is used for data-center capex without being named a proxy. Old misses it the same way |\n    52\t| P3 Talks stay talks | 2/2 | v40: \"reports of talks, not a signed or completed investment\". v47: \"consideration, negotiations, or discussions—not a signed agreement … NVIDIA's separate existing investment agreement … does not establish an IPO anchor commitment\" (c8 had filed that older filing item, and the judge did not take it) |\n    53\t| P4 Opening rule (diagnostic) | 0 of 7 proposals | c8 proposed 7 theses on the 140 sends: DRAM ×3, H100 rental rate, AI-lab revenue run rate, Anthropic anchor ×2. None is a standalone NVIDIA financial figure. None was opened |\n    54\t| P5 Supported answer | 4/4 | Both readers, round 0 |\n    55\t| V Validity | **Missed**: 3/4 control cards supported; the reader controls pass | The control's v47 flipped to \"Yes\" on 09-16 and stayed there (below) |\n    56\t\n    57\t## How much of each final answer rests on c8's filing (counted from the hybrid's final requests)\n    58\t| Question | Items shown | From c8 (window) | From old history (to 09-06) | Older items in force not shown | Items cited | Cited from c8 |\n    59\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    60\t| old-2 v5 capex | 46 | 26 | 20 | 123 | 20 | 7 |\n    61\t| old-3 v6 capex | 43 | 27 | 16 | 113 | 13 | 5 |\n    62\t| old-2 v40 Anthropic | 12 | 12 | 0 | 0 | 12 | 12 |\n    63\t| old-3 v47 Anthropic | 7 | 7 | 0 | 0 | 7 | 7 |\n    64\t\n    65\t- **The capex answers rest partly on old's history.** Rung 4 was reached before 09-06. On capex the test shows that c8's filing added the\n    66\t  latest four-company estimate without breaking the answer or drifting its scope.\n    67\t- **The two Anthropic questions opened in the window, and their answers rest wholly on c8's filing.** They are the cleanest evidence that\n    68\t  the wiring works.\n    69\t\n    70\t## The V exception, traced ($0.04 of re-sends)\n    71\t- **The final requests are stable.** Each arm's final v47 request, re-sent 3 times unchanged, gives its own answer 3/3: old No, control\n    72\t  Yes, hybrid No.\n    73\t- **The fork is 09-16.**\n    74\t  - The control's 09-16 request, re-sent 3 times, gives rungs 2, 1, 2.\n    75\t  - Old's 09-16 request gives 2, 2, 2.\n    76\t  - The two requests differ only in the theme block (other questions held at 09-06 in the copy) and in the wording of the previous\n    77\t    conclusion. Their evidence is identical.\n    78\t- **Reading (council e).** The request can give either answer, and the previous conclusion then carried the control's \"Yes\" forward.\n    79\t  Three sends neither set the odds nor rule out a reconstruction effect, since the theme block and the previous wording differ. V stays\n    80\t  missed.\n    81\t- **It does show that v47 is sampler-sensitive:** one draw of the hybrid's \"No\" is not proof that the hybrid is more stable there.\n    82\t\n    83\t## Evidence cleanliness (descriptive, one draw: old-2 v5's window material, read by Claude)\n    84\t| Window material, 09-07..09-20 | Old (23 items) | Hybrid (26 items) |\n    85\t|---|---|---|\n    86\t| Direct: the four companies, or one of them, on 2026 capex | 2 | 3–4 |\n    87\t| Adjacent and useful: leases, cloud backlog | 0 | 4 |\n    88\t| Five-company totals (context, other scope) | 8 | 8 |\n    89\t| Unclear period | 1 | 1 |\n    90\t| Off-question | 12 (4 on AI-lab safety) | 9 (Western Digital results, AI-lab revenue, PwC to 2050) |\n    91\t\n    92\tc8 also links loosely: the merge cleans this pile only slightly. Step 2–3 grouping was not run.\n    93\t\n    94\t## What this decides, and what it does not\n    95\t- **Decided:** wiring c8 under the old questions delivers the matters' decisive statements to the judge, with no five-company or 2027\n    96\t  figure taken as the answer. Both readers support the hybrid answers on M04 and M17, as they support old's. The registered test still\n    97\t  did not pass: P2's proxy clause and V missed.\n    98\t- **Not decided:**\n    99\t  - whether the merge serves *more* matters: the new-only matters need the clean opener;\n   100\t  - Steps 2–3 grouping;\n   101\t  - the other 30 matters;\n   102\t  - cost.\n   103\t- **Cost, priced from tokens.** The merge pays the old pipeline plus c8 reading: about $0.0125 per article at $1.75 / 140. Over the\n   104\t  82-day window's 1,884 articles that is about $24 on top of old's about $9.\n   105\t- **The sampler-sensitive v47 question needs more than one draw** before stability is claimed.\n   106\t\n   107\t## Where the old system loses on the 32 matters, and why this merge cannot raise its score ($0, read after the verdict)\n   108\tSources: the comparison's `reading-diagnostic/mapping-out/mapping.json` and `deck/readings.json`, and the deck key.\n   109\t- **Old serves 10 and never opens 17.** The 17 are M01–M03, M07, M12, M16, M20–M24, M26–M29 and M32 (only new-3 opened M32). The merge\n   110\t  keeps old's opening, so these stay lost.\n   111\t- **Opened in at least 2 of 3 old draws but not served: M08, M14 and M30.**\n   112\t  - Every M08 and M14 answer was rejected by the readers. The readers' reasons are judge reasoning, not missing material:\n   113\t    - M14 (four companies' 2027 capex): rung 4 was taken from NVIDIA's own $1.3T forecast or a five-company $1.08T total. In old-1 the\n   114\t      material held BofA's four-company $930–980B.\n   115\t    - M08 (NVIDIA's outstanding investment commitments): $25B and $18B were added, though they probably overlap (old-2, old-3). In\n   116\t      old-1 the answer was anchored on Bank of America's $70B, a cumulative investment estimate rather than an outstanding balance.\n   117\t      (Corrected 2026-11-20. A first version cited \"a range against its own estimate of about $53B\". That card, C140, was a planted\n   118\t      reader control, not a system answer.)\n   119\t  - M30 is a company figure (D382/D505).\n   120\t- **Served in only 1 of 3 old draws:** M05, M09 and M25.\n   121\t- **The consequence.** Clean Step 1 reads more of the facts. The old system loses at opening and at the judge's reasoning, neither of\n   122\t  which better reading fixes. The test's targets, M04 and M17, were matters old already served, so the test could show \"does not\n   123\t  break\", never \"improves\". That was a design error of mine.\n   124\t- **Across all old cards rated worth tracking:** 18–21 of 28–29 supported per draw; 3–5 unsupported per draw.\n   125\t\n   126\t## Errors of mine in this test (logged)\n   127\t- **I read the census article numbers as positions in `kept-articles.json`.** They are numbers in `census/numbering.json`. Codex caught it\n   128\t  in round c. My first claim, that old filed none of the census articles, was wrong: 8 of 11 reached the old questions.\n   129\t- **The first article list included custom-chip and DRAM theses** as \"capex\" (the same misreading in PATH-TO-PASS's census C, to be\n   130\t  re-checked).\n   131\t- **The first judging copy failed.** The store's append-only triggers refused the cut; Codex found it in round d before any send.\n   132\t- **P2's second clause has no source in the frozen ruler** (checked after the verdict, $0). The judgement ruler (`ruler-v1.md`) and the\n   133\t  judge's prompt say nothing about naming a proxy. The extraction standard counts \"another population or proxy of one aggregate\" as\n   134\t  evidence for the same question (D190, D197), and asks for no label. I wrote the clause myself, as a bar the frozen judge was known not\n   135\t  to meet. The verdict stays as scored. A judge that labels proxies would be a new rule, and its source can only be Alex's words.\n     1\t\"\"\"06-views.md step 7: one re-judging round. Open views whose reading changed are judged in dependency order, each\n     2\tonce; a view whose conclusion changed adds its open dependants. The model reads the evidence in force (with each\n     3\treplaced predecessor), the dependencies' current conclusions and its own last conclusion (for comparison only).\n     4\tCode checks every citation and that a rung move or a first judgement says changed; one retry, then the item fails.\n     5\t\n     6\tThe request is judging-v7 (D354, frozen with steps 3 and 4): no other version is built, and a configuration that names another is\n     7\trefused before any call. Every answer is stored in the canonical vocabulary.\n     8\t\n     9\tThe theme block (D191, D195): an asset thesis is judged with every open, judged theme thesis in view and answers, in theme_pushes,\n    10\twhich way each theme it weighed pushes it; theme theses are judged before asset theses in a round, and a theme judgement that says\n    11\tchanged queues every open asset thesis with evidence in force, once. The round's queue is derived from the ledger (the day's changed\n    12\tviews, the dependants of views judged at this sequence number with a changed conclusion, and the asset theses a changed theme queues),\n    13\tso a resumed round recovers what an earlier commit had queued.\n    14\t\n    15\tThe evidence in view (D198): a judgement reads the arrivals and the basis among the evidence in force, not everything in force.\n    16\t\n    17\tThe reference (D260): the rung is the forecast the evidence in force makes most likely, judged against what the market expects\n    18\t(consensus, guidance, the stated plan); the answer states the reference and the outcome the evidence implies, both stored beside the\n    19\trung, and an empty one is refused.\"\"\"\n    20\timport dataclasses\n    21\timport json\n    22\timport re\n    23\t\n    24\tfrom .. import config, model, vocabulary\n    25\tfrom . import store\n    26\t\n    27\tPUSHES = vocabulary.PUSHES\n    28\tSUPPORTED_VERSIONS = ('judging-v7',)   # D354: the frozen judging prompt\n    29\tREADINGS = 5                      # a piece is shown in the thesis's first five judgements after it arrives\n    30\tBASIS = 10                        # and while one of the last ten judgements cited it\n    31\tTHEME_REASONS_CHARS = 300\n    32\tUPDATED_MARK = 'updated since this thesis was last judged'\n    33\t\n    34\t\n    35\tdef require_supported(prompt_version):\n    36\t    \"\"\"Refuses a judging prompt version this code does not build, before any request is made.\"\"\"\n    37\t    if prompt_version not in SUPPORTED_VERSIONS:\n    38\t        raise ValueError(f'judging {prompt_version!r} is not built; this code builds only {\", \".join(SUPPORTED_VERSIONS)} (D354)')\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\tdef evidence_in_view(conn, view_id):\n    42\t    \"\"\"(the pieces a judgement of this thesis reads, how many pieces in force are not shown): the arrivals (linked after the\n    43\t    READINGS-th most recent judgement, or all when there are fewer) and the basis (cited by one of the last BASIS judgements),\n    44\t    both among the evidence in force.\"\"\"\n    45\t    in_force = store.evidence_in_force(conn, view_id)\n    46\t    recent = store.recent_judgements(conn, view_id, BASIS)\n    47\t    arrivals_after = recent[READINGS - 1]['seq'] if len(recent) >= READINGS else -1\n    48\t    basis = {evidence_id for j in recent for evidence_id in j['cites']}\n    49\t    linked_at = {r['evidence_id']: r['seq'] for r in conn.execute('select evidence_id, seq from evidence_links where view_id=?', (view_id,))}\n    50\t    shown = [p for p in in_force if linked_at.get(p['evidence_id'], -1) > arrivals_after or p['evidence_id'] in basis]\n    51\t    return shown, len(in_force) - len(shown)\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef schema_for(prompt_version):\n    55\t    require_supported(prompt_version)\n    56\t    pushes = PUSHES\n    57\t    schema = {\n    58\t        'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False,\n    59\t        'required': ['rung', 'reasons', 'cites', 'cites_views', 'pushes', 'changed', 'why_changed'],\n    60\t        'properties': {\n    61\t            'rung': {'type': 'integer'}, 'reasons': {'type': 'string'},\n    62\t            'cites': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}}, 'cites_views': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}},\n    63\t            'pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['evidence_id', 'push'],\n    64\t                                                  'properties': {'evidence_id': {'type': 'string'}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': pushes}}}},\n    65\t            'changed': {'type': 'boolean'}, 'why_changed': {'type': 'string'},\n    66\t        },\n    67\t    }\n    68\t    schema['required'].append('theme_pushes')\n    69\t    schema['properties']['theme_pushes'] = {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['view_id', 'push'],\n    70\t                                                                       'properties': {'view_id': {'type': 'string'}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': pushes}}}}\n    71\t    schema['required'][1:1] = STANCE_FIELDS           # after rung: the reference and the implied outcome are written before the reasons\n    72\t    schema['properties'].update({field: {'type': 'string'} for field in STANCE_FIELDS})\n    73\t    return schema\n    74\t\n    75\t\n    76\tdef themes_in_force(conn, view_id):\n    77\t    \"\"\"The theme block of an asset thesis: every open theme thesis with a current state, with its rung label and whether a\n    78\t    changed conclusion was recorded after this thesis's last judgement. Empty for a theme thesis.\"\"\"\n    79\t    if store.view(conn, view_id)['object_kind'] == 'theme':\n    80\t        return []\n    81\t    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n    82\t    out = []\n    83\t    for row in conn.execute(\"select view_id from views where object_kind='theme' order by view_id\"):\n    84\t        theme_id = row['view_id']\n    85\t        state = store.current_state(conn, theme_id)\n    86\t        if not state or not store.is_open(conn, theme_id):\n    87\t            continue\n    88\t        updated = bool(last) and bool(conn.execute('select 1 from judgements where view_id=? and changed=1 and judgement_id>? limit 1',\n    89\t                                                   (theme_id, last['judgement_id'])).fetchone())\n    90\t        rung = store.rungs_of(conn, theme_id)[state['rung'] - 1]\n    91\t        out.append({'view_id': theme_id, 'view': store.view(conn, theme_id), 'state': state, 'rung_label': rung['label'], 'updated': updated})\n    92\t    return out\n    93\t\n    94\t\n    95\tSTANCE_FIELDS = ['reference', 'implied_outcome']   # D260: what the market expects, and the figure or date the evidence implies\n    96\t\n    97\t\n    98\tclass JudgementRefused(Exception):\n    99\t    pass\n   100\t\n   101\t\n   102\tdef topological(conn, view_ids):\n   103\t    \"\"\"Dependencies first: a view comes after every view it depends on that is also in the set.\"\"\"\n   104\t    remaining = list(dict.fromkeys(view_ids))\n   105\t    ordered, placed = [], set()\n   106\t    while remaining:\n   107\t        progressed = False\n   108\t        for view_id in list(remaining):\n   109\t            deps = {d['resolved_to'] for d in store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id) if d['resolved_to']}\n   110\t            if deps.isdisjoint(set(remaining) - {view_id}) or deps <= placed:\n   111\t                ordered.append(view_id)\n   112\t                placed.add(view_id)\n   113\t                remaining.remove(view_id)\n   114\t                progressed = True\n   115\t        if not progressed:                 # a cycle slipped through: keep going in the given order\n   116\t            ordered.extend(remaining)\n   117\t            break\n   118\t    return ordered\n   119\t\n   120\t\n   121\tdef _lines_english(conn, view_id, v, rungs, pieces, deps, last, themes=(), hidden=0):\n   122\t    \"\"\"hidden: how many pieces in force are not shown.\"\"\"\n   123\t    lines = [f'Thesis {view_id} ({v[\"object_id\"]}, {v[\"kind\"]}, focus \"{v[\"looks_at\"]}\", {v[\"year\"] or \"\"}, due {v[\"due_day\"]}): {v[\"question\"]}',\n   124\t             'Ladder: ' + ' / '.join(f'rung {r[\"position\"]} {r[\"label\"]}: {r[\"condition\"]}' for r in rungs), '',\n   125\t             f'[Evidence in view under this thesis, {len(pieces)} items]']\n   126\t    if hidden:\n   127\t        lines.append(f'[Not shown: {hidden} more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]')\n   128\t    for p in pieces:\n   129\t        src = p['sources'][0] if p['sources'] else {}\n   130\t        lines.append(f'- {p[\"evidence_id\"]} [{p[\"tier\"]}; {p[\"speaker\"]}; original source {p[\"original_who\"]} {p[\"original_day\"]}; from {src.get(\"input_id\")} {src.get(\"publication_day\")}] {p[\"one_line\"]}\\n    Quote: {p[\"quote\"][:400]}')\n   131\t        if p.get('replaced'):\n   132\t            lines.append(f'    It replaces {p[\"replaced\"][\"evidence_id\"]} ({p[\"replaced\"][\"original_day\"]}): {p[\"replaced\"][\"one_line\"]}')\n   133\t        if p.get('same_as'):\n   134\t            lines.append(f'    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as {\", \".join(p[\"same_as\"])}; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.')\n   135\t        if p.get('revises'):\n   136\t            lines.append(f'    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of {p[\"revises\"][\"evidence_id\"]} ({p[\"revises\"][\"original_day\"]}); both are in force: {p[\"revises\"][\"one_line\"]}')\n   137\t    if deps:\n   138\t        lines.append('\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]')\n   139\t        for d in deps:\n   140\t            target = d['resolved_to']\n   141\t            if not target:\n   142\t                lines.append(f'- the {d[\"wait_year\"]} thesis on chain {d[\"wait_chain_view_id\"]}: not yet created ({d[\"direction\"]})')\n   143\t                continue\n   144\t            tv = store.view(conn, target)\n   145\t            state = store.current_state(conn, target)\n   146\t            flag = 'historical only' if d['resolution'] == 'history_only' else ('closed' if not store.is_open(conn, target) else 'open')\n   147\t            rung_label = store.rungs_of(conn, target)[state['rung'] - 1]['label'] if state else 'not judged yet'\n   148\t            lines.append(f'- {target} ({flag}; {d[\"direction\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now rung {state[\"rung\"] if state else \"-\"} {rung_label}; reasons: {(state or {}).get(\"reasons\", \"\")[:600]}')\n   149\t    if themes:\n   150\t        lines.append(f'\\n[Themes in force, {len(themes)}]')\n   151\t        for theme in themes:\n   152\t            tv, state = theme['view'], theme['state']\n   153\t            mark = f'; {UPDATED_MARK}' if theme['updated'] else ''\n   154\t            lines.append(f'- {theme[\"view_id\"]} ({tv[\"object_id\"]}, due {tv[\"due_day\"]}) {tv[\"question\"]}: now rung {state[\"rung\"]} {theme[\"rung_label\"]}; '\n   155\t                         f'judged {state[\"publication_day\"]}{mark}; reasons: {(state[\"reasons\"] or \"\")[:THEME_REASONS_CHARS]}')\n   156\t    if last:\n   157\t        lines.append(f'\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung {last[\"rung\"]}; reasons: {last[\"reasons\"][:800]}')\n   158\t    else:\n   159\t        lines.append('\\n[Previous conclusion] none: this is the first judgement, so changed must be true.')\n   160\t    lines.append('\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; '\n   161\t                 + ('cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above' if deps else 'cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis')\n   162\t                 + ('; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views' if themes else '') + '.')\n   163\t    return lines\n   164\t\n   165\t\n   166\tdef build_prompt(conn, view_id, prompt_version):\n   167\t    require_supported(prompt_version)\n   168\t    v = store.view(conn, view_id)\n   169\t    rungs = store.rungs_of(conn, view_id)\n   170\t    pieces, hidden = evidence_in_view(conn, view_id)\n   171\t    deps = store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id)\n   172\t    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n   173\t    lines = _lines_english(conn, view_id, v, rungs, pieces, deps, last, themes_in_force(conn, view_id), hidden=hidden)\n   174\t    with open(config.prompt_path(prompt_version + '.txt'), encoding='utf-8') as handle:\n   175\t        return handle.read(), '\\n'.join(lines)\n   176\t\n   177\t\n   178\tdef retry_note(errors):\n   179\t    return '\\n\\nThe code returned the previous answer because: ' + '; '.join(errors) + '\\nCorrect it and answer again, citing only the listed ids.'\n   180\t\n   181\t\n   182\tdef themes_shown_in(request_text):\n   183\t    \"\"\"The theme ids of the block a request carried: the ids the answer may name, whatever the ledger says now.\"\"\"\n   184\t    block = request_text.split('\\n[Themes in force, ', 1)\n   185\t    return set(re.findall(r'^- (v\\d+) \\(', block[1].split('\\n\\n[', 1)[0], re.M)) if len(block) == 2 else set()\n   186\t\n   187\t\n   188\tdef evidence_shown_in(request_text):\n   189\t    \"\"\"The evidence ids the request listed: the ids the answer may cite and push.\"\"\"\n   190\t    block = request_text.split('\\n[Evidence in ', 1)\n   191\t    return set(re.findall(r'^- (e\\d+) \\[', block[1].split('\\n\\n[', 1)[0], re.M)) if len(block) == 2 else set()\n   192\t\n   193\t\n   194\tdef check_answer(conn, view_id, data, request_text=None):\n   195\t    \"\"\"request_text: the user message that was sent, so theme and evidence ids are checked against what it carried (a theme may have\n   196\t    closed or opened since); without it, against what the ledger would show now.\"\"\"\n   197\t    errors = []\n   198\t    shown = themes_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {theme['view_id'] for theme in themes_in_force(conn, view_id)}\n   199\t    named = []\n   200\t    for entry in data.get('theme_pushes', []):\n   201\t        if entry['view_id'] in named:\n   202\t            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is named twice in theme_pushes')\n   203\t        elif entry['view_id'] not in shown:\n   204\t            errors.append(f'{entry[\"view_id\"]} is not a theme in force shown to {view_id}')\n   205\t        named.append(entry['view_id'])\n   206\t    # the closed set is what the request showed (D198), read from the text that was sent, else computed from the ledger\n   207\t    allowed = evidence_shown_in(request_text) if request_text is not None else {p['evidence_id'] for p in evidence_in_view(conn, view_id)[0]}\n   208\t    not_allowed = lambda e: f'{e} is not among the evidence shown to {view_id}'\n   209\t    bad_push = lambda e: f'push on {e} which was not shown to {view_id}'\n   210\t    deps = {d['resolved_to'] for d in store.dependencies_of(conn, view_id) if d['resolved_to']}\n   211\t    rungs = store.rungs_of(conn, view_id)\n   212\t    if not 1 <= data['rung'] <= len(rungs):\n   213\t        errors.append(f'rung {data[\"rung\"]} outside 1..{len(rungs)}')\n   214\t    for cited in data['cites']:\n   215\t        if cited not in allowed:\n   216\t            errors.append(not_allowed(cited))\n   217\t    for cited in data['cites_views']:\n   218\t        if cited not in deps:\n   219\t            errors.append(f'{cited} is not a dependency of {view_id}')\n   220\t    for push in data['pushes']:\n   221\t        if push['evidence_id'] not in allowed:\n   222\t            errors.append(bad_push(push['evidence_id']))\n   223\t    for field in STANCE_FIELDS:\n   224\t        if not str(data.get(field) or '').strip():\n   225\t            errors.append(f'{field} is empty: state what the market expects and the figure or date the evidence implies')\n   226\t    last = store.current_state(conn, view_id)\n   227\t    if last is None and not data['changed']:\n   228\t        errors.append('first judgement must say changed')\n   229\t    if last is not None and last['rung'] != data['rung'] and not data['changed']:\n   230\t        errors.append(f'rung moved from {last[\"rung\"]} to {data[\"rung\"]} but changed is false')\n   231\t    return errors\n   232\t\n   233\t\n   234\tdef _record(conn, cfg, view_id, seq, item, answer):\n   235\t    data = vocabulary.judgement_answer(answer.data)\n   236\t    judgement_id = store.insert(conn, 'judgements', view_id=view_id, seq=seq, input_id=item['input_id'], rung=data['rung'],\n   237\t                                reasons=data['reasons'], cited_evidence_json=json.dumps(data['cites']), cited_views_json=json.dumps(data['cites_views']),\n   238\t                                pushes_json=json.dumps(data['pushes'], ensure_ascii=False), changed=1 if data['changed'] else 0,\n   239\t                                theme_pushes_json=json.dumps(data.get('theme_pushes', []), ensure_ascii=False),\n   240\t                                reference=data.get('reference'), implied_outcome=data.get('implied_outcome'),\n   241\t                                why_changed=data['why_changed'], config_checksum=cfg.checksum, call_id=answer.call_id,\n   242\t                                request_sha256=answer.request_sha256, publication_day=item['publication_day'])\n   243\t    conn.commit()\n   244\t    return {'view_id': view_id, 'judgement_id': judgement_id, 'rung': data['rung'], 'changed': data['changed'], 'cost': answer.cost_usd, 'cached': answer.cached}\n   245\t\n   246\t\n   247\tJUDGE_WORKERS = 32     # a day's round is up to 40 judgements of about 16 s each in two dependency levels (themes, then assets); 32 at a time makes a round about two calls long (2026-11-02)\n   248\t\n   249\t\n   250\tdef upstream(conn, view_id):\n   251\t    \"\"\"Every view this one depends on, directly or through its dependencies.\"\"\"\n   252\t    seen, stack = set(), [view_id]\n   253\t    while stack:\n   254\t        for dep in store.dependencies_of(conn, stack.pop()):\n   255\t            target = dep['resolved_to']\n   256\t            if target and target not in seen:\n   257\t                seen.add(target)\n   258\t                stack.append(target)\n   259\t    return seen\n   260\t\n   261\t\n   262\tdef open_asset_views(conn):\n   263\t    return [v for v in store.open_views(conn) if store.view(conn, v)['object_kind'] != 'theme']\n   264\t\n   265\t\n   266\tdef queued_by_change(conn, view_id):\n   267\t    \"\"\"What a changed conclusion of this view queues: its dependants and, for a theme thesis, every open asset thesis. The one rule,\n   268\t    read for a live judgement and for the judgements a resumed round finds committed.\"\"\"\n   269\t    queued = store.dependants_of(conn, view_id)\n   270\t    if store.view(conn, view_id)['object_kind'] == 'theme':\n   271\t        queued = queued + open_asset_views(conn)\n   272\t    return queued\n   273\t\n   274\t\n   275\tdef queued_by_ledger(conn, seq):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
