('window/old-2/replay/checks.json', 1616, 1612, 1616), ('prodpath/replay/checks.json', 72, 72, 72)]\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/deck-full/C100.txt | sed -n '1,20p;78,112p'; rg -n '## 7.3|## 11|read-only' AGENTS.md | head -25; cat poc/config/poc-v118.json | head -55\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/deck-full/C100.txt | sed -n '1,20p;78,112p'; rg -n '## 7.3|## 11|read-only' AGENTS.md | head -25; cat poc/config/poc-v118.json | head -55\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tQuestion: At NVIDIA’s FY2027 annual filing, what amount of equity investment commitments will NVIDIA report as outstanding or committed?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-08-12\n     3\tDue: 2027-02-28\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. NVIDIA reports less than $10 billion of outstanding or committed equity investment commitments.\n     6\t2. NVIDIA reports at least $10 billion but less than $30 billion.\n     7\t3. NVIDIA reports at least $30 billion but less than $50 billion.\n     8\t4. NVIDIA reports at least $50 billion but less than $70 billion.\n     9\t5. NVIDIA reports at least $70 billion.\n    10\t\n    11\tLatest answer at the cutoff: NVIDIA reports at least $10 billion but less than $30 billion.\n    12\tReason given: Reference: The latest company-reported balance, $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of July 26, 2026, is the reference and sits in rung 2. No separate market consensus for the FY2027 ending balance is supplied. Implied outcome: Approximately $20–25 billion outstanding or committed at the annual filing: the $25 billion reported July balance anchors the estimate, while the later $18 billion remainder-of-FY commitment is not shown to be additive or to remain outstanding at year-end. Reasons: The latest company-reported balance is $25 billion, in rung 2. NVIDIA later said $18 billion was committed for the remainder of FY2027, but the evidence does not establish that this is additive to the July balance or that it will remain outstanding at the annual filing; it is a remainder-of-year commitment, not an ending balance. The implied ending amount is therefore around $20–25 billion, below the $30 billion rung-3 boundary. NVIDIA’s statement that several very large FY2027 investments would not recur next year also argues against projecting another comparable step-up.\n    13\t\n    14\tMaterial that answer was given (96 items, by day):\n    15\t1. (third_party_view; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker Yahoo Finance; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya; published 2026-08-12) Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA has committed $70 billion in equity investments in ecosystem partners; the period was not specified.\n    16\t   Quote: Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya.\n    17\t2. (third_party_view; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker Yahoo Finance; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya; published 2026-08-12) Arya’s research cited recent NVIDIA investments of $30 billion in OpenAI, up to $10 billion in Anthropic, and $5 billion in Safe Superintelligence; the period was not specified.\n    18\t   Quote: Recent investments include $30 billion in OpenAI, up to $10 billion in Anthropic, and $5 billion in Safe Superintelligence.\n    19\t3. (third_party_view; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker Yahoo Finance; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya; published 2026-08-12) Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya estimated NVIDIA could generate up to about $470 billion of free cash flow over 2026–2027, and said its $70 billion in investments would equal about 15% of that cash flow.\n    20\t   Quote: Overall, we view Nvidia's $70 billion investments as easily manageable given its ability to generate as much as about $470 billion of free cash flow over just the next two years (2026-2027), or about 15% of free cash flow,\n    78\t   Quote: and invest another $1 billion in the startup at a $12 billion pre-money valuation.\n    79\t33. (party_statement; publisher NVIDIA Corporation; speaker Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA said it had invested nearly $50 billion in Frontier AI labs, a small fraction of expected free cash flow over the same period.\n    80\t   Quote: First, we've invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI labs. This was a meaningful commitment, but it represented a small fraction of our expected free cash flow over the same period.\n    81\t34. (party_statement; publisher NVIDIA Corporation; speaker NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA said its equity investments focus on AI model makers, infrastructure financiers, and other private companies, subject to contingencies.\n    82\t   Quote: Our equity investments are focused on AI model makers, infrastructure financiers, and other private companies, subject to certain contingencies.\n    83\t35. (fact; publisher NVIDIA Corporation; speaker NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA reported $99 billion of equity investments and $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of July 26, 2026.\n    84\t   Quote: We have made, and may continue to make, investments and commitments in our ecosystem to enhance our growth opportunities, cultivate our ecosystem, and strengthen our competitive position. These include equity investments of $99 billion and equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026.\n    85\t36. (party_statement; publisher cnbc.com; speaker Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) Jensen Huang said NVIDIA had several very large AI-lab investments in FY2027 and did not expect those investments to recur next year.\n    86\t   Quote: furthermore, this year we have several very large investments. And those large investments in AI labs are not going to repeat.\n    87\t37. (party_statement; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA had invested or committed nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\n    88\t   Quote: Nvidia has invested or committed nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, Kress said.\n    89\t38. (rumour; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com, reporting unnamed sources; published 2026-08-26) Unnamed sources reported on 2026-08-26 that NVIDIA was in advanced talks to invest in Perplexity at a valuation above US$30 billion; the investment amount and stake size were not stated.\n    90\t   Quote: Nvidia (NasdaqGS: NVDA) is in advanced talks to invest in AI startup Perplexity at a valuation above US$30b, according to people familiar with the matter.\n    91\t39. (third_party_view; publisher gurufocus.com; speaker gurufocus.com; original source Bank of America; published 2026-08-26) Bank of America estimated NVIDIA’s committed capital to date at about $300 billion, including about $70 billion of direct equity investments and $230 billion of guarantees or backstops.\n    92\t   Quote: The firm estimates roughly $300 billion of committed capital to date, including about $70 billion of direct equity investments and $230 billion of guarantees or backstops.\n    93\t40. (party_statement; publisher marketbeat.com; speaker MarketBeat; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO; published 2026-08-26) Colette Kress said NVIDIA had invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI laboratories.\n    94\t   Quote: Kress said NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI laboratories\n    95\t41. (third_party_view; publisher barchart.com; speaker barchart.com author; original source barchart.com author; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA was reported to be in talks about taking a stake in Perplexity AI at a valuation of $30 billion, up from $20 billion last year.\n    96\t   Quote: And now it's in talks with Perplexity AI as it considers taking a stake that would value the AI startup at $30 billion, an increase from $20 billion last year.\n    97\t42. (third_party_view; publisher barchart.com; speaker barchart.com author; original source Perplexity AI; published 2026-08-26) Perplexity AI's annualized revenue increased in 2026 from $250 million to more than $750 million.\n    98\t   Quote: Perplexity, which is an AI-powered platform that searches the internet and provides answers with numbered source citations, has seen its annualized revenue increase this year from $250 million to more than $750 million.\n    99\t43. (party_statement; publisher taekim.substack.com; speaker NVIDIA, quoted by taekim.substack.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA said it expected Anthropic and OpenAI to become the largest technology companies in history and said it expected excellent returns and limited risk from its investments in them.\n   100\t   Quote: “We expect them to become the largest technology companies in history. We believe these investments, measured against the strength of their demand, the business they create for us, the ecosystem they build on Nvidia’s platform, and the equity returns on our invested capital will be excellent, and our risk is limited. The Nvidia Compute platform is fungible and durable and can be redeployed to supp\n   101\t44. (fact; publisher fortune.com; speaker Fortune; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA has publicized investments totaling nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\n   102\t   Quote: Nvidia has also publicized investments of nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs\n   103\t45. (party_statement; publisher fortune.com; speaker Fortune, reporting Colette Kress's remarks; original source Colette Kress 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) CFO Colette Kress characterized NVIDIA's partner investments as low-risk and high-reward.\n   104\t   Quote: Kress said the investments in partners were low-risk, high-reward for Nvidia.\n   105\t46. (party_statement; publisher fortune.com; speaker Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) Colette Kress said NVIDIA expected excellent returns and limited risk from its partner investments, considering their demand, business generated, ecosystem value, and equity returns.\n   106\t   Quote: “We believe these investments, measured against the strength of their demand, the business they create for us, the ecosystem they build on Nvidia’s platform, and the equity returns on our invested capital will be excellent, and our risk is limited,” said Kress.\n   107\t47. (party_statement; publisher businessinsider.com; speaker Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's statement; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA said it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of FY2027, in addition to $47.9 billion in existing private-company holdings.\n   108\t   Quote: The company said Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year, on top of $47.9 billion it already holds in private companies.\n   109\t48. (party_statement; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress, quoted by Yahoo Finance; original source Colette Kress 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-27) NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said the company expects its investments’ returns to be excellent and considers its risk limited.\n   110\t   Quote: “We believe these investments, measured against the strength of their demand, the business they create for us, the ecosystem they build on Nvidia's platform, and the equity returns on our invested capital will be excellent, and our risk is limited,” Kress said on the call.\n   111\t49. (party_statement; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-27) NVIDIA has earmarked $18 billion for equity investments through the rest of its fiscal year.\n   112\t   Quote: Nvidia has also earmarked $18 billion for equity investments through the rest of this fiscal year, part of a shift CNBC has described as Nvidia's moat moving from chips to capital .\n43:  an independent read-only Codex review and Claude argue it, round after round, on the data, the PRD and its quantified goals, until\n202:  clause by clause, in both directions, with a blind read-only second reader: each clause is consistent, contradicts, keeps wider, keeps\n275:  or lists it for Alex's decision with a lean; settling wording is no licence to change behaviour. After each rewrite, a read-only Codex\n326:### 7.3 Iterating\n456:## 11. Design documents and decisions\n477:- Codex reviews read-only: `codex exec -s read-only -C <repo> --skip-git-repo-check --ephemeral -o <out> - < <prompt>`. The prompt names\n479:- Before a freeze, review in rounds. Each round runs read-only reviews by angle: an end-to-end simulation of realistic items,\n{\n  \"version\": \"poc-v118\",\n  \"_note\": \"Field-free configuration derived from poc-v117.json; all active model and prompt settings remain unchanged.\",\n  \"provider\": \"openai\",\n  \"search_top_n\": 10,\n  \"extraction\": {\n    \"T1\": {\n      \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\",\n      \"prompt_version\": \"T1-v74i\",\n      \"reasoning_effort\": \"low\",\n      \"max_output_tokens\": 16000\n    },\n    \"T2\": {\n      \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\",\n      \"prompt_version\": \"T2-v74i\",\n      \"reasoning_effort\": \"low\",\n      \"max_output_tokens\": 16000\n    },\n    \"T3\": {\n      \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\",\n      \"prompt_version\": \"T3-v74i\",\n      \"reasoning_effort\": \"low\",\n      \"max_output_tokens\": 12000\n    },\n    \"T4\": {\n      \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\",\n      \"prompt_version\": \"T4-v74i\",\n      \"reasoning_effort\": \"low\",\n      \"max_output_tokens\": 16000\n    },\n    \"T5\": {\n      \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\",\n      \"prompt_version\": \"T5-v74i\",\n      \"reasoning_effort\": \"low\",\n      \"max_output_tokens\": 12000\n    },\n    \"T6\": {\n      \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\",\n      \"prompt_version\": \"T6-v74i\",\n      \"reasoning_effort\": \"low\",\n      \"max_output_tokens\": 12000\n    },\n    \"T7\": {\n      \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\",\n      \"prompt_version\": \"T7-v74i\",\n      \"reasoning_effort\": \"low\",\n      \"max_output_tokens\": 8000\n    },\n    \"T8\": {\n      \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\",\n      \"prompt_version\": \"T8-v74i\",\n      \"reasoning_effort\": \"low\",\n      \"max_output_tokens\": 8000\n    },\n    \"T10\": {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^## D(51|368|520|521|524|526|532|534|544)'\"\\\\b|dependenc(y|ies).*(Jev|jev)|(?:Jev|jev).*dependenc' docs/DECISIONS-log.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | head -80; rg -n -i 'dependency|dependencies|depends_on' src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py; rg -n 'dependencies|dependency' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/{DESIGN-J2.md,PLAN.md}\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^## D(51|368|520|521|524|526|532|534|544)'\"\\\\b|dependenc(y|ies).*(Jev|jev)|(?:Jev|jev).*dependenc' docs/DECISIONS-log.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md | head -80; rg -n -i 'dependency|dependencies|depends_on' src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py; rg -n 'dependencies|dependency' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/{DESIGN-J2.md,PLAN.md}\"","aggregated_output":"docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:145:4. **A thesis opened from an article gets no dependency of its own**: the default Jev matching asks for none (`views/jev_matching.py`), although D51 has the matching call choose them; themes still reach every company thesis through the theme block (D191, D195). Found in the 2026-11-05 consolidation; building it is decided (D368).\ndocs/CURRENT_STATE.md:150:9. **Earlier defects, unchanged.** Matching (build log row 49): another document's copy of a figure can be treated as new evidence; one matter can occupy several groups; same-answer candidate references caused seven chain or duplication decisions of the generative matcher; empty `original_who` values can count as the same party; different names for one population cause two of nine Jev merge misses. Nine 2026 aggregate-spending theses under `ai-capex` each have a different population; whether to combine them is a product choice. Extraction's instructions remain narrower than the PRD's important-question rule and omit the personal-memo exception, and extraction can change a quantity's meaning before matching (a guarantee amount against financing provided). Classification keeps its [recorded simulation defects](research/2026-10-25-english/classification-existing-defects.txt). The corpus audit recorded 97 official notices with 56 body and file-name collisions, to re-fetch before relying on a rebuilt corpus. An older intermittent search-test failure is untraced. Fresh Idea allocations vary while saved weights do not; the confidence chart applies the current plan to past states and is not a calibrated probability; broad analyst-prose quality and fresh Idea stability remain unaccepted (the [Idea contract](poc/idea-card.md) lists its limits); generative matching dependencies await `matching-v4`. The readers' doubts about the frozen standard, listed in the [extraction branch's state page](research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/current-state-extraction-branch-2026-11-03.md), remain for Alex.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:1256:## D51 · 2026-10-21 · Claude's open items settled: one re-judging rule, dependencies chosen in the matching call, no consensus\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:2364:- At 32 workers, a three-wave schedule using recorded Jev call durations projects 8.6× on July 27 and 3.2× over 31 nonempty days, before reconciliation/rate-limit overhead. This replaces the unsupported general 10× claim, not the speed target. It is a schedule estimate, not fresh measured latency. Judging already uses six workers per dependency level.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:3177:- The default page browses the collected English Jev library directly. Stored groups organize peer theses; ungrouped theses remain visible. No parent thesis, group score or dependency is invented. The existing Idea submission and graph remain a separate explicit user action.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:3957:  Alex. Found on the way: the default Jev path chooses no dependencies although D51 has matching choose them.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:3960:## D368 · 2026-11-05 · Alex's answers to the seven items left open by the consolidation\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:3970:     (D191 point 2); the matching step chooses a new thesis's dependencies (D51), which the default Jev path does not ask today.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:6107:## D520 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the judge line stops at a note for Alex with nothing sent; the next step is a $0 trace of the old system's three agreed duplicate question pairs through their dated requests (council consensus, reflection rounds 10 and 11)\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:6132:## D521 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the duplicate-pair trace stands as corrected; the same-day candidates are traced against D375 at $0 (council consensus, reflection round 12)\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:6191:## D524 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the lean path is not closed; next a reviewed $0 design and deterministic proof of D375's per-article list (council consensus, reflection round 15)\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:6231:## D526 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the D375 proof's design is accepted for building once its recovery comparison is corrected; the unread lead does not come first (council consensus, reflection round 17)\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:6353:## D532 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the two filing decisions stand as traced, a common mechanism is not established; next a bounded $0 trace of the dependency lead under D368's direction (council consensus, reflection round 23)\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:6394:## D534 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency-channel assessment is corrected (its lines came from later conclusions); a $0 chronology and ordering proof comes before any measurement design (council consensus, reflection round 25)\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:6616:## D544 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the work toward production carries forward on the new system; the old system stays the running default until the new one passes its gates (council consensus; D543's deferral of the choice is withdrawn)\n7:piece is the same statement, `replace` when the incoming piece supersedes it, else `new`. Dependencies are not asked.\n269:            data['candidates'].append({'id': c['id'], 'outcome': fixed[0], 'target_view_id': fixed[1], 'dependencies': [],\n351:        data['candidates'].append({'id': c['id'], 'outcome': outcome, 'target_view_id': target or '', 'dependencies': [],\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md:37:| O, overlapping amounts added | \"derive the outcome … check it against the dependencies' conclusions (a part cannot exceed its whole …)\", applied only to dependencies, not to the judge's own sums | Reader brief and D260 (3): \"A part cannot exceed the whole it belongs to\"; \"the same claim … counts once\" | **Missing at its owner:** the identity is stated for dependencies, not for the judge's own arithmetic |\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md:423:- **H2 is not sent.** One account in 24 is too little, and \"this evidence\" could be read to leave out the dependencies and themes. It\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
