{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,120p' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/census/rejected-answers.md; rg -n 'without_previous|fullprev|hidden.*100|def gold' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py\"","aggregated_output":"# Rejected or split answers among worth-tracking questions, 5 draws (dev window 09-07..09-20)\n\n### 1. new-2 Q11 (C187) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=figure\nQ: How many U.S. states other than New York enact new data-center construction moratoriums or material restrictions during calendar 2026, as verified by state legislative records, enacted statutes and official governor announcements available by January 31, 2027? Count each state once if it enacts a statewide or geographically limited construction ban or moratorium, ends data-center construction tax breaks, requires operators to fund associated power-grid infrastructure, or introduces a binding local approval requirement for construction; exclude proposals that are not enacted.\nA: from 1 count to below 2 count\nReason: The reference is no reference figure: no market consensus or official target is supplied, and the ladder notes that no reference figure existed when the question opened. The forecast is one qualifying state beyond New York, which is rung 2. A dozen proposed bills, states considering similar measures, and Maine's vetoed bill raise the chance of another enactment, but proposals and a vetoed bill do not count. Texas's reported grid-connection freeze and local opposition add pressure, but the evidence does not identify an enacted, qualifying state construction restriction; a city-level proposal is also outside the state count. The evidence therefore does not make a second qualifying state more likely than not.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | Spreading state restrictions could constrain U.S. AI data-center buildout and NVIDIA demand. But the material shows no qualifying enactment beyond New York, and Maine's bill was vetoed. The reasons never justify one state over zero.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | State restrictions could materially constrain data-center capacity and NVIDIA’s growth. The evidence identifies no qualifying enactment outside New York; proposals and political pressure do not establish why one state is more likely than zero.\n- round-1/claude: unsupported | The reasons say no state beyond New York has enacted a qualifying restriction. Maine's ban was vetoed, and Texas and the other states have only proposals. That points to zero, yet the answer picks one state without justifying it.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | State restrictions could materially constrain data-center capacity and NVIDIA’s growth. No qualifying enactment beyond New York is identified, but proposals and political pressure do not establish whether zero or one state is the better forecast for the full year.\n- round-2/claude: unsupported | Keep. The reasons find no qualifying enactment outside New York; Maine was vetoed, the rest are proposals. They give no basis for one rather than zero, so the answer contradicts its own reasons rather than resting on thin material.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | State restrictions could materially constrain data-center capacity and NVIDIA’s growth. No qualifying enactment beyond New York is identified, while proposals and political pressure provide too little basis to prefer a one-state forecast over zero.\n\n### 2. new-2 Q15 (C197) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=figure\nQ: What total dollar principal of debt will Apollo, Blackstone, BlackRock, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR actually issue or arrange during calendar 2027 for NVIDIA customers under their joint AI-infrastructure financing initiative, secured by NVIDIA compute, as documented by special-purpose-entity bond and private-placement announcements, pricing disclosures, quarterly deal disclosures and financing deal-volume data, counting each transaction only once?\nA: from 25,000,000,000 USD to below 75,000,000,000 USD\nReason: There is no consensus or company guidance for calendar-2027 issuance, so there is no market-reference rung. NVIDIA's stated plan is to mobilize more than $500 billion of third-party capital over time, not a 2027 debt-issuance target. I estimate roughly $30–45 billion actually issued or arranged in 2027: the reported structure can support tens of billions per deal, and the $35 billion Broadcom compute-financing package is a scale comparator, but NVIDIA's own platforms remain preliminary and the $500 billion is not volume in hand. The estimate is above $25 billion and below $75 billion, so it remains on rung 2. The more formal disclosure that the MoUs may not lead to definitive agreements lowers the estimate versus the previous $35–50 billion range, without moving it across a rung boundary.\n- round-0/claude: supported | $500bn compute-backed financing, with up to $125bn NVIDIA residual-value support, materially affects demand and credit risk. Not a duplicate of earlier questions. Unsigned MoUs and no money raised, against a $35bn Broadcom comparator, justify rung 2 over rung 3.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | The financing initiative could materially affect NVIDIA’s demand and credit exposure. But preliminary MoUs, an untimed capital ambition and a Broadcom comparator do not establish 2027 issuance or justify choosing $25–75 billion over neighboring ranges.\n- round-1/claude: supported | Keep supported: deals in the works, SPVs issuing tens of billions each and the $35bn Broadcom comparator make under $25bn unlikely. Unsigned MoUs, no money raised and an untimed $500bn make $75bn+ in 2027 a stretch. Rung 2 is reasoned.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | The initiative could materially affect NVIDIA’s demand and credit exposure. Preliminary MoUs, an untimed capital ambition and a Broadcom comparator do not justify choosing $25–75 billion of 2027 issuance over the neighboring ranges, particularly below $25 billion.\n- round-2/claude: supported | Keep supported: qualifying deals reportedly in the works, SPVs issuing tens of billions each and the $35bn Broadcom comparator argue against under $25bn. Unsigned MoUs and no timeline for the $500bn argue against $75bn+. Rung 2 follows.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | The financing initiative could materially affect NVIDIA’s demand and credit exposure. Deals in the works and a $35 billion comparator establish potential scale, but provide no basis for estimating 2027 issuance or favoring $25–75 billion over below $25 billion.\n\n### 3. new-2 Q19 (C020) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\nQ: By January 31, 2027, will the United States reach a formal decision milestone toward extending export controls to remote access to advanced AI compute, evidenced by Senate passage or enactment of the Remote Access Security Act, or publication of a BIS rule controlling such remote access?\nA: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\nReason: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or official forecast for the deadline is supplied, and the stated legislative status is that RASA has passed the House but not the Senate. Reports of remote access and the White House official’s accusation increase the policy impetus, while the expert view that BIS could act quickly makes a rule possible; however, there is no concrete Senate schedule, announced BIS decision to proceed, or published rule. The stated implementation hurdles and potential industry pushback make an open outcome more likely than either a milestone or formal abandonment of both routes. Rung 2 also requires formal decisions against both routes, and none is reported.\n- round-0/claude: supported | Remote-access controls could cut Chinese use of offshore NVIDIA compute, a material revenue risk; distinct from the BIS diversion/enforcement question since RASA passage settles only this. No Senate schedule or rule reported, so rung 3 justified.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | Remote-access controls could materially affect demand for NVIDIA compute and are distinct from the earlier diversion probe. House passage, policy impetus and potential hurdles establish plausible paths, but do not justify choosing no milestone over action by the deadline.\n- round-1/claude: supported | RASA has stalled in the Senate for seven months, BIS has announced no rulemaking, and the window is short. Hurdles and pushback are named. Status quo rung 3 reasonably beats rung 1; a speculative 'could act quickly' isn't a signal.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | Remote-access controls could materially affect NVIDIA demand and are distinct from the earlier diversion question. House passage, policy pressure and possible hurdles establish competing paths, but do not justify choosing no milestone over action by the deadline.\n- round-2/claude: supported | Material shows RASA stalled in the Senate since January and no BIS rulemaking or schedule announced. 'Could act quickly' is hypothetical, with named hurdles. Status-quo rung 3 is the reasoned, consistent choice over rung 1; it is not unsupported guessing.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | Remote-access controls could materially affect NVIDIA demand and are distinct from the earlier diversion question. House passage, policy pressure, possible rapid BIS action and implementation hurdles establish competing paths, but do not justify choosing no milestone by the deadline.\n\n### 4. new-2 Q20 (C051) answer-mark=insufficient type=figure\nQ: What percentage of net new investment-grade bond issuance in calendar 2026 will AI-infrastructure borrowers represent, as reported in index-provider full-year new-issue composition statistics and issuance tallies?\nA: from 25 percent to below 35 percent\nReason: The market reference is “as much as 30%” in some investment-grade indexes, which is rung 3; it is not a full-market, full-year figure. The evidence still implies roughly 28% for the broader comparison, also rung 3: S&P's tally was about $225 billion through July, with up to $400 billion projected by year-end. Confirmed NVIDIA issuance supports ongoing supply, but its general-corporate-purpose use does not establish that the full amount is AI-infrastructure borrowing or justify adding it separately to S&P's broader tally. Higher yields and buyer cash-flow pressure are headwinds, but are not quantified as a revision to the projected share. Neither the implied figure nor the new evidence crosses a rung boundary.\n- round-0/claude: insufficient | AI-borrower share of IG issuance gauges credit funding for NVIDIA customers' capex. The only share datum is 'as much as 30%' in some indexes; the claimed 28% has no stated issuance denominator, and gross-versus-net definitions differ.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | AI financing capacity could materially affect NVIDIA demand, and this is distinct from the earlier financing initiative. The 30% figure covers only some indexes; S&P’s issuance totals provide no denominator supporting the claimed 28% full-year share.\n\n### 5. new-2 Q26 (C158) answer-mark=unsupported type=figure\nQ: What aggregate amount, in USD, of NVIDIA's residual value guaranties and related data-center lease credit-support obligations outstanding at FY2027 year-end will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K?\nA: from 110,000,000,000 USD to below 150,000,000,000 USD\nReason: The forecast is above rung 4's $110 billion floor and below rung 5's $150 billion floor. The $105 billion Ohio guaranty is within the disclosed $108.5 billion maximum gross guarantees, not an amount to add again; the Texas lease proxy lifts the estimate above $110 billion. Evidence that the Ohio support phases in beginning around FY2029, and the separate $200 billion third-party estimate for broader potential exposure by 2028, do not establish a larger amount for this narrower obligation at FY2027 year-end. The possible $125 billion of residual-value support is not a confirmed, sized obligation outstanding by then.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | A guaranty of over $100B is credit risk. Disclosed guarantees of $108.5B plus Nscale's $0.86B give about $109.4B, rung 3. Reaching rung 4 relies on adding the Texas lease, which NVIDIA holds as lessee, not as guarantor.\n- round-0/codex: unsupported | The guarantees are material and distinct from the earlier questions. The $108.5 billion disclosed maximum falls below $110 billion; the Texas lease’s $19.6 billion value is not established as an additional credit-support obligation and may overlap existing disclosures.\n\n### 6. new-2 Q27 (C157) answer-mark=insufficient type=yes/no\nQ: Will the Trump administration issue a formal action imposing new U.S. tariffs on semiconductors by January 31, 2027, as verified by a presidential proclamation or an official Federal Register or Commerce notice and the resulting tariff schedule?\nA: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\nReason: No market consensus or other external reference is supplied; the neutral reference is rung 3, Still open, because neither a formal tariff action nor a formal abandonment is established. The report that the administration was considering tariffs modestly raises the prospect of rung 1, but it gives no decision, schedule, or timing, and the White House official said that, unless officially announced, reporting about tariffs should be regarded as “baseless speculation.” That does not amount to a formal decision against the tariffs. The evidence therefore does not imply either rung 1 or rung 2 by the deadline.\n- round-0/claude: supported | Semiconductor tariffs could materially change NVIDIA's costs, margins and demand, and no earlier question covers them. The only material is an unconfirmed rumour that the White House dismissed as speculation, so the neutral 'neither' answer is justified.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | New semiconductor tariffs could materially affect NVIDIA’s costs and demand, and no earlier question tracks the same outcome. The rumor and official’s caveat do not provide enough evidence to favor no formal action or abandonment by the future deadline.\n- round-1/claude: supported | The only material is an unconfirmed report of tariffs being 'considered', which the White House called speculation. Nothing points to rung 1 or 2, so the status-quo 'neither' answer is justified rather than unanswerable.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | Semiconductor tariffs could materially affect NVIDIA, and no earlier question tracks the same outcome. An unconfirmed rumor and an official’s caveat do not establish whether action or abandonment will occur by the future deadline.\n- round-2/claude: insufficient | Changing my mark: the only material is an unconfirmed report that tariffs are being considered, plus an official's caveat. The reason admits nothing implies any rung and falls back to rung 3 by default. That is too thin to choose.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | Semiconductor tariffs could materially affect NVIDIA, and no earlier question tracks the same outcome. The rumor and official’s caveat do not justify choosing no action or abandonment by January 2027 over either alternative.\n\n### 7. new-2 Q30 (C012) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\nQ: Will NVIDIA complete its proposed equity investment in Thinking Machines Lab by March 4, 2028, as confirmed by NVIDIA's announcements or Form 10-K investment disclosures, or Thinking Machines Lab's confirmation of the closing?\nA: By March 4, 2028, NVIDIA or Thinking Machines Lab confirms that NVIDIA has completed the proposed equity investment, including through NVIDIA's Form 10-K investment disclosures.\nReason: The reference is the reported active discussion of an approximately $2.5 billion investment in a $5–6 billion round, which sits at rung 3 (Still open): there is no reported closing or formal abandonment. The specific amount, active fundraising and Accel's reported role make completion and confirmation by March 4, 2028 the most likely outcome, though the round has no reported commitment and the prior, higher-valuation round collapsed. That earlier round does not rule out this proposed investment, and no evidence establishes the investment's eventual stake or disclosed amount.\n- round-0/claude: supported | A $2.5B NVIDIA stake in a frontier lab is material and adds to circular AI-financing exposure; no earlier card covers it. Active talks with a named amount, a lead investor and an 18-month window reasonably favour completion over 'still open'.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | A potential $2.5 billion investment is material and distinct from earlier questions. Active talks and a named potential lead do not establish that closing is more likely than remaining open or abandonment, particularly after an earlier round collapsed.\n- round-1/claude: supported | Rung 2 needs formal abandonment, which rarely happens, so the real choice is closing versus still open. Named amount, active round, lead investor and 18 months favour closing; the collapsed earlier round was acknowledged.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | The proposed $2.5 billion investment is material and distinct from earlier questions. Uncommitted talks and a potential lead do not establish that completion is more likely than the other outcomes, particularly given the earlier collapsed round.\n- round-2/claude: supported | Two reports give a named amount, valuation and lead investor, enough to choose. Failed talks usually fade to rung 3, not rung 2. Still, 18 months makes closing likelier, and the collapsed round is weighed.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | The proposed $2.5 billion investment is material and distinct. Active talks and a potential lead do not establish that closing is more likely than remaining open; neither a commitment nor evidence about closing rates is supplied.\n\n### 8. new-2 Q31 (C160) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=figure\nQ: How many gigawatts of Australian AI data-center capacity using NVIDIA's DSX platform across Firmus, CDC, NEXTDC and AirTrunk will be deployed or covered by binding deployment contracts as of December 31, 2027, counting each project's capacity only once, according to the partners' capacity and deployment disclosures and capex filings?\nA: below 0.5 GW\nReason: The reference is NVIDIA's “up to 2 gigawatts” 2027 target, which sits in rung 3. The disclosed evidence does not establish that target as binding capacity for this question: the clearest quantified NVIDIA collaboration is IREN's 72 MW, while CDC's 800 MW under construction is a broader Australia-and-New Zealand portfolio and NVIDIA has not said it is all DSX capacity; IREN's 800 MW Bundey figure is a development-campus capacity, not an additional confirmed DSX commitment. I therefore forecast roughly 0.3 GW by the deadline, allowing for some further deployment from the announced programme but discounting its unallocated, unscheduled target. That remains below the 0.5 GW boundary for rung 2.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | A 2 GW, ~$80B buildout can move NVIDIA revenue. The answer anchors on IREN's 72 MW, which is outside the four named partners, ignores CDC's 800 MW under construction and Firmus's $2B raise, and drops a rung without new evidence.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | The potential buildout is material to NVIDIA, but the disclosures provide no quantified commitments or deployment schedules for the four named partners. IREN is outside the question’s scope, and the 0.3 GW forecast lacks support over neighboring ranges.\n- round-1/claude: unsupported | Not too thin: the 2 GW target and CDC's 800 MW under construction point to rung 2 or above. The answer anchors on IREN's out-of-scope 72 MW and drops a rung from identical reasoning without new evidence.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | The potential buildout is material to NVIDIA, but the disclosures do not quantify eligible capacity for the four named partners or support 0.3 GW over neighboring ranges. IREN is outside the question’s scope.\n- round-2/claude: unsupported | Material supports a choice: the 2 GW target, Huang's $80B plan and CDC's 800 MW under construction point to rung 2. The answer rests on out-of-scope IREN's 72 MW and drops a rung on unchanged evidence.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | The potential buildout is material, but neither the 2 GW upper target nor CDC’s broader portfolio establishes eligible capacity for the four named partners. The evidence does not justify 0.3 GW over neighboring ranges; IREN is outside scope.\n\n### 9. new-2 Q32 (C055) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=yes/no\nQ: Will the DOJ Antitrust Division publish a formal resolution of its review of Nvidia's Groq licensing transaction by January 31, 2027, as evidenced by a public DOJ statement or court filing?\nA: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\nReason: The reference is rung 3 (Still open): no market consensus or DOJ deadline for a public resolution is supplied. The investigation and formal information request make a resolution by the deadline plausible, but they provide no timetable; the evidence therefore still makes the review remaining open on January 31, 2027, more likely than a public conclusion by then. No evidence establishes a formal abandonment after September 10, 2026, but its absence is not itself evidence that rung 2 holds.\n- round-0/claude: supported | A DOJ challenge to a $17-20B Groq deal could force remedies affecting NVIDIA's inference roadmap, and no earlier question covers it. The investigation has no timetable, and DOJ often closes reviews quietly, so 'still open' fits.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | The antitrust review concerns a roughly $20 billion transaction and could materially affect NVIDIA. The evidence establishes an active inquiry but supplies no timeline or basis for judging a public resolution less likely than no resolution by January 31.\n- round-1/claude: supported | The material shows an active inquiry with a recent information request and no sign of imminent action. DOJ conduct reviews usually run long and often close without public statement, so 'still open' beats a public conclusion within 4.5 months.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | The review concerns a roughly $20 billion transaction. The material establishes an active inquiry but gives no basis for judging whether a public resolution by January is less likely than no qualifying public resolution.\n- round-2/claude: supported | The material shows an active, intensifying inquiry and a recent formal information demand, with no settlement or complaint signals. DOJ conduct probes typically run long and often close silently, so 'still open' rightly beats a published conclusion within 4.5 months.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | The roughly $20 billion transaction is material. The inquiry and information request establish no timetable or basis for comparing a public resolution with no qualifying resolution by January; the claimed typical DOJ timelines are not in the supplied material.\n\n### 10. new-2 Q33 (C040) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'supported']} type=figure\nQ: What percentage of China's AI-accelerator market will domestic Chinese vendors collectively hold in calendar 2026, as reported in IDC's full-year China AI-accelerator market-share release, or Canalys's equivalent release if IDC's is unavailable?\nA: from 50 percent to below 60 percent\nReason: The reference is roughly 40% domestic share, implied by IDC's 2025 figure of nearly 60% for international chipmakers; that sits at rung 3. I forecast about 52% for 2026, in rung 4: NVIDIA's no-China data-center-sales guidance and evidence of domestic systems already shipped support further substitution, while Huawei's admitted inability to meet domestic demand and possible renewed international supply constrain the upside. The new shipment and deployment evidence does not quantify domestic vendors' share of the full market, so it does not justify moving the estimate across another rung boundary.\n- round-0/claude: supported | Domestic share of China's accelerators directly sets NVIDIA's China revenue ceiling; no earlier card asks this. From an IDC-implied ~40% base, NVIDIA's zero-China guidance supports rung 4, and Huawei's capacity limits are weighed against it.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | China’s accelerator market is material to NVIDIA, and no earlier question settles the same outcome. The evidence supports domestic substitution, but does not quantify 2026 market shares sufficiently to distinguish 50–60% from adjacent ranges.\n- round-1/claude: supported | IDC gives a ~40% domestic base for 2025. NVIDIA's zero-China data-center guidance justifies moving up one rung, and Huawei's admitted capacity shortfall argues against 60%+. That is enough to pick rung 4 over its neighbours.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | China’s accelerator market is material to NVIDIA and distinct from earlier bets. The 2025 baseline and evidence of substitution support an increase, but do not establish that 2026 domestic share is more likely above 50% than in an adjacent range.\n- round-2/claude: supported | IDC puts 2025 domestic share near 40%. NVIDIA's zero-China data-center guidance, Biren's surge and the DeepSeek–Huawei deployment justify a roughly 10-point gain to rung 4. Huawei's capacity shortfall caps it below 60%.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | China’s accelerator market is material and distinct from earlier bets. The roughly 40% baseline and substitution evidence suggest an increase, but one quarter’s guidance and unquantified deployments do not establish that 50–60% is likelier than adjacent ranges.\n\n### 11. new-2 Q4 (C078) answer-mark={'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']} type=yes/no\nQ: Will the U.S. Commerce Department or BIS announce a new export-control restriction or enforcement action tied to the Deepseek-circumvention probe or third-country diversion of NVIDIA chips after July 2, 2026 and by NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end on January 31, 2027, as established by official rules or enforcement announcements?\nA: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\nReason: The market reference is rung 3 (Still open): no consensus, current Commerce/BIS plan, or deadline for a qualifying announcement is supplied. New evidence raises the chance of rung 1, especially the reported Commerce investigation, the White House accusation concerning Moonshot's access to chips in Thailand, and increasingly specific evidence of diversion. But the reported investigation is not an announcement of a new action explicitly tied to the DeepSeek probe or third-country diversion; the White House accusation and Taiwan prosecutions are not Commerce/BIS actions. No formal abandonment is shown either. The evidence therefore does not make either rung 1 or rung 2 the most likely outcome by the deadline.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | BIS action on diversion could cut NVIDIA's Southeast Asian/Taiwan-billed sales; no earlier duplicate. The answer treats 'no announcement yet' as favouring rung 3, despite active Commerce probes, Moonshot/Thailand accusations and named diverters over seven months.\n- round-0/codex: insufficient | New restrictions could materially affect NVIDIA’s revenue. The reported investigations and diversion cases establish enforcement risk, but provide too little basis to judge whether a qualifying U.S. announcement is more or less likely than no qualifying action by January 31.\n- round-1/claude: unsupported | Rung 1 is broad. The material shows active Commerce probes, Moonshot/Thailand accusations, Taiwan and US diversion cases, and named diverters. The answer reads 'nothing announced yet' as favouring rung 3 over a seven-month window, confusing current status with the outcome.\n- round-1/codex: insufficient | New restrictions could materially affect NVIDIA’s revenue. The investigations and diversion cases establish risk, but provide too little basis to judge whether a qualifying Commerce or BIS announcement is more likely than no qualifying action by the deadline.\n- round-2/claude: unsupported | Material is rich: formal Commerce probes, Moonshot/Thailand accusations, Megaspeed and a Thai firm named, and US/Taiwan diversion cases. Rung 3 rests only on 'nothing announced yet', treating current status as the outcome by January.\n- round-2/codex: insufficient | Export restrictions could materially affect NVIDIA. Active probes and diversion cases raise the chance of action, but provide too little basis to choose between a qualifying announcement and no announcement by the deadline.\n\n### 12. new-2 Q5 (C035) answer-mark=unsupported type=yes/no\nQ: Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture reach first commercial shipments in calendar 2027, as confirmed by NVIDIA shipment or production disclosures by December 31, 2027?\nA: After July 5, 2026, NVIDIA formally cancels Kyber NVL144 or decides to defer its first commercial shipments until after December 31, 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-07-05 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not.\nReason: The reference is NVIDIA's stated 2027 plan for Kyber, which sits at rung 1. The specific SemiAnalysis report that production has slipped to 2028 because of the PCB midplane bottleneck makes a post-July 5 deferral that remains in force at the due date the most likely outcome. NVIDIA's statements that its roadmap is intact, and its 2027 statements about Rubin Ultra/NVL576, do not specifically confirm Kyber NVL144 commercial shipments or clearly rebut the reported midplane issue. This is a forecast of the likely standing decision, not a claim that NVIDIA has formally confirmed a deferral.\n- round-0/claude: unsupported | Kyber timing drives Rubin Ultra revenue. Rung 2 needs a formal post-July-5 deferral; the reported delay predates that date, NVIDIA repeatedly denies it, and the reason admits none exists. That points to rung 3.\n129:def without_previous(user):\n149:        user = without_previous(user)\n160:    for suffix in ('-fullnoprev', '-fullprev', '-noprev'):\n226:    full = arm.endswith('-fullprev') or arm.endswith('-fullnoprev')\n251:def gold_entries():\n261:def gold_agrees(entry, question, rungs, rung):\n273:def goldcases():\n315:def goldprobe(ledger_path, arm, repeats):\n391:def goldscore(arm):\n524:def deck_full(arms=('v7-fullprev', 'v7-fullnoprev')):\n923:    Writes fullcases.json with both user texts and the hidden count; only cases with at most 100 hidden pieces are kept.\"\"\"\n939:        if hidden > 100:\n952:def fullscore(arms=('v7-fullprev', 'v7-fullnoprev')):\n974:            text = without_previous(case['user_full']) if a.endswith('-fullnoprev') else case['user_full']\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '3960,3982p;6107,6130p;6353,6427p;6428,6489p'; rg -n 'new system|revers|stop' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | rg 'D368|dependenc' | tail -35\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '3960,3982p;6107,6130p;6353,6427p;6428,6489p'; rg -n 'new system|revers|stop' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | rg 'D368|dependenc' | tail -35\"","aggregated_output":"  3960\t## D368 · 2026-11-05 · Alex's answers to the seven items left open by the consolidation\n  3961\t\n  3962\t- Alex, on the seven items at the head of `docs/DECISIONS.md` (D367): \"1. 这个你记住，我们要做 2. 做 3. 好那就按照后面的那条来 4. 好你来定 5. 你要来帮我看到底做不做\n  3963\t  6. 你看该怎么处理核实 7. 不，用英文吧。\"; then \"你开始逐条改，不要drift, 记录下来把1-7都清理了我们再commit push,开始快速做\".\n  3964\t- Decided:\n  3965\t  1. The asset-thesis rungs stated against guidance, each thesis naming its favourable end (D186), and the rename of `industry_aggregate`\n  3966\t     to `shared_quantity` (D191 point 4) are built in the next extraction version. The frozen v74i stays until then (D274); the new\n  3967\t     version is measured against it.\n  3968\t  2. The three decided rules not yet built are built, each designed and measured as its own slice (AGENTS §2, §7): a company's own\n  3969\t     quantity is a thesis under that company, private companies included (D187); a later item may add a dependency to an existing thesis\n  3970\t     (D191 point 2); the matching step chooses a new thesis's dependencies (D51), which the default Jev path does not ask today.\n  3971\t  3. At the due day pull mode closes the due questions and does nothing else (D356); D248 boundary 2's step that opens the next-deadline\n  3972\t     successor of an event question falling due unresolved is dropped.\n  3973\t  4. Settled by Claude on Alex's delegation, by D208's design: a seed question's dependency arrow means \"supports\". For filing, the\n  3974\t     supporting (driver) question is the narrower one, so a statement linked to both goes to it, as `extraction.narrowest_links` does\n  3975\t     today. For judging, the supported (root) question reads the supporting questions' conclusions, so drivers and themes are judged\n  3976\t     before their roots. Today the judging code reads the stored edge the other way: the driver reads the root and is judged after it\n  3977\t     (`seeding.seed_views` stores the seed's `depends_on` as the driver's dependency). The fix separates the two uses of the edge; it\n  3978\t     changes judgements, so it is measured before it enters the default.\n  3979\t  5. Settled by Claude on Alex's delegation: PRD 10 is corrected without changing any rule. §4 names the frozen standard's version 5 and\n  3980\t     §5 carries a status line (its links-rule version failed in §8 step 5; D361 keeps the fallback sentence).\n  3981\t  6. Settled by Claude on Alex's delegation: the three statements of the frozen extraction standard page that later decisions changed are\n  3982\t     noted in a dated correction record beside it, `docs/prd/05-extraction-standard-corrections.md`; the page is not edited (D261). Each\n  6107\t## D520 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the judge line stops at a note for Alex with nothing sent; the next step is a $0 trace of the old system's three agreed duplicate question pairs through their dated requests (council consensus, reflection rounds 10 and 11)\n  6108\t\n  6109\t- **The four accounts (D519), $0.0041:** in all four the frozen judge, given its stored answer back and asked what led to the band,\n  6110\t  said the band rested on an assumption it had introduced and that the evidence did not give; in the two cases where nothing sizes\n  6111\t  the amount it added that its own instructions forbade the band (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unsupported/ACCOUNT-RESULT.md`).\n  6112\t  The council (round 10) found a common lead and corrected Claude's reading of the previous-answer path: the one-fifth discount was\n  6113\t  new in the answer that used it; only the 25% growth (one day) and a 50% discount (five days) were repeated from a previous answer\n  6114\t  shown back, and that this causes the repeat is not established.\n  6115\t- **The design note (`unsupported/DESIGN-NOTE.md`), $0:** two questions traced day by day; a keyword count on stored answers (most\n  6116\t  answers the reviewers pass also state an assumption or an estimate: a warning, not a proof); every reader of the answer's\n  6117\t  fields; the multi-day path. Against the council's stop conditions: the next request shows back only the band and 800 characters\n  6118\t  of reasons, so a label has no path to the next day as the code stands, and what the product shows when nothing sizes an amount,\n  6119\t  or when an estimate uses an unsupported step, is an open product choice.\n  6120\t- **Decided, citing D439:** (1) nothing is sent and nothing changes in the judge; the note, corrected as the council asked (the\n  6121\t  band also moved on 2026-07-06; the options put evenly; **no recommendation among them**; no cost quoted for an unsized test),\n  6122\t  is left for Alex with the choice that is his; the decision page points to it and to the side-by-side. (2) **The next step, $0:**\n  6123\t  the three question pairs of the old system that both reviewing teams called duplicates in the fresh week (RTX Pro 5500\n  6124\t  approval, Vera Rubin shipping, fiscal 2028 revenue) are traced through their actual dated judging requests: repeated reports\n  6125\t  matched by the claim they convey; two independent reader families check whether a fact present for one answer was absent in\n  6126\t  substance from the other request and could bear on it; the answers and reasons are read for any concrete consequence. Different\n  6127\t  record numbers do not prove different facts (the council reproduced the link counts and noted repeats on both sides). **Stop:**\n  6128\t  if no distinct, answer-bearing fact is found missing from the relevant request, that is recorded as \"this reading found no\n  6129\t  delivery loss in these pairs\", not as \"duplicates cost only clutter\"; a candidate is reported with its uncertainty before any\n  6130\t  change is designed. D354's \"about one in five\" is one request re-sent ten times and is not taken as these pairs' cause.\n  6353\t## D532 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the two filing decisions stand as traced, a common mechanism is not established; next a bounded $0 trace of the dependency lead under D368's direction (council consensus, reflection round 23)\n  6354\t\n  6355\t- **The trace** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/missing-input/FILING-TRACE-RESULT.md`): in the new system the later-linking check,\n  6356\t  given the question and the whole statement, answered 0.12 for Broadcom's guidance and linked a neighbouring statement that\n  6357\t  names two buyers; in the old system all fourteen raw reading answers filed the next quarter's guidance under total revenue\n  6358\t  only, and no check removed a link.\n  6359\t- **The council's corrections:** the findings hold at the observed level; \"a rule working as written\" and \"mostly\" are withdrawn.\n  6360\t  The old system's link instruction also names indirect evidence (\"through the demand, supply, competition or pricing behind its\n  6361\t  answer; compare the specific questions before a company-wide revenue or margin question\", `placement.py`, D259), so whether\n  6362\t  total-revenue guidance belongs under the segment question is an unresolved judgement under the one-home rule; the new system's\n  6363\t  wording is consistent with its answer and not shown to have caused it. The trial's 19 and 4 are counts of undelivered\n  6364\t  reference facts, not of this cause.\n  6365\t- **A decision Claude had missed: D368 (Alex, 2026-11-05)** already directs that the matching step's choice of a new thesis's\n  6366\t  dependencies, which the default path does not ask, be built and measured as its own slice, and fixes the direction (the\n  6367\t  supported question reads its supporting questions' conclusions). The stored runs' empty dependencies are therefore known.\n  6368\t- **Decided, citing D439:** a bounded $0 trace, kept apart from PRD 10's filing work: the trial's 19 undelivered facts of the old\n  6369\t  system against the stored relationships between the questions concerned, the direction D368 gives, the dependency's\n  6370\t  conclusion as a judge would receive it (the conclusion and shortened reasons, not the statements) and the judging time;\n  6371\t  reported as valid under D368, figure present in that conclusion, or unavailable or ambiguous. **Stop** if no directionally\n  6372\t  valid case would put the needed figure in the receiving request, or the records cannot establish it. A positive count is a\n  6373\t  design lead for D368's slice; no model call, no build, no change to the frozen filing standard follows from it.\n  6374\t\n  6375\t## D533 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: this stretch of the lean investigation is closed and consolidated; the default and both systems stay; next a separate $0 assessment of D368's dependency channel on a stored, correctly directed case (council consensus, reflection round 24)\n  6376\t\n  6377\t- **The dependency trace** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/missing-input/DEPENDENCY-TRACE-RESULT.md`): no directionally valid\n  6378\t  case in which a dependency's conclusion would supply the needed figure, among the 22 reference facts the old system filed\n  6379\t  under another question (three with no question on their matter; the trial's 19 are the never-delivered subset). The council's\n  6380\t  wording governs: the one valid example is an unsized report, which may still have value; \"D368 is not a remedy\" is withdrawn\n  6381\t  (no judgement was re-run; some relations were unrecorded); D368's build stays decided.\n  6382\t- **Decided, citing D439:** (1) this stretch is closed and consolidated on the decision page (its section 7: what is still Alex's\n  6383\t  to choose; what he has decided and awaits implementation and acceptance, D375 and D368, not asked again; the defects with\n  6384\t  their evidence). (2) Production stays the default and both systems stay. (3) Waiting for Alex is not the next task: D439 lets\n  6385\t  the council decide a new version or a paid step, and a frozen piece or a cost of several dollars is not by itself a reason to\n  6386\t  wait; no such step is approved by this review. (4) **The next slice, $0, outside PRD 10's filing work:** on stored data,\n  6387\t  identify an existing supporting-question-to-supported-question pair under D368 and inspect the conclusion and shortened\n  6388\t  reasons that would reach the supported question at the relevant time (the Ohio example included as an unsized case): does\n  6389\t  the decided channel convey useful information in a real, correctly directed case. **Stop:** if no stored case establishes both\n  6390\t  the direction and what the receiving judge would see, the exercise is reported as unexercised and returned to the council\n  6391\t  before any measurement is designed; that absence does not cancel D368's build. A case found frames a separate design and its\n  6392\t  controls; no improved answer is inferred without a test.\n  6393\t\n  6394\t## D534 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency-channel assessment is corrected (its lines came from later conclusions); a $0 chronology and ordering proof comes before any measurement design (council consensus, reflection round 25)\n  6395\t\n  6396\t- **Claude's assessment** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-channel/CHANNEL-ASSESSMENT.md`): on the first frozen seeded\n  6397\t  draw, 14 conclusion lines built in D368's direction, 8 holding a figure absent from the receiving request.\n  6398\t- **The council's finding (checked in the store):** the supporting conclusions used were judged after the receiving question in the\n  6399\t  same day-end round (total revenue at judgement 198; its data-center supporter at 202) and had themselves read the receiver's\n  6400\t  conclusion: the lines are a counterfactual assembled from later outputs, and feeding them back would be a circle. The eight\n  6401\t  are edges into two receiving questions only. The stop condition of round 24 is not met as written; the count is a lead. The\n  6402\t  \"48 calls, ten cents\" estimate is withdrawn.\n  6403\t- **Decided, citing D439:** before any measurement design, at $0: (1) reconstruct the stored chronology for the two receiving\n  6404\t  questions (what existed before each call; what relied on the receiver; what the frozen reference and the sources say of the\n  6405\t  figures); (2) on a scratch copy, exercise the decided ordering and propagation with labelled fake judgement outputs\n  6406\t  (supporters first; changed conclusions queue the right dependants; no same-round circle; the edges' filing meaning unchanged;\n  6407\t  an article-opened question as a stated limit or a synthetic check); (3) stop if this cannot be done without changing a frozen\n  6408\t  piece, if a cycle or an unintended filing change appears, or if no source-backed, decision-relevant case is identified. D368\n  6409\t  directs both the corrected reading and the separate dependency selection; neither is accepted by the other's result.\n  6410\t\n  6411\t## D535 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the one-chain ordering proof of D368's direction is accepted with its limits; a source-backed case and a verified pre-judgement state of 2026-07-14 come before any measurement design (council consensus, reflection round 26)\n  6412\t\n  6413\t- **Done at $0** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-channel/CHANNEL-ASSESSMENT.md`): the stored chronology (every\n  6414\t  supporting conclusion that existed before a receiver's call had been produced with the receiver's earlier conclusion in view);\n  6415\t  the ordering and propagation proof (`ordering_proof.py`: the production judging round on a scratch copy, the edges read in\n  6416\t  D368's direction for the judging use only, labelled fake outputs; seven registered expectations hold; a mutant keeping the\n  6417\t  stored direction fails).\n  6418\t- **The council's review:** the proof stands as a bounded wiring result for one chain; it does not show the information free of\n  6419\t  circular influence (the other supporting conclusions shown are stored ones made under the old direction). Claude's statement\n  6420\t  that the frozen reference answers both questions from their own figures is wrong and corrected: it also uses data-center,\n  6421\t  shipment and Vera CPU evidence for total revenue, and its total-revenue view with a segment-share inference for data-center\n  6422\t  revenue.\n  6423\t- **Decided, citing D439:** before the council reviews any measurement design, at $0: (a) read the figures of the candidate\n  6424\t  supporting lines against their source passages and the reference, stated figures apart from a judge's calculations and\n  6425\t  assumptions, and identify at least one source-backed, decision-relevant case; (b) establish whether the state of 2026-07-14\n  6426\t  before that day's judging round can be rebuilt and verified against the stored run, the retired preset questions accounted\n  6427\t  for; if not, stop and return the mismatch. A measurement would run the old and the corrected direction from copies of that\n  6428\t  one state (a one-day switch diagnostic of the corrected reading, not acceptance for today's default or for dependency\n  6429\t  selection), with harm, benefit and inconclusive registered before any send and its rule sized and calibrated before a pass\n  6430\t  line is named. An extra round after the window is at most a separately labelled exploration.\n  6431\t\n  6432\t## D536 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: no measurement of the reversed reading direction is sent now; next, at $0, size where dependency selection could act in the stored unseeded fresh week (council consensus, reflection round 27)\n  6433\t\n  6434\t- **Done at $0 after D535** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-channel/CHANNEL-ASSESSMENT.md`): the seeded run's state\n  6435\t  of 2026-07-14 before its judging round rebuilt from stored answers and verified table by table, the stored-direction round\n  6436\t  re-run from it identical to the stored one; the whole round dry in D368's direction with labelled fakes (14 to 18 calls; seven\n  6437\t  registered expectations hold; a mutant fails); the reference's ten facts for data-center revenue traced through the stored\n  6438\t  requests; the three stored seeded draws scored on the frozen reference as they are (15, 15, 16 of 17 agree; total revenue and\n  6439\t  data-center revenue in all three); judgements shown not to feed back into reading or matching requests.\n  6440\t- **Claude's errors, recorded:** the first source-backed case was the wrong pick; a lookup by wording matched statement ids; the\n  6441\t  table counted statements only, so \"four facts would be brought\" and \"the only sized reference is removed\" were too strong (the\n  6442\t  council's correction: the consensus, the guidance and the H200 permission already arrive through the total-revenue line or the\n  6443\t  theme block); and the earlier record of 2026-11-03 and 04 (`docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/core-problem.md`,\n  6444\t  `review-core-codex.md`) had not been searched before D533, nor D368 item 4 read whole: it was settled by Claude on Alex's\n  6445\t  delegation (\"好你来定\"), not in Alex's own words.\n  6446\t- **Decided, citing D439:** neither the one-day switch nor a 14-day judging-only comparison is sent now. D368 item 4 stays a\n  6447\t  recorded decision awaiting build and measurement; these results do not establish that a uniform reversal helps, and no other\n  6448\t  design replaces it here (a design that changes how a part and its whole read each other returns to the council). The harness\n  6449\t  is kept as that slice's acceptance material. **Next, $0:** in the stored unseeded fresh-week run of the old system, for each\n  6450\t  question opened, identify the questions and same-article candidates the matching step was allowed to consider; find candidate\n  6451\t  supporting relations among them, negative cases included; read their sources and the conclusions existing at that time; count\n  6452\t  which could carry a useful, source-backed conclusion in the permitted direction and which would repeat the theme block, need\n  6453\t  a question not yet open, or risk a cycle. A design census, not a claim about what the matching step would choose. Stop, and\n  6454\t  return to the council, if the openings yield no identifiable positive case and negative control, or if the relation cannot be\n  6455\t  stated without changing D368's contract. No send or build follows automatically.\n  6456\t\n  6457\t## D537 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: the dependency-selection census has one clear case and negative controls; before any send, the edge's effect on filing and judging is proved on stored data (council consensus, reflection round 28)\n  6458\t\n  6459\t- **The census** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/DEPENDENCY-SELECTION-CENSUS.md`): over the fifteen\n  6460\t  questions the old system opened in the stored unseeded fresh week, 97 pairs on which a dependency could have been chosen (each\n  6461\t  new question against the questions it was compared with and its same-article siblings). Claude's own read written first, then\n  6462\t  two blind readers of different model families (the same mark on 68 of 97; both mark \"the new question would want the earlier\n  6463\t  one's conclusion\" on 11, three of them strong). One clear case: data-center revenue FY2027 against total revenue FY2027 (the\n  6464\t  matching engine recorded \"part of\", 1.00): the total's conclusion carried the year's consensus ($406.05 billion from 09-23,\n  6465\t  about $412 billion from 09-25) and the $108 billion guidance (from 09-26), none of them anywhere in the data-center question's\n  6466\t  seven requests; its last answer names the gap and adds a step of its own. Negative controls: the Hugging Face commitments\n  6467\t  question (\"none\" in all three readings); a weakly granted pair whose line would carry a judge's own assumed numbers. The\n  6468\t  strongest driver relations of the week (Rubin shipping against the revenue questions) run from a later question to an earlier\n  6469\t  one and cannot be chosen at creation (D191 point 2, decided, unbuilt).\n  6470\t- **The council's corrections:** the $96.2 billion quarter was already in all seven requests (not a figure the line would\n  6471\t  supply); the one \"same question\" pair is one unordered pair; the line would not by itself make the answer supported.\n  6472\t- **Decided, citing D439:** the pair is a positive lead for the decided dependency-selection slice (D368 item 2), not a\n  6473\t  validation of it. Before the council reviews any six-call send on it: a bounded proof on stored data of what the edge would do\n  6474\t  to filing and to judging (a statement linked to both questions, the direction, ordering, cycle rejection, what the judge's\n  6475\t  request would contain, the unrelated pair as a negative case), with no frozen file changed. Stop, and return the conflict to\n  6476\t  the council as a design question, if the edge makes filing discard a home contrary to the governing rule, creates a cycle,\n  6477\t  cannot be represented without conflating the two meanings of the edge, or fails to show the intended conclusion.\n  6478\t\n  6479\t## D538 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: one dependency edge shows the intended conclusion to the judge and removes seven filing links; the dependency slice's design is opened at $0, beginning with the separation of the edge's two uses (council consensus, reflection round 29)\n  6480\t\n  6481\t- **The proof** (`docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/dependency-selection/EDGE-PROOF-RESULT.md`, `edge_proof.py`, $0): on scratch\n  6482\t  copies of the stored unseeded fresh week, the edge \"data-center revenue FY2027 depends on total revenue FY2027\" written by the\n  6483\t  writer's own steps. Judging, conditional on the rest of the week as stored: thirteen registered expectations hold (the reverse\n  6484\t  edge refused as a cycle; total revenue judged first; only the data-center request changes, by the dependency block holding\n  6485\t  total revenue's conclusion of the round cut at 600 characters and the closing sentence; the judge's check accepts the\n  6486\t  citation; every other judgement equal to the stored one); two mutants fail. Filing, every stored reading filed again by the\n  6487\t  production entry point (the control reproduces all 235 articles): through the narrowest rule in code (D265) the same edge\n  6488\t  removes the total-revenue link of seven statements: the company-wide outlook excluding China, which a total-revenue judgement\n  6489\t  cites and no other statement of it holds; three statements of the quarter's total and segment revenue in one sentence; three\n2608:- D201 measured further ([results](research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/results.md), 2026-10-28 00:00–01:20, $0.85): a fresh 16-case gold written from the complete expansions before any send (action 11/12 and 4/4 held out; nothing wrongly retired 16/16; same-claim 8/12 with one class: a second outlet's own reporting treated as independent, which the contract could not express); arm 2 split claim identity from source independence (the class is gone; two new judgement weaknesses appeared); three draws per disagreeing case under both prompts show the arm-to-arm differences are within one draw's noise, while in 144 draws no must-not-touch piece was ever marked same or revised. Decided: the contract keeps arm 2's split; prompt arms stop here; the variable fields are annotations under D202 and the next build is the argument record (steps 5 and 6), where a wrong relation in one draw is corrected by the next instead of being frozen into the ledger.\n3099:- Measured (three draws, finished 21:27; `e2e-funnel-r7-v94.txt`; draw 3 stopped once on 07-05 when the judge named the H200 theme as a dependency of the China-share question twice and the check refused it, a judge-and-check mismatch not seen in the nine earlier draws, resumed from 07-04). Regressions first, v92 → v94: core not extracted 6.7 → 8.0, supporting dropped for no home 12.0 → 14.3, both inside the draw noise; core reaching 73.7 → 74.3 and supporting reaching 90.0 → 89.7, flat against predictions of +2 and +10 (the v73 boundary examples recover about two supporting matters a draw on the whole bench, against six a send on the 17 proof articles). Held: noise reaching 56.9 → 45.0 (−21%), the three revenue questions' share of links 61% → 45% with no statement under both total and Data Center revenue, quote refusals 16/11/14 → 6/10/8, judgement agreement 15/18, 16/17, 17/18 → 16/18, 16/19, 16/18, judge requests 5.1K → 4.2K tokens on average, cost $0.88 → $0.80 a draw, must_open 28/28 and may_open 40/42 unchanged, zero re-sent requests. Verdict: cleaner and cheaper, not worse on the desk's counts; whether poc-v94 becomes the default is Alex's call. Open defect recorded: the judge may name a shown theme as a dependency of an asset question (D263's consistency clause) and the check refuses it; twice in one round fails the day (resumable).\n6432:## D536 · 2026-11-15 · Under D439: no measurement of the reversed reading direction is sent now; next, at $0, size where dependency selection could act in the stored unseeded fresh week (council consensus, reflection round 27)\n6494:  the need to an article-created dependency. It decides no representation and does not authorize reversing D368's direction.\n7250:- **Limits recorded:** the dependency case is not exercised (the new system writes no dependencies, open defect 4); the replay\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nimport json,pathlib,collections\np=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20\\\")\ncs=json.loads((p/\\\"fullcases.json\\\").read_text())\nprint(cs[0])\ncs=[x for x in cs if 1<=x[\\\"hidden\\\"]<=100]\nprint(len(cs), collections.Counter(x[\\\"role\\\"] for x in cs))\nfor a in (\\\"v7-fullprev\\\",\\\"v7-fullnoprev\\\"):\n hits=[]\n for x in cs:\n  ys=[json.loads((p/\\\"probe\\\"/a/(x[\\\"case\\\"]+f\\\"-{i}.json\\\")).read_text()) for i in (1,2,3)]\n  source=ys[0][\\\"case\\\"][\\\"rung\\\"]\n  rs=[y[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"rung\\\"] for y in ys]\n  if sum(r\"'!=source for r in rs)>=2: hits.append((x[\"case\"],source,rs))\n print(a,hits)\nfor a in (\"v7\",\"J2a\",\"J2b\",\"v7-noprev\"):\n for f in sorted((p/\"goldprobe\"/a).glob(\"*q17*\")):\n  if \"d3-q17\" in f.name: print(a,f.name,json.loads(f.read_text())[\"answer\"][\"rung\"])\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nimport json,pathlib,collections\np=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20\\\")\ncs=json.loads((p/\\\"fullcases.json\\\").read_text())\nprint(cs[0])\ncs=[x for x in cs if 1<=x[\\\"hidden\\\"]<=100]\nprint(len(cs), collections.Counter(x[\\\"role\\\"] for x in cs))\nfor a in (\\\"v7-fullprev\\\",\\\"v7-fullnoprev\\\"):\n hits=[]\n for x in cs:\n  ys=[json.loads((p/\\\"probe\\\"/a/(x[\\\"case\\\"]+f\\\"-{i}.json\\\")).read_text()) for i in (1,2,3)]\n  source=ys[0][\\\"case\\\"][\\\"rung\\\"]\n  rs=[y[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"rung\\\"] for y in ys]\n  if sum(r\"'!=source for r in rs)>=2: hits.append((x[\"case\"],source,rs))\n print(a,hits)\nfor a in (\"v7\",\"J2a\",\"J2b\",\"v7-noprev\"):\n for f in sorted((p/\"goldprobe\"/a).glob(\"*q17*\")):\n  if \"d3-q17\" in f.name: print(a,f.name,json.loads(f.read_text())[\"answer\"][\"rung\"])\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"{'case': 'T-new-2-Q11', 'role': 'target', 'draw': 'new-2', 'label': 'Q11', 'view_id': 'v18', 'type': 'figure', 'class': 'E', 'card': 'C187', 'mark': {'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']}, 'hidden': 0, 'shown': 17, 'user_clipped': 'Thesis v18 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"The number and scope of U.S. states enacting new data-center construction moratoriums or material restrictions (beyond New York\\'s one-year moratorium) in calendar 2026.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many U.S. states other than New York enact new data-center construction moratoriums or material restrictions during calendar 2026, as verified by state legislative records, enacted statutes and official governor announcements available by January 31, 2027? Count each state once if it enacts a statewide or geographically limited construction ban or moratorium, ends data-center construction tax breaks, requires operators to fund associated power-grid infrastructure, or introduces a binding local approval requirement for construction; exclude proposals that are not enacted.\\nLadder: rung 1 below 1 count: below 1 count / rung 2 from 1 count to below 2 count: from 1 count to below 2 count / rung 3 from 2 count to below 4 count: from 2 count to below 4 count (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 4 count to below 7 count: from 4 count to below 7 count / rung 5 7 count or more: 7 count or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 17 items]\\n- e1180 [fact; Al Jazeera (Andy Hirschfeld); original source New York state ; from gnews:abf7551608c81d3cf4fe39b7 2026-07-27] New York became the first U.S. state to officially impose a one-year moratorium on new data-center construction.\\n    Quote: New York last week became the first US state to officially put a one-year moratorium on new data centre construction.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1181 [fact; Al Jazeera (Andy Hirschfeld); original source Various U.S. states (unnamed in aggregate) ; from gnews:abf7551608c81d3cf4fe39b7 2026-07-27] At least a dozen other U.S. states have proposed legislation similar to New York\\'s restriction on new data-center construction, though New York\\'s legislation went the furthest.\\n    Quote: At least a dozen other states have proposed similar legislation although New York’s legislation went the furthest.\\n- e1182 [party_statement; Al Jazeera (Andy Hirschfeld); original source Greg Abbott; James Talarico ; from gnews:abf7551608c81d3cf4fe39b7 2026-07-27] In Texas, Republican Governor Greg Abbott called in late June for a ban on data-center construction in rural areas, and Democratic congressman James Talarico — in a tight U.S. Senate race — unveiled a late-last-week proposal to curb data-center construction, end construction tax breaks, require operators to pay for the power grid and give local communities more say on siting.\\n    Quote: In Texas, Republican Governor Greg Abbott called in late June for a ban on data centre construction in rural areas. On the other side of the aisle, James Talarico, a state Democratic congressman who is in a tight race with Republican Ken Paxton for a US Senate seat in what has historically been a reliably conservative state, unveiled a proposal late last week to curb data centre construction. … Th\\n- e64 [rumour; Jowi Morales, Tom\\'s Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom\\'s Hardware reported, without naming a source, that 7 out of 10 Americans say they do not want a data center in their backyard and that this resistance led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026.\\n    Quote: These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in their backyard . This resistance has led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 projects in just the first quarter of 2026.\\n- e166 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com (article), citing Tom\\'s Hardware; original source Tom\\'s Hardware (running tally, as cited) ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] According to a running tally at Tom\\'s Hardware cited by the article, more than 75 data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed across the US so far in 2026, and polling covered in the same reporting found 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center built anywhere near their home.\\n    Quote: With over $130 billion in data center projects already blocked or delayed by communities fed up with power and water demands, a zero-local-water nuclear option isn\\'t just an engineering story — it\\'s a potential unlock for the entire AI buildout. … More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3438; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e840 [fact; FOX 4 News (Alex Boyer); original source Fort Worth City Council members / City of Fort Worth ; from gnews:8ba10884932cdb1bcd660bcb 2026-07-21] Earlier in July 2026, five Fort Worth City Council members signed a letter calling for a temporary moratorium on data center construction, and the Fort Worth City Council is scheduled to vote on Aug. 11, 2026 on whether to adopt a data center moratorium that would give the city a longer window to draft guidelines future data centers would have to follow. The item reports the pushback as aimed at data centers, which the city manager says is a different land use from NVIDIA\\'s new factory.\\n    Quote: Earlier in July, five Fort Worth City Council members signed a letter calling for a temporary moratorium on data center construction. … On Aug. 11, Fort Worth City Council will vote on whether to adopt the data center moratorium, which would give the city a longer window to draft guidelines that future data centers in the city would have to abide by. … Fort Worth City Manager Jay Chapa tells FOX 4\\n- e1255 [fact; PYMNTS, citing a Foley & Lardner analysis; original source New York state; Maine legislature/Governor Janet Mills; state legislatures of Minnesota, Michigan, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, New Hampshire and Virginia (compiled by Foley & Lardner) ; from gnews:1156febd573b9bcaf066f322 2026-07-27] New York placed a one-year moratorium on construction of new data centers earlier in July 2026, the first state-wide ban of its kind; Maine\\'s legislature approved a state-wide ban but Governor Janet Mills vetoed it, and Minnesota, Michigan, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, New Hampshire and Virginia are all currently considering similar legislation, according to an analysis by law firm Foley & Lardner. The analysis said that if additional states follow suit, New York\\'s decision could become the beginning of a broader regulatory trend rather than an isolated event.\\n    Quote: Earlier this month, New York placed a one-year moratorium on construction of new data centers, the first state-wide ban of its kind. Maine’s legislature had approved a state-wide ban, bit it was vetoed by Gov. Janet Mills. Minnesota, Michigan, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, New Hampshire and Virginia are all currently considering similar legislation, according to an analysis from law firm Foley & L\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1180; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2310 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:75707928fe6c883bcd724702 2026-08-19] The article reports, citing The Information, that data-center bans have hit more than 500 locations, presented as one of the siting/permitting risks that could block AI data-center projects and thereby the electricity-consuming infrastructure the buildout needs.\\n    Quote: So is the chance that laws and ordinances could block these projects. The Information says data center bans have hit over 500 locations.\\n- e2920 [party_statement; Benzinga, quoting Jensen Huang; original source NVIDIA Corp. (CEO Jensen Huang) 2026-08-26; from gnews:0f6c59de72ffa6b9a2036962 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said in the company\\'s earnings announcement that actual demand for NVIDIA\\'s chips is far greater than 70%, but that limited memory, power and data-center capacity are holding growth to that pace for now — attributing the constraint to supply-side bottlenecks rather than demand.\\n    Quote: Huang said actual demand for Nvidia\\'s chips is far greater than 70%, but limited memory, power, and data center capacity are holding growth to that pace for now.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2899; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e3438 [third_party_view; TechRadar Pro (Benedict Collins); original source  ; from gnews:295caa6e12f0b739d2e14ea6 2026-08-29] Public opposition to data centers has led many U.S. state representatives to impose moratoriums on data-center construction or introduce legislation making it harder to build them, and $130 billion of AI data-center projects were cancelled or delayed in Q1 of calendar 2026 because of public opposition, TechRadar Pro reported on 2026-08-29 (no source for the figure given).\\n    Quote: So much so that many state representatives are implementing moratoriums on data centers, or introducing legislation to make it harder for data centers to be built. In Q1 of this year, $130 billion worth of AI data center projects were cancelled or delayed due to public opposition.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e166; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e3439 [party_statement; TechRadar Pro (Benedict Collins); original source Greg Abbott, Governor of Texas ; from gnews:295caa6e12f0b739d2e14ea6 2026-08-29] Texas Governor Greg Abbott, who TechRadar Pro says was once an advocate for data-center development in Texas, recently criticized data-center developers over their public image, saying they \\'got the backlash they deserve\\'.\\n    Quote: Texas Gov. Greg Abbott - who was once an advocate for data center development in Texas - recently criticized data center developers for their public image, stating they “got the backlash they deserve.”\\n- e3440 [third_party_view; TechRadar Pro (Benedict Collins); original source  ; from gnews:295caa6e12f0b739d2e14ea6 2026-08-29] Data centers have become a key issue in the 2026 U.S. midterm elections, which have drawn a record $646 million in donations from corporate entities, TechRadar Pro reported on 2026-08-29.\\n    Quote: Data centers have now become a key issue of the 2026 midterm elections, which has seen a record $646 million in donations from corporate entities.\\n- e3478 [third_party_view; Jake Conley, Yahoo Finance; original source Ellen Hazen, F.L.Putnam Investment Management ; from gnews:9f03bbc66269c49a5ec8afee 2026-08-30] Ellen Hazen, chief market strategist and portfolio manager at F.L.Putnam Investment Management, said the biggest risk is whether the trillion dollars of hyperscaler capex next year can actually be spent, citing labor shortages, permitting delays, rising NIMBY opposition and component shortages; if the money cannot be spent, she expects a second-derivative slowdown.\\n    Quote: \"The biggest risk is whether or not the trillion dollars in hyperscaler capex next year can actually be spent — we have labor shortages, we have permitting delays, we have NIMBY increasing in volume, and we have component shortages,\" Hazen said. … \"If we don\\'t get the ability to spend all of that money, then the second derivative slowing,\" Hazen added. \"That\\'s one of the things that I\\'m concerned \\n- e3714 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Texas state/grid officials and unnamed U.S. utilities ; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit data-center developers, and utilities in other U.S. markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed data-center sites are actually controlled, according to GuruFocus\\'s 2026-09-01 report. The item gives no number of affected projects, megawatts or a duration for the freeze.\\n    Quote: Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers. Utilities in other markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed sites are actually controlled.\\n- e3715 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com 2026-09-01; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Electricity interconnection requests across parts of the United States now exceed 700 gigawatts, more than ten times estimated current data-center electricity consumption, per GuruFocus\\'s 2026-09-01 report. The figure is not attributed to a named grid operator, regulator or data provider.\\n    Quote: Electricity requests across parts of the United States now exceed 700 gigawattsmore than ten times estimated current data-center consumption.\\n- e4717 [party_statement; CNBC, citing a video posted on social media by an All-In Summit attendee; original source Donald Trump (President of the United States) 2026-09-14; from gnews:4c28f13be664d1332e4f8857 2026-09-14] In a phone call put on speaker at the All-In Summit in Los Angeles on Monday 2026-09-14 while NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang was on stage, U.S. President Donald Trump said opposition to AI data-center construction is \"all a hoax,\" that \"the data centers are great, and they make people wealthy, and they make states wealthy,\" and that opponents are \"playing right into the hands of a lot of people that don\\'t want to see it happen,\" adding it could be coming from China and that \"We\\'re not going to let that happen.\" Huang replied, \"We\\'re not going to let that happen, sir.\" The remarks are a presidential endorsement of data-center construction, reported from a video posted by a summit attendee; no policy action or decision was announced.\\n    Quote: With Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang speaking on stage at the All-in Summit in Los Angeles, Trump called the tech exec, and was then put on speaker.\\n\"I\\'m telling you, it\\'s all a hoax,\" Trump said, according to a video posted on social media by an attendee. \"The data centers are great, and they make people wealthy, and they make states wealthy.\" … Those opposing data center construction are \"playing right \\n- e5051 [third_party_view; qz.com (Quartz), citing Fortune; original source Data Center Watch (project-halt data); unnamed survey for the 70% figure ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Roughly 70% of Americans would object to a data center being built in their community, citing strain on local power resources and higher utility bills, and community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\\n    Quote: The announcement comes as AI data centers have faced mounting public and political opposition. Roughly 70% of Americans would object to a data center being built in their community , citing strain on local power resources and higher utility bills as their leading concerns. Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is not a supplied consensus, guidance figure, or official target, so it has no rung; rung 3 is the 2-to-below-4 bucket, not a reference. The forecast remains one qualifying state beyond New York, which is rung 2: the proposal pipeline and political pressure raise the chance of further action, but the supplied items do not report another qualifying enactment, and proposals, local actions, or grid constraints alone do not count. The evidence does not imply the two states needed to cross into rung 3.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.', 'user_full': 'Thesis v18 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"The number and scope of U.S. states enacting new data-center construction moratoriums or material restrictions (beyond New York\\'s one-year moratorium) in calendar 2026.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many U.S. states other than New York enact new data-center construction moratoriums or material restrictions during calendar 2026, as verified by state legislative records, enacted statutes and official governor announcements available by January 31, 2027? Count each state once if it enacts a statewide or geographically limited construction ban or moratorium, ends data-center construction tax breaks, requires operators to fund associated power-grid infrastructure, or introduces a binding local approval requirement for construction; exclude proposals that are not enacted.\\nLadder: rung 1 below 1 count: below 1 count / rung 2 from 1 count to below 2 count: from 1 count to below 2 count / rung 3 from 2 count to below 4 count: from 2 count to below 4 count (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 4 count to below 7 count: from 4 count to below 7 count / rung 5 7 count or more: 7 count or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 17 items]\\n- e1180 [fact; Al Jazeera (Andy Hirschfeld); original source New York state ; from gnews:abf7551608c81d3cf4fe39b7 2026-07-27] New York became the first U.S. state to officially impose a one-year moratorium on new data-center construction.\\n    Quote: New York last week became the first US state to officially put a one-year moratorium on new data centre construction.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1181 [fact; Al Jazeera (Andy Hirschfeld); original source Various U.S. states (unnamed in aggregate) ; from gnews:abf7551608c81d3cf4fe39b7 2026-07-27] At least a dozen other U.S. states have proposed legislation similar to New York\\'s restriction on new data-center construction, though New York\\'s legislation went the furthest.\\n    Quote: At least a dozen other states have proposed similar legislation although New York’s legislation went the furthest.\\n- e1182 [party_statement; Al Jazeera (Andy Hirschfeld); original source Greg Abbott; James Talarico ; from gnews:abf7551608c81d3cf4fe39b7 2026-07-27] In Texas, Republican Governor Greg Abbott called in late June for a ban on data-center construction in rural areas, and Democratic congressman James Talarico — in a tight U.S. Senate race — unveiled a late-last-week proposal to curb data-center construction, end construction tax breaks, require operators to pay for the power grid and give local communities more say on siting.\\n    Quote: In Texas, Republican Governor Greg Abbott called in late June for a ban on data centre construction in rural areas. On the other side of the aisle, James Talarico, a state Democratic congressman who is in a tight race with Republican Ken Paxton for a US Senate seat in what has historically been a reliably conservative state, unveiled a proposal late last week to curb data centre construction. … Th\\n- e64 [rumour; Jowi Morales, Tom\\'s Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom\\'s Hardware reported, without naming a source, that 7 out of 10 Americans say they do not want a data center in their backyard and that this resistance led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026.\\n    Quote: These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in their backyard . This resistance has led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 projects in just the first quarter of 2026.\\n- e166 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com (article), citing Tom\\'s Hardware; original source Tom\\'s Hardware (running tally, as cited) ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] According to a running tally at Tom\\'s Hardware cited by the article, more than 75 data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed across the US so far in 2026, and polling covered in the same reporting found 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center built anywhere near their home.\\n    Quote: With over $130 billion in data center projects already blocked or delayed by communities fed up with power and water demands, a zero-local-water nuclear option isn\\'t just an engineering story — it\\'s a potential unlock for the entire AI buildout. … More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3438; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e840 [fact; FOX 4 News (Alex Boyer); original source Fort Worth City Council members / City of Fort Worth ; from gnews:8ba10884932cdb1bcd660bcb 2026-07-21] Earlier in July 2026, five Fort Worth City Council members signed a letter calling for a temporary moratorium on data center construction, and the Fort Worth City Council is scheduled to vote on Aug. 11, 2026 on whether to adopt a data center moratorium that would give the city a longer window to draft guidelines future data centers would have to follow. The item reports the pushback as aimed at data centers, which the city manager says is a different land use from NVIDIA\\'s new factory.\\n    Quote: Earlier in July, five Fort Worth City Council members signed a letter calling for a temporary moratorium on data center construction. … On Aug. 11, Fort Worth City Council will vote on whether to adopt the data center moratorium, which would give the city a longer window to draft guidelines that future data centers in the city would have to abide by. … Fort Worth City Manager Jay Chapa tells FOX 4\\n- e1255 [fact; PYMNTS, citing a Foley & Lardner analysis; original source New York state; Maine legislature/Governor Janet Mills; state legislatures of Minnesota, Michigan, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, New Hampshire and Virginia (compiled by Foley & Lardner) ; from gnews:1156febd573b9bcaf066f322 2026-07-27] New York placed a one-year moratorium on construction of new data centers earlier in July 2026, the first state-wide ban of its kind; Maine\\'s legislature approved a state-wide ban but Governor Janet Mills vetoed it, and Minnesota, Michigan, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, New Hampshire and Virginia are all currently considering similar legislation, according to an analysis by law firm Foley & Lardner. The analysis said that if additional states follow suit, New York\\'s decision could become the beginning of a broader regulatory trend rather than an isolated event.\\n    Quote: Earlier this month, New York placed a one-year moratorium on construction of new data centers, the first state-wide ban of its kind. Maine’s legislature had approved a state-wide ban, bit it was vetoed by Gov. Janet Mills. Minnesota, Michigan, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, New Hampshire and Virginia are all currently considering similar legislation, according to an analysis from law firm Foley & L\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1180; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2310 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:75707928fe6c883bcd724702 2026-08-19] The article reports, citing The Information, that data-center bans have hit more than 500 locations, presented as one of the siting/permitting risks that could block AI data-center projects and thereby the electricity-consuming infrastructure the buildout needs.\\n    Quote: So is the chance that laws and ordinances could block these projects. The Information says data center bans have hit over 500 locations.\\n- e2920 [party_statement; Benzinga, quoting Jensen Huang; original source NVIDIA Corp. (CEO Jensen Huang) 2026-08-26; from gnews:0f6c59de72ffa6b9a2036962 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said in the company\\'s earnings announcement that actual demand for NVIDIA\\'s chips is far greater than 70%, but that limited memory, power and data-center capacity are holding growth to that pace for now — attributing the constraint to supply-side bottlenecks rather than demand.\\n    Quote: Huang said actual demand for Nvidia\\'s chips is far greater than 70%, but limited memory, power, and data center capacity are holding growth to that pace for now.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2899; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e3438 [third_party_view; TechRadar Pro (Benedict Collins); original source  ; from gnews:295caa6e12f0b739d2e14ea6 2026-08-29] Public opposition to data centers has led many U.S. state representatives to impose moratoriums on data-center construction or introduce legislation making it harder to build them, and $130 billion of AI data-center projects were cancelled or delayed in Q1 of calendar 2026 because of public opposition, TechRadar Pro reported on 2026-08-29 (no source for the figure given).\\n    Quote: So much so that many state representatives are implementing moratoriums on data centers, or introducing legislation to make it harder for data centers to be built. In Q1 of this year, $130 billion worth of AI data center projects were cancelled or delayed due to public opposition.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e166; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e3439 [party_statement; TechRadar Pro (Benedict Collins); original source Greg Abbott, Governor of Texas ; from gnews:295caa6e12f0b739d2e14ea6 2026-08-29] Texas Governor Greg Abbott, who TechRadar Pro says was once an advocate for data-center development in Texas, recently criticized data-center developers over their public image, saying they \\'got the backlash they deserve\\'.\\n    Quote: Texas Gov. Greg Abbott - who was once an advocate for data center development in Texas - recently criticized data center developers for their public image, stating they “got the backlash they deserve.”\\n- e3440 [third_party_view; TechRadar Pro (Benedict Collins); original source  ; from gnews:295caa6e12f0b739d2e14ea6 2026-08-29] Data centers have become a key issue in the 2026 U.S. midterm elections, which have drawn a record $646 million in donations from corporate entities, TechRadar Pro reported on 2026-08-29.\\n    Quote: Data centers have now become a key issue of the 2026 midterm elections, which has seen a record $646 million in donations from corporate entities.\\n- e3478 [third_party_view; Jake Conley, Yahoo Finance; original source Ellen Hazen, F.L.Putnam Investment Management ; from gnews:9f03bbc66269c49a5ec8afee 2026-08-30] Ellen Hazen, chief market strategist and portfolio manager at F.L.Putnam Investment Management, said the biggest risk is whether the trillion dollars of hyperscaler capex next year can actually be spent, citing labor shortages, permitting delays, rising NIMBY opposition and component shortages; if the money cannot be spent, she expects a second-derivative slowdown.\\n    Quote: \"The biggest risk is whether or not the trillion dollars in hyperscaler capex next year can actually be spent — we have labor shortages, we have permitting delays, we have NIMBY increasing in volume, and we have component shortages,\" Hazen said. … \"If we don\\'t get the ability to spend all of that money, then the second derivative slowing,\" Hazen added. \"That\\'s one of the things that I\\'m concerned \\n- e3714 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Texas state/grid officials and unnamed U.S. utilities ; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit data-center developers, and utilities in other U.S. markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed data-center sites are actually controlled, according to GuruFocus\\'s 2026-09-01 report. The item gives no number of affected projects, megawatts or a duration for the freeze.\\n    Quote: Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers. Utilities in other markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed sites are actually controlled.\\n- e3715 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com 2026-09-01; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Electricity interconnection requests across parts of the United States now exceed 700 gigawatts, more than ten times estimated current data-center electricity consumption, per GuruFocus\\'s 2026-09-01 report. The figure is not attributed to a named grid operator, regulator or data provider.\\n    Quote: Electricity requests across parts of the United States now exceed 700 gigawattsmore than ten times estimated current data-center consumption.\\n- e4717 [party_statement; CNBC, citing a video posted on social media by an All-In Summit attendee; original source Donald Trump (President of the United States) 2026-09-14; from gnews:4c28f13be664d1332e4f8857 2026-09-14] In a phone call put on speaker at the All-In Summit in Los Angeles on Monday 2026-09-14 while NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang was on stage, U.S. President Donald Trump said opposition to AI data-center construction is \"all a hoax,\" that \"the data centers are great, and they make people wealthy, and they make states wealthy,\" and that opponents are \"playing right into the hands of a lot of people that don\\'t want to see it happen,\" adding it could be coming from China and that \"We\\'re not going to let that happen.\" Huang replied, \"We\\'re not going to let that happen, sir.\" The remarks are a presidential endorsement of data-center construction, reported from a video posted by a summit attendee; no policy action or decision was announced.\\n    Quote: With Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang speaking on stage at the All-in Summit in Los Angeles, Trump called the tech exec, and was then put on speaker.\\n\"I\\'m telling you, it\\'s all a hoax,\" Trump said, according to a video posted on social media by an attendee. \"The data centers are great, and they make people wealthy, and they make states wealthy.\" … Those opposing data center construction are \"playing right \\n- e5051 [third_party_view; qz.com (Quartz), citing Fortune; original source Data Center Watch (project-halt data); unnamed survey for the 70% figure ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Roughly 70% of Americans would object to a data center being built in their community, citing strain on local power resources and higher utility bills, and community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\\n    Quote: The announcement comes as AI data centers have faced mounting public and political opposition. Roughly 70% of Americans would object to a data center being built in their community , citing strain on local power resources and higher utility bills as their leading concerns. Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is not a supplied consensus, guidance figure, or official target, so it has no rung; rung 3 is the 2-to-below-4 bucket, not a reference. The forecast remains one qualifying state beyond New York, which is rung 2: the proposal pipeline and political pressure raise the chance of further action, but the supplied items do not report another qualifying enactment, and proposals, local actions, or grid constraints alone do not count. The evidence does not imply the two states needed to cross into rung 3.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.'}Traceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"<string>\", line 12, in <module>\n\n21 Counter({'target': 13, 'control': 8})\nKeyError: 'rung'\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
